1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/158399009/anna-elizabeth-lambert
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Elizabeth “Nettie” Chambers Lambert
2 CONT BIRTH
2 CONT 25 Jul 1844
2 CONT Lancaster County, Pennsylvania, USA
2 CONT DEATH
2 CONT 8 Feb 1923 (aged 78)
2 CONT Independence, Jackson County, Missouri, USA
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Lamoni Rose Hill Cemetery
2 CONT Lamoni, Decatur County, Iowa, USA
2 CONT PLOT
2 CONT Section 1s, Lot 162, Space 1
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID
2 CONT 158399009 · View Source
2 CONT MEMORIAL
2 CONT PHOTOS 1
2 CONT FLOWERS 0
2 CONT 1.) MRS. J. R. LAMBERT PASSES
2 CONT The death of Mrs. J. R. Lambert at Independence last Thursday took from this earth one of the earliest settlers of Lamoni. She came here with her companion in 1881, just after the town was started, and lived in Lamoni almost conti
2 CONC nuously until 1919 when they moved to Independence, Mo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Elisabeth Chambers was the eldest of twelve children born to George and Martha Brubaker Chambers, then living in Lancaster county, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born July 25, 1844, spent her childhood in Pennsylvania and Indiana, and married Joseph R. Lambert, December 11, 1873, having joined the Reorganized Church of Jesus Christ of latter Day Saints in May of the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Lambert spent her years of physical vigor as the wife of a traveling missionary, in western Iowa and at Lamoni, moving to Independence, Missouri, in May, 1919, where she could be near to her two surviving children, Richard J.
2 CONC  Lambert and Mrs. A. H. Mills.
2 CONT
2 CONT She also got great comfort from the society of her five grandchildren, by whom she was affectionately cherished. The loss of one of these, Harold A. Lambert, just little more than a year since, was a severe loss to her.
2 CONT
2 CONT For three weeks before her death, Mrs. Lambert had been trying to throw off a cold which had fastened itself upon her. On Monday of last week, she went to the Independence Sanitarium, where affectionate nurses and friends watched 
2 CONC her carefully the remaining four days of her life. There was but a very brief warning of her passing away which came quietly and peacefully at ten o'clock Thursday evening February 8, 1923.
2 CONT
2 CONT A short service was held Friday afternoon at the home of her son on West Walnut Street, in Independence, Evangelist Frederick A. Smith speaking. The body was then brought to Lamoni to the home of her sister-in-law, Mrs. D. F. Lamb
2 CONC ert on Saturday afternoon.
2 CONT
2 CONT A service was held at the church Sunday afternoon in charge of Bishop A. J. Yarrington, sermon by Bishop A. Carmichael. Music was furnished by a quartette consisting of Miss Aliene Brackenbury, Mrs. Wilhelmina Johnson, Walter Wald
2 CONC en, and Paul N. Craig. Also a solo by Mr. Craig.
2 CONT
2 CONT Remains were laid to rest in Rose Hill by the side of her three children who preceded her. Her life has been one of devotion and sacrifice to her family and her church and her victory was glorious.
2 CONT Source: The Lamoni Chronicle, published in Lamoni, Iowa on Thursday, February 15th, 1923, pg 1
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.) Mrs. Anna Elisabeth Lambert, 79 years old, died of pneumonia Thursday night at the Independence Sanitarium.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born June 25 1844 In Southwestern Pennsylvania and spent her girlhood in that state and in Indiana.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Joseph R. Lambert on December 1, 1878. Three and one-half years ago they moved to Independence and lived at 1110 West Walnut street.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is survived by a son Richard J. Lambert, 1609 West Walnut street, Independence, and a daughter Mrs. Arthur H. Mills, 1514 West Short street, Independence. Burial will be in Lamoni, Ia.
2 CONT Source: newspapers.com; The Kansas City Times
2 CONT Kansas City, Missouri, 10 Feb 1923, Sat • Page 9
2 CONT
