1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Strong, the eldest of eleven children of Deacon Ezra Strong (1763 - 1833) and Nancy Gates (1766 - 1819), was born 1 November 1786 at Williamsburg, Hampshire County, Massachusetts; he died 8 October 1864 at Tur
2 CONC tlecreek Township, Warren County, Ohio. He married twice, first to Hannah Lowell and then to Lucina A. "Luany" Hoard.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Marriages and Children==
2 CONT # Hannah Lowell (1792 – 1815), married 1810 Wales Center, Erie County, New York
2 CONT ## Ezra Strong (1812 – )
2 CONT ## Seth Strong (1813 – )
2 CONT ## Mary Strong (1814 – 1870)
2 CONT # Lucina A. Hoard (1790 - ____), married 1820
2 CONT ## Reuben William Strong 1820 – 1876
2 CONT ## Lydia "Lidda" Strong (1825 – 1844)
2 CONT ## John Strong, Jr. (1829 –)
2 CONT ## Simeon Strong
2 CONT ## Daniel Strong
2 CONT ## Hannah Strong (not proven to be a daughter of John and Lucina Strong)
2 CONT ## Phoebe "Feba" Strong (not proven to be a daughter of John and Lucina Strong)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Discussion==
2 CONT *There were several John Strongs born in Massachusetts in this period. Do not confuse John Strong (1786 - 1864) with John Strong (1789 - 1849). 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Some sources list John Strong's mother as Olive Lowell (born 1794). This could not be correct, as he was born eight years prior to her birth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * John Strong may have become a Shaker, according to information at [http://www.turtlecreektownship.org/geninfo/history.htm Turtle Creek Township]. Further research needs to be done to determine if this is the same man.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>According to the Combined Atlases of Warren County, Ohio (sponsored by the Warren County Historical Society), "Following the remarkable and peculiar revivals experienced from 1801 to 1803 came strangers representing a new sect-t
2 CONC he Shakers. The news of the remarkable revival had reached them at New Lebanon, New York, and, as their method of worship was attended with more or less physical manifestation, not entirely unlike those witnessed in revivals, they
2 CONC  hoped their stange doctrine would find a ready acceptance."
2 CONT
2 CONT >>"March 22, 1805, three strangers in peculear garb arrived at Turtlecreek. They were Shakers-the first even seen west of the Allegheny Mountains-and had made the entire journey of over one thousand miles from New Lebanon on foot.
2 CONC "
2 CONT
2 CONT >>"The following Sunday, they were permitted to preach in Turtlecreek Church. They had been correct in believing that a revival of two years before had prepared the way for the acceptance of their doctrine. Within a month a dozen 
2 CONC families, including the pastor of the little flock of Turtlecreek, had embraced their faith. The Shaker doctrine forbidding marriage, husbands and wives abandoned their family relations, and, giving up their property to the societ
2 CONC y which had been formed, became the Shakers. Thus was obtained a large part of a splendid tract of four thousand acres of land lying in the western portion of Turtlecreek Township, and which was kept intact for many years until tw
2 CONC o large tracts eventually were sold."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT * [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=strong&GSfn=john&GSbyrel=all&GSdy=1864&GSdyrel=in&GSob=n&GRid=89490329&df=all& Find A Grave Memorial# 89490329]
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://trees.ancestry.com/tree/16046700/person/438731313
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John was the eldest of 11 children of Deacon Ezra Strong and Nancy Gates Strong.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married twice, first to Hannah Lowell and then to Lucina A "Luany" Hoard. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Hannah supposedly had 3 children, Ezra, Seth and Mary, before Hannah died ca 1815.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Luany had 5, possibly 7 more children: Reuben William Strong, memorial #66012684 Lydia "Lidda" Strong Simeon Strong, memorial #13172576 John Strong, Jr. Daniel Strong Hannah Strong ? Phoebe "Feba" Strong?
