1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Rev. Gershom Bulkeley, Colonial Surgeon===
2 CONT
2 CONT He was master of several languages among which may be reckoned Greek, Latin, and Dutch. He was famous as a surgeon, preeminent as a chemist, and highly respected as a magistrate. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Extracted from https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bulkeley-62
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Gershom Bulkley,[1] son of Rev. Peter Bulkley of Concord, Massachusetts, was born on December 26, 1635. [2] His parents were the Rev. Peter Bulkley, who was born January 31, 1582/3, at Odell Co., Bedford, England, and his sec
2 CONC ond wife, Grace Chetwood, who was born about 1602 and died April 21, 1669 at New London, Connecticut. [3] [4] His parents came to New England in 1635, after his father, a preacher for twenty years, had been 'silenced' for his non-
2 CONC conformity to the English Church. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Gershom Bulkeley is named in his father's will dated April 14, 1658 and proved June 20,1659. [3] In a codicil dated February 26, 1658, regarding the list of books appended to the will of Rev. Peter Bulkeley, part of the books
2 CONC  were to go to son Edward and the other part to his son Gershom.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Gershom married Sarah Chauncey on October 6, 1659 at Concord, Massachusetts. [10] Sarah was born in Ware, England June 13, 1631, daughter of Charles Chauncy, President at Harvard College. [4] [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife Sarah died June 3, 1699 and was buried the next day.[11][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT # Catherine, born about 1660; married Richard Treat; left a daughter Catherine. [19] [4]
2 CONT # Dorothy born about 1662; died in 1757; married to Thomas Treat of Nayaug on July 5, 1693. [4] [19] [4]
2 CONT # Charles born about 1663; died before his father; licensed a physician New London 1687; married ___. [4] [19]
2 CONT # Peter born on November 7, 1660 [10]; married Rachel Talcott; lost at sea in 1701 at the age of thirty seven. [4] [19]
2 CONT # Edward born ___; died on August 27, 1748 at Wethersfield; married Dorothy Prescott at Concord, Massachusetts. [10] [4] [19]
2 CONT # John born___; graduated Harvard College in 1699; died in June, 1731; married Patience Prentice in 1701; settled at Colchester, Connecticut. [4][19]
2 CONT
2 CONT Military
2 CONT
2 CONT * After being installed at Wethersfield, he was appointed by the General Court in 1675, surgeon to the army that had been raised against the Indians in King Philip's War.[6] [5]
2 CONT * He was also placed on the Council of War. [7]
2 CONT * In 1675, while on an expedition the party to which he was attached was attacked by Indians near Wachusett Hill in Massachusetts and he was wounded in the thigh. [8] [4] [5]
2 CONT * Roster of the Officers of the Army of the United Colonies, as organized for the Narragansett Campaign, and as mustered at Pettisquamscot, December 19, 1675. Connecticut Regiment: Robert Treat, of Milford, Major: Gershom Bulkeley
2 CONC , Surgeon.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Will and Death
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at Glastonbury, Connecticut on December 2, 1713. [4][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Gershom Bulkeley is dated May 26, 1712, and in it he takes note of the grievous illness which he suffered for the last twenty years of his life.[2] Gershom died at Glastonbury, Connecticut at the home of his daughter D
2 CONC orothy (Bulkeley) Treat, 2 Dec. 1713, ae 77 yrs 11 mos. (p. 110)[13][14][15][16][17][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT They are buried at Wethersfield Village Cemetery, Wethersfield, Hartford, Connecticut. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Inscription''':
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Rev. and Hon. Gershom Bulkley died Dec. 2nd 1713, aged 77. He was honorable in his descent. Of rare abilities, excellent in learning, master of many languages, exquisite in his skill in divinity, physic and law and a most exempl
2 CONC ary and Christian life. In certam spem beatae resurrectionis repositus." [5] [18]
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Rev. Gershom Bulkeley names the following: (p. 81-86)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sons Edward and John.
2 CONT * Daughter Dorothy Treat.
2 CONT * Grandaughter Hannah Goodrich, daughter of son Charles, now deceased.
2 CONT * Daughters Hannah Avery (widow of son Charles) and Rachel Wolcott.
2 CONT * Dorothee, wife of son Edward.
2 CONT * Gershom, Peter, Grace, Margaret and Dorothee, children of his brother Peter.
2 CONT * Grandson Richard Treat, son of Thomas and Dorothee.
2 CONT * Granddaughter Catherine Treat, daughter of Richard and Catharine (now deceased) Treat.
2 CONT * Son-in law Thomas Treat to be sole executor. After Thomas' death, he made a revision on Nov. 24, 1713, naming daughter Dorothy Treat sole executrix. [4]
2 CONT __________________________________________________________
2 CONT Gershom and his wife Sarah had seven children, the youngest of whom was a son
2 CONT named John who was born in Wethersfield in 1679. John graduated from
2 CONT Harvard in 1699, married Patience Prentice of New London in 1701 and was
2 CONT ordained minister of the church at Colchester on December 20, 1703. Like 
2 CONT his father Gersham, John was not only a minister but also a lawyer and physicians as well. 
2 CONT Dr. Chauncy wrote of John Bulkeley that he was one of the greatest geniuses of his day and surpassed his predecessors in the strength of his intellectual power.
2 CONT John and his wife Patience had 12 children, the third of whom was John and part
2 CONT of this exhibit will focus on his lineal descendents, Eliphalet Adams Bulkeley and
2 CONT Morgan Gardner Bulkeley. John was born in Colchester in 1705 and died here in
2 CONT 1753. His first wife was Mary Gardiner from New London, and after her death he
2 CONT married Abigail Hastings. He was the first Bulkeley to graduate from Yale (1726).
2 CONT John’s younger brother Gershom was born in Colchester in 1708 and died here in 1788. He married Abigail Robbins of Wethersfield in 1733 and they had 8 children.
2 CONT Gershom served as a Captain in the 1st Company which fought during the 1757 Campaign in the French and Indian War. Gershom also was Colchester’s deputy to the Connecticut General Assembly. Gershom’s family lineage is displayed in 
2 CONC some detail in the exhibit, which tells about the re-discovery and exhumation of “The Tomb of Gershom Bulkeley and his Descendents”.
2 CONT http://colchesterhistory.org/wp-content/themes/chs/images/bulk2.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT *An official Ancestor registered in the Order of Descendants of Colonial Physicians & Chirurgiens https://ordercolonialpandc.org/ancestor-list 
2 CONT *[https://archive.org/stream/historyofancient12adam#page/794/mode/2up History of Ancient Wethersfield by Stiles]
2 CONT *[http://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Bulkeley-62 WikiTree Profile]
2 CONT --------------------
