1 NAME Dorothy /North/
2 GIVN Dorothy
2 SURN North
2 _MARNM Ransom
2 NICK Awbrey
2 NICK Gouldman
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1669
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY British Colonial America
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1748
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY British Colonial America
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dorothy North was born c. 1669 at Essex County, Colony of Virginia, a daughter of Augustine North and Dorothy (Unknown). This birth date is a guess, based on the approximate birth date of her second child and other
2 CONC  known dates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some have assumed that Richard Awbrey's wife, Dorothy, was a sister of John Meador. Not has such a sister been found. In actuality, Richard's wife was Dorothy North, daughter of Augustine North (Torrence, p. 174).
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy married three times.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy married, 1st to Richard Awbrey (b about 1664). She may have been a young bride. If she was no older than her husband, then her second child may have been born when she was as young as 19 years. The will of Richard Awbrey w
2 CONC as dated 18 April 1697 and was probated on October 11, 1697 (Torrence, pp 175, 187).
2 CONT She married after c. 1697, as her 2nd, to Thomas Gouldman.
2 CONT She married by 11 Sep 1699, as her 3rd, to Peter Ransone. "Peter Ransone and Dorothy, his wife, executrix of Thomas Gouldman, deceased, to return the inventory and appraisement of the said decadent's estate ..." (p 178).
2 CONT On August 11, 1699, Dorothy was in court with her 3rd husband, Peter Ransone, to settle her 2nd husband's estate, and the court was continued to September 11, 1699:
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Ransone and Dorothy, his wife, executrix of Thomas Gouldman, deceased, presented the inventory and appraisement of the said decedent's estate upon oath except such estate as was willed by her former husband, Richard Awbrey, 
2 CONC in his last will to be delivered to his children in specie which is ordered to be recorded.
2 CONT Death and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy Ransone may have been living as late as 9 June 1748 when Thomas Waring, of Essex County, wrote his will, "I give my negro Tom, now in my mother Ransom's possession, with all my other negros not herein before bequeathed ...
2 CONC  " This was doubtless Thomas Waring, Dorothy's son-in-law, husband of her daughter, Elizabeth (Gouldman) Waring (p 182).
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Dorothy and Richard Awbrey:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Awbrey, b
2 CONT Henry Awbrey, b after 1683
2 CONT Dorothy Awbrey, b 1687 mar John Billups before 1718.
2 CONT Child Awbrey, b after 1697
2 CONT Child of Elizabeth and Thomas Gouldman:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Gouldman, b c. 1698; m c. 1713/4 to Thomas Waring of Essex Co, VA
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ 1.0 1.1 1.2 Briggs, Betty West Virginia Pioneers citing Our Meador Families in Colonial America, by Victor Paul Meador and Bernal Meador.
2 CONT ↑ 2.0 2.1 2.2 2.3 2.4 2.5 Torrence, Clayton, (1927) Winston of Virginia ... Richmond: Whittet & Shepperson HathiTrust.org (Pages 174, 178, 183).
2 CONT ↑ 3.0 3.1 Awbrey, Jon Anthony (2007) Aubrey : dominion and decline : a concise history of the Welsh Marcher family from Henry III through the American Revolution and the Victorian Age, 1250-1856. WorldCat.org.
2 CONT ↑ Richard Awbrey's estate was settled on October 11, 1697, Essex County Records, Volume 1699. pages 5 and 14.
2 CONT ↑ from Essex County Records, Volume 1699. page 14, cited by Torrence, Winston of Virginia ... pp 174-75, footnote.
2 CONT ↑ Jon Awbrey's work, Aubrey : dominion and decline ..., does not mention the posthumous child of Richard Awbrey.
2 CONT See also:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alford, Norris, (Jun 2013) "Meadows Descendants." Archive.org 2 Oct 2015.
2 CONT Records of Old Rappahnnock Co. Essex Co Virginia
2 CONT Tyler, Lyon Gardiner, ed. (1915) Encyclopedia of Virginia Biography, Volume I-II. New York: Lewis Historical Publishing Company, Archive.org (Vol 1, Pages 312 Peter Ransone).
2 CONT (Oct 1901) "Historical and Genealogical Notes." The William and Mary Quarterly. Omohundro Institute of Early American History and Culture, JSTOR.org (Vol. 10, No. 2, pp. 141-144, citing p 142).
2 CONT (Apr 1902) "Ransone Family." The William and Mary Quarterly , Omohundro Institute of Early American History and Culture, JSTOR.org (Vol. 10, No. 4, pp. 264-267).
2 CONT https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I30654&tree=Tree1
