3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} THE WAR OF 1812
2 CONT
2 CONT SON = Capt. Mark Anthony, Sr. was in command of 500 men from Bedford Co., VA. This was the VA Militia 10th Regiment. This company under Mark’s command was called “Anthony’s Artillery Company”. For 6 months, this artillery company 
2 CONC was @ Ft. Norfolk, VA under the general command of Lt. Col. Edmund Lucas of the 4th Infantry Militia Regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________
2 CONT   ~~   CAPTAIN  MARK  ANTHONY,  SR.   ~~ __________________________________ “Mark Anthony, Sr.” (1779 VA—1859 VA).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mark was the son of: ~ “John Anthony, JR.” (1746 VA—1822 VA) &  ~ “Susan Austin” (1749 VA—1817 VA). ______________________________
2 CONT **  REV.  WAR = Mark's Dad's Service: ~ “John Anthony, JR.” was a Soldier in the 10th Continental VA Regiment during  The Rev. War.  He served from January 1777 til the end of the war.   DAR # 002884 & SAR #P-104167.  * * ________
2 CONC ______________________ John JR. & Susan married in 1771 VA.   They had 8 Children = 6 sons & 2 daughters who were raised @ a brick home which his dad built overlooking the Anthony Mill Creek (today termed Mill Creek).  Mark was th
2 CONC eir 3rd. son.
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT ~ John Anthony, JR. was the “only son” of John Anthony, Sr. (1715 VA—1760 VA).  Thus, JR. inherited much of his dad's vast landholdings along the Otter River.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ SR. lived in Albemarle Co., Bedford Co., & Campbell Co., VA & purchased 1,012 acres in 1754 on both sides of the Otter River & 674 acres in 1759 @ the head of Blackwater/Ivy Creek.  He was a Justice-of-the-Peace of Albemarle Co.
2 CONC , VA in 1745-1746.  He died in 1760.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ JR. purchased 1,300 acres in 1780 on the south branch of Otter Creek.  He built a brick home on a hill that overlooked Anthony Mill Creek (a tributary of Goose Creek).  The Anthony Family lived here in the late 1700’s-mid 1800’s
2 CONC .  This was home to John Anthony JR., wife Susan, & their 8 Children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ JR. became a Baptist minister in 1776.  He started a church that he named the Otter Church which later became the Bethlehem Church of Bedford Co., VA.  He served there til his death in 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Capt. Mark Anthony had a grist mill & a saw mill & an Anthony ferry station @ Anthony Mill Creek @ Campbell Co., VA (today known as Mill Creek).
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Mark's 2 brothers, Charles & Abner Anthony, also ran ferry stations on the Roanoke & Otter River in Franklin & Pittsylvania Counties, VA.   ~ Charles turned mostly to farming & built a home in the early 1800's that he called "Wa
2 CONC lnut Hill" near Evington.  His descendants live in the beautiful home today. [Another Walnut Hill (also near Evington) is on the registry of historic homes which is owned by the Leftwich family.]  ~ Abner even started a town in 18
2 CONC 18 called Monroe on the banks of the Strauton River.  It's post office was Monroeton.  The town's plat consisted of many streets; in 1869, Monroe was no longer on the tax rolls.  Monroe was submerged by the formation of Smith Moun
2 CONC tain Lake.  It would have been on the south shoreline of the lake. Abner became a Baptist minister (like their dad JR. was).  Abner ministered in Bedford, Franklin, & Pittsylvania Counties of VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ There was even an Anthony Bridge which a few decades ago was torn down to further progress the area.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Much of the Anthony holdings have been submerged by the formation of Smith Mountain Lake & Leesville Lake.  Roads around these lakes still bear the name Anthony.
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT In the Library of VA @ Richmond, there is a large collection of letters, deeds, documents, etc. from the youngest son of John Anthony JR. =  Charles Anthony (1793- 1884) & his daughter (Callie J. Brandon 1839-1886) & his grand‘dau
2 CONC ghter’ (Charles Anthony 1883-1966).  One of the accounts is that Charles saw his older brother, Banks Anthony, riding up to the homeplace one evening in 1815.  He then saw this approaching Banks & his horse literally disappear.  D
2 CONC ays later, the family learned that William Banks Anthony had been killed @ The Battle of New Orleans (during The War of 1812) on the very day that Charles saw Banks’ ghost riding his horse up to the homeplace.  As late as 1960, Ch
2 CONC arles’ granddaughter (who was literally named Charles Anthony) told a reporter the story of her grandpa seeing his brother Banks’ ghost riding up to the old homeplace on the same day that Banks died in battle.  ___________________
2 CONC _______ THE  WAR  OF  1812
2 CONT
2 CONT * Capt. Mark Anthony, Sr. was in command of 500 men from Bedford Co., VA.  This was the VA Militia 10th Regiment.  This company under Mark’s command was called''' “Anthony’s Artillery Company”. ''' For 6 months, this artillery com
2 CONC pany was @ Ft. Norfolk, VA under the general command of Lt. Col. Edmund Lucas of the 4th Infantry Militia Regiment. * ''' Capt. Anthony’s wife’s relatives were also active during The War of 1812.  They were:  Maj. Littleberry Left
2 CONC wich, Maj. Jabez Leftwich, Lt. Nicodemus Leftwich, Capt. Jesse Leftwich, & Capt. James Leftwich.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Capt. Anthony’s older brother, William Banks Anthony (1775 VA—1815 LA), was killed during The Battle of New Orleans. __________________________ MARK’s  WIFE: Mark Anthony, Sr. married''' “Emelie Leftwich” (1784 VA—1883 VA).''' The
2 CONC y married on 12-13-1799 @ Bedford Co., VA.  (Their surety bond was on 12-12-1799.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Emelie was born, married, lived, & died @ Bedford Co., VA.  She was born on 9-2-1784; she died on 3-23-1883.  I read that she died from a fall from a horse @ 99!  
2 CONT
2 CONT Emelie was the daughter of: ~ “Col. Thomas Leftwich” (1740 VA—1816 VA) & ~ “Jane ‘Gincey’ Stratton” (1762 VA—1806 VA).   Jane was Thomas’ 3rd. wife.  Emelie was their 1st child.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE  REV.  WAR = Emelie's Dad's Service: 
2 CONT * Col. Thomas Leftwich was a Rev. War Soldier. * He later became a Sheriff of Bedford Co., VA.  __________________________ MARK  &  EMELIE’s   13  CHILDREN:
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Susannah Anthony (1800-1883) m:  Josiah Morton.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Thomas J. Anthony (1802-1876) m:  Catherine Barfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Lucy Anthony (1805) Thought to have died by age 8.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ John Anthony (Dr.) (1806) m:  Louvenia Peatross.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Mark Anthony, Jr. (1808-1896) m:  Mary Hunt.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ William Banks Anthony (1810) Thought to have died before age 2. (Named-for Mark’s brother Banks who died in 1815 LA.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Abner A. Anthony (Col.) (1812) m:  Jane L. Brown on 2-24-1836. (Named-for Mark's younger brother Abner.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ~  Emelie Leftwich Anthony (1814) m:  William V. Haden or Headon on 1-3-1837. This was found as Headon in surety bond for the marriage. (Named exactly the same as her mother.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Sallie E. Anthony (1816) Thought to have died about age 6.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Mary C. Anthony (1819) m:  Adison Ward.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Thomas William Anthony (1821-1901) m:  Ellen F. Leftwich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twins: ~ Catherine Louvinea Anthony (1828) m:  William H. Tandy or Tardy on 12-3-1845.  It was Tardy was  from the surety bond.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~ Charles Anthony (1828) Thought to have died about age 16.  (Named-for Mark's youngest brother Charles.) _____________________________ ~ Lynda, 4-great’s-granddaughter to Mark & Emelie through their son, Thomas J. Anthony. ______
2 CONC _______________________* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/142323585/mark-anthony Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 27 2020, 5:23:11 UTC''
2 CONT
