1 TITL Dr
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Graduate of the University of Pennsylvania
2 CONT Member of the State Convention 1829-1830
2 CONT House of Delegates from Lynchburg
2 CONT Source: News & Advance, Lynchburg, VA, 
2 CONT June 14, 2015, C7
2 CONT
2 CONT "Death of Dr. Saunders, of Lynchburg"
2 CONT
2 CONT We grieve to hear of the death of Dr. Jas Saunders, one of the most widely known and highly esteemed citizens of Lynchburg.The event occurred at his residence in that city, night before last, after an illness of about three weeks.
2 CONC  His life, extended through a period of near or quite seventy-five years, had been one of great activity, and of much usefulness, public and private. He was in the war of 1812, was for some years a member of the State Senate, and 
2 CONC also of the Conventions of 1820 and 1840 to reform the Constitution.
2 CONT
2 CONT His business operations were on a large scale, and though attended with varied fortune, were conducted with a boldness, energy and sagacity, that individualized him as a man and showed him to be possessed of strong and dominant tr
2 CONC aits of character. His mind was one of great vigor, and his information extensive. His hospitality was without bounds, and his charities many and liberal. His nature was cordial and his sensibilities quick and generous. These were
2 CONC  the leading characteristics of a man who has filled a large space in the social, business and political history of Virginia for half a century. The whole community suffers when such a man dies. It will temper the grief of those n
2 CONC earest to him to remember that after having lived a life of usefulness and honor, he died the death of a Christian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Published in the Richmond Whig (Richmond, Va.) 
2 CONT Tuesday, January 26, 1864, Vol. 41, no. 10, page 4.
2 CONT Provided by Sandra Pearson
2 CONT
2 CONT Links to his parents provided by 
