1 NOTE Colonial and Revolutionary Lineages, Vol 1, p. 172-3.  John Drake, son of John and Elizabeth Rogers Drake, was born probably in England, and left a will, dated 9/12/1689, and proved 11/11/1689 (date of death from Ancestral File). 
2 CONC  He was one of the first settlers of Simsbury, CT, but later resided for a time in Windsor, until 1659, or perhaps longer.  Simsbury was so named in 1670, having been previously known by its Indian name of Massaco.  It was quaintl
2 CONC y described in the records of the CT General Assembly as "an appendix to the town of Windsor."  In 1676 the town was burned by Indians, forcing the settlers to flee to Windsor.  In 1680 John Drake's "Ratable List" was one person, 
2 CONC house land 4 acres, meadow 8 1/2 acres, etc., all valued at Đ70-10-00.  He bought from the overseers of the estate of Ephraim Huit land in the Great Meadow, which in 1684 he claimed by right of undisputed possession for 20 years o
2 CONC r more.  John Drake married, 11/30/1648, in Windsor, CT, Hannah Moore.  (Moore II.)
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 154, Stiles.  John had a lot, 4 1/2 acres, bd. S on Bloomfield Ave., N partly on Mill Highway.
2 CONT Alt Death: 12 Sep 1689 Simsbury, CT
1 NOTE RFN: 19758
2 CONT A first settler at Simsbury CT.
