1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "The Barber Genealogy" by John Barber White p. 23 Possibly daughter of a Dutch settler; possibly first white woman to land in CT 
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Connecticut Barbers by Donald S. Barber 1992: Jane's last name was unknown and the name Coggins is highly suspect.  Seen as daughter of https://www.geni.com/people/John-Cogan-I/6000000000618874500?through=6000000000318516
2 CONC 659 & https://www.geni.com/people/Maryane-Gaspye/6000000000618922094?through=6000000000318516659 without supporting evidence 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been said by some that this Jane may have been the daughter of one of the Dutch traders at Old Saybrook, or Hartford, and “the first white woman to land in Connecticut”.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT (f/g) 
2 CONT * Jane Barber
2 CONT * Birth: 	1620, England
2 CONT * Death: 	Sep. 10, 1662 Windsor Hartford County Connecticut, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber married at Windsor, 7 Oct 1640, Jane ____. She died at Windsor on 10 Sep 1662 as "[t]he wife of Thomas Bar Ber." In the inventory of Thomas Barber, taken 20 Oct 1662, "his wives apparel deceased" was valued at [pound
2 CONC s]15.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had six children: John, Thomas, Sarah Hall, Samuel, Marcy Gillett, & Josiah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Anderson's Great Migration Study Project,
2 CONT
2 CONT FINDaGRAVE contributor architype adds: She died the day before her husband Thomas. She is almost certainly interred at the same cemetery Palisado Cemetery: Windsor, Hartford, CT
2 CONT
2 CONT Find A Grave contributor Charlene Teifer Stiffinella suggests that Jane Barber #38520655 is Jane/Joane Coggin Barber, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
