3 CITY Stamford
3 STAE England
1 BAPM
2 DATE 25 DEC 1612
2 PLAC St. George Parish
2 ADDR
3 CITY Stamford
3 STAE England
1 BURI
2 DATE SEP 1662
2 PLAC Palisado Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 plot: Founder's Monument
3 CITY Windsor
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 11 SEP 1662
2 ADDR
3 CITY Windsor
3 STAE Connecticut Colony
1 OCCU Sergeant, carpenter, yeoman, first settler of Windsor CT
1 TITL Sgt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1. Sgt. THOMAS1 BARBER (JohnA) baptised at St George Parish, Stamford, Lincolnshire, England 25 Dec 1612; b probably 21 Dec 1612 (St Thomas’s Day); d Windsor CT 11 Sep 1662; m Windsor 7 Oct 1640 Jane ‑‑‑, b  ; d Wi
2 CONC ndsor 10 Sep 1662; r Windsor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before coming to New England Thomas was apprenticed on 18 Dec 1634 for a period of 9 years (until December 1643) under Francis STILES, a master carpenter from Millbrook, Bedfordshire, England.  Stiles was contracted by Sir Richard
2 CONC  SALTONSTALL to bring apprentices to Windsor, and to build houses in America for Englishmen who were to follow. Thomas Barber at the age of 21 was among the twenty apprentices plus others who sailed with Stiles for New England in 
2 CONC the ship "Christian" (John White, Master), which left London 16 Mar 1634 (Julian Calendar), and arrived 3 months later in Boston June 1635.  Each of the passengers had a certificate which read in part: "with certificate from St. M
2 CONC ildred, Bread Street, London, and having taken the oaths, to be transported to New England from London in the "Christian".  [~ from the Public Record Office, Westminster Hall, London ~]
2 CONT
2 CONT After 10 days at Boston the Christian sailed up the Connecticut River to Windsor, arriving there on the first of July 1635. That same year Thomas was granted a lot of a few acres, extending from Mill Brook, near the old Warham gri
2 CONC stmill, north along both sides of Poquonock Avenue.  The author's father was born on this same land, and my brother, sister, and I were brought up on this, the original land grant.  After 330 years of continuous Barber ownership, 
2 CONC the land has now all been sold.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pequot War in 1637, which according to the settlers was precipitated by the Pequot Indians and their continual harassment of the settlers, the friendly Mohegan Indians, and sometimes the Narragansetts, found Thomas a Sargeant,
2 CONC  one of 30 soldiers from Windsor who were enrolled under Major Stoughton for 3 weeks and 2 days. Under the leadership of Captain John MASON, the May 26 1637 night attack on the Pequot fort was a complete surprise to the sleeping P
2 CONC equots, and a large percentage of the tribe was massacred.  Thomas Barber's bravery (he was inside the Pequot Fort at Mystic during the attack, and along with Edward Pattison, “having no time to reload their muskets, slew seven fl
2 CONC eeing Indians with axes and knives”), gained him honorable mention from Capt. Mason, and in return for this service, in 1641 he was granted 600 acres of land in Massaco, in the western part of Windsor. Massaco became Simsbury in 1
2 CONC 669.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 28, 1637 Francis Stiles (master carpenter) was ordered to teach his servants, George Chapple, Thomas Cooper and Thomas Barber in the trade of carpentry.  The year 1645 found Thomas still an apprentice carpenter. Stiles ap
2 CONC parently was slow to finish Thomas's apprenticeship, and needed a court order to force him to do so.  Shortly after that Thomas was a free man.   At the time of his death in 1662, Thomas may have been making preparations to move t
2 CONC o Northampton MA, where he was offered a home lot and 20 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life was apparently not completely peaceful for Thomas1 BARBER.  Court records in 1649 show that he was found liable for a debt to William FRANKLIN.  Thomas claimed that he had given the money to Thomas FORD to pay to Franklin.  T
2 CONC hen in 1650 Sargeant Barber was fined 5 pounds and forced to surrender his rank, for the disorderly striking of Lt. COOKE in an argument over church matters; Thomas believed that the church had no right to interfere in temporal ma
2 CONC tters. This penalty was later canceled when apparently Thomas apologized: "he is affected with his great evil and rash passionate carriage in striking the Lt."  
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas left an estate appraised at over 132 pounds.
2 CONT
2 CONT A deep mystery surrounds Jane, wife of Thomas.  He married her in 1640, but the written record by Matthew Grant gives only the name Jane or Joan.  Two of Thomas's sons married COGGIN ladies, but there is little sign that Jane was 
2 CONC a Coggin, as some have suggested.  One report (LDS record) has it that Jane COGGIN, b Bedfordshire ENG 1619 was daughter of John COGGIN, b in Bedford, Bedfordshire about 1593. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It has also been suggested that Thomas BARBER married Jane BANCROFT, widow of John BANCROFT who died in 1637. Jane Bancroft had ties to Windsor thru her daughter Anna, b 1627, who married 1647 John GRIFFIN of Windsor and Simsbury,
2 CONC  and her son John, born about 1620, who married in 1650 Hannah DUPPER and had a family in Windsor. But Jane BONYTHON who married John BANCROFT was born in 1573, and would have been way too old to have borne Thomas BARBER’s childre
2 CONC n.  This theory against the wife being Jane Bancroft was written up in The American Genealogist, Vol 37, p 164, in 1961 by George E McCracken  and more or less disproved at that time. He points out that she would have had to have 
2 CONC borne children for too long a time span - highly unlikely, and also she would have had 2 sons named John and 2 named Thomas - also unlikely.  Another account has John BANCROFT born about 1596, died 1637, m. Jane about 1622.  That 
2 CONC would have meant she was born about 1606 or before, making her about 47 in 1653 when Thomas’s youngest child Josiah Barber was born - not impossible, but very unlikely.  
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been said by some that Thomas may have married the daughter of one of the Dutch traders at Old Saybrook, or Hartford, and also that the one he married was “the first white woman to land in Connecticut”.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Francis STILES' sisters was named Jane, born 1605.  She married in England and presumably remained there.  There was a Jane MORDEN or WORDEN, age 35 (in 1635), on the passenger list of the Christian; however I know nothing 
2 CONC further about her.  It seems she was too old to have borne all of Thomas's children.  
2 CONT
2 CONT There seems as yet no way of knowing who Jane was (an all too frequent problem in genealogy). [~ Windsor Hist Soc:Jay Mack Holbrook, 1992; 1909 Barber Gen; Barbour Index; Lyman Barber Gen; Stiles:Windsor; Lure of the Litchfield Hi
2 CONC lls ~]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Thomas and Jane, born at Windsor:
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT # Occupation: Carpenter, farmer, founder of Windsor, Connecticut
2 CONT # Event: Military Sargeant in the Pequote War of 1637
2 CONT # Event: Freeman 1645
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 
2 CONT The Barber family history dates back nearly 900 years, taking their name from their occupation of barber, which was hair dressing, shaving beards, pulling teeth, and wound dressing, in which they were accounted very skillful. They
2 CONC  were styled the Baron's Barber by the nobility before surnames were much used. The red and white sign posts (barber poles) signify white bandages soaked in blood. The Barber name has several variations, including Barbour. It is a
2 CONC lso Anglo-Norman French, Barber, Old French, Barbier, from Late Latin Barbarius, a derivative of barba, "beard". The name is found in Northumberland and Cumberland, but also branches north over the border into Scotland, from Engla
2 CONC nd. The Barber coat of arms has a gold shield with two chevrons and three fleur de lys with a silver bull's head.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber was the son of John Barber and Elizabeth Lumley. John Barber was called a labourer in 1609, and a yeoman later. Yeoman were small land owners, a step up from labourers, but below gentry. John and Elizabeth lived firs
2 CONC t in St. George Parish, Stamford, Lincoln County, which is the part of Stamford adjacent to Uffington, and later in All Saints Parish on the other side of Stamford. Stamford is about 80 miles north of London. John and Elizabeth ha
2 CONC d five children, Rebecca, Sarah, Thomas, Elizabeth, and John.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber was baptized on Dec. 25 1612 in St. George, Stamford, Lincoln Co., England. He was probably born on Dec. 21, 1612 on St. Thomas's Day. Beford coming to New England Thomas was apprenticed on Dec. 18, 1634 for a period
2 CONC  of 9 years until Dec. 1643, under Francis Stiles, a master carpenter from Millbrook, Bedfordshire, England and a London freeman. A search of the appropriate guild records in London by Frances Markham, a London researcher, located
2 CONC  the following entry in the records of the "Worshipful Company of Carpenter" at the guildhall. From the minutes of a meeting held on Dec. 18, 1634, "Received of Francis Stiles for apprenticing Thomas Barber, son of John Barber of 
2 CONC Stamford in the County of Lincoln, yeoman, deceased, from St. Thomas's day next for 9 years. Stiles was later contracted by Sir Richard Saltonstall to bring apprentices to Windsor, and build houses in America for Englishmen who we
2 CONC re to follow. Thomas Barber at age 21 was among the twenty plus apprentices who sailed with Stiles for New England on the ship, Christian, with John White as their Master. The ship left London on March 16, 1634 (Julian calender) a
2 CONC nd arrived 3 months later in Boston in June of 1635. Each of the passengers had a certificate which read in part, "with certificate from St. Mildren, Bread Stree, London, having taken the oaths, to be transported to New England fr
2 CONC om London in the Christian." (from the Public Record Office, Westminster Hall, London). After 10 days in Boston the ship, Christian, sailed up the Connecticut River to Windsor, arriving there on the first day of July in 1635.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the early Massachusetts towns became crowded, their land hungry people spilled over into Connecticut. Windsor was settled by Englishmen from Plymouth, Massachusetts in 1633. The rivers and the coastal inlets of the sea were t
2 CONC he first highways of Connecticut and Windsor lay close to the Connecticut River. The people of Connecticut were almost entirely of English descent until about 1850.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Windsor, Connecticut records, Thomas Barber was granted in 1635, "a lot ten rods west of Humphrey Hyde's Mill Road, 8 acres and 22 rods wide, bounded south by Mill Brook, extending as stated 2 rods wide, to accomm
2 CONC odate Barber and Alvord, and also a way for Mr. Wareham, Minister, to go to his lot north of Barber's and Alvord's and ended in the Poquonnock."
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber's residence was located, "upon an ancient road which running about southwesterly from the rivulet (near where the present road from Palisade Green comes in) intersected the Poquonnock road above the old mill." On the
2 CONC  North side of this road were the residences of Thomas Barber, Humphrey Hyde, and Alex Alvord, and on the south side that of Jonathan Gillett.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is evident from the records, that Francis Stiles failed to fulfill his contract with Thomas Barber and the other young men of his party, for on March 28, 1637 the following order was made by the court of Hartford, Connecticut, 
2 CONC "that Mr. Francis Stiles shall teach George Chapple, Thomas Cooper, and Thomas Barber, his servant apprentices in the trade of a carpenter, according to his promise for their services for their term, behind 4 days a week only to s
2 CONC aw and slitt their own work that they are to frame themselves with their own hands, together with himself or some other master workman; the time to begin for the performance of this order is 14 days hence without fail."
2 CONT
2 CONT Many Indians were friendly with the settlers. They taught the settlers to grow corn and other crops, but the Pequots didn't want the settlers on their land and they were Connecticut's largest tribe. The Indians were upset as the c
2 CONC olonists moved more and more into Indian Territory and made it harder for the Indians to fish and hunt. In 1636, a settler was killed and the colonist blamed the Pequots. This led to the Pequot War (1636-1637). The Pequot Indians 
2 CONC made many raids on the early settlements, but were decisively defeated in a battle at West Mystic in 1637, where more than 700 Pequot men, women, and children died. Thomas Barber, a Sargeant, was one of 30 soldiers from Windsor wh
2 CONC o was enrolled under Major Stoughton for 3 weeks and 2 days. Under the leadership of Captain John Mason, the May 26, 1637 night attack on the Pequot fort was a complete surprise to the sleeping Pequots, and a large percentage of t
2 CONC he tribe was massacred. Thomas Barber took part in several battles with the Pequot Indians and greatly distinguished himself in the attack made upon the Pequot Fort. Thomas Barber was inside the Pequot Fort at West Mystic during t
2 CONC he attack, which the Indians considered impregnable. In an account of this battle, written by Mason and published in Boston in 1727, the following reference is made to the part taken by Thomas Barber, "We had entered the fort and 
2 CONC in getting out of a wigwam encountered seven Indians. They fled and we pursued to the end of a lane, but before we could reach them they were met by Thomas Barber and Edward Patterson, who slew the entire seven with their axes and
2 CONC  knives, their muskets having been discharged."
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his return to Windsor, he contracted to build the first meeting house. In 1640 Thomas Barber married Jane or Joan and they had six children, John, Thomas, Sarah, Samuel, Mary, and Josiah. A deep mystery of Jane's maiden name 
2 CONC surrounds her. Two of Thomas's sons married Coggin ladies, but there is little sign that Jane was a Coggin, as some have suggested. It has also been suggested that Thomas Barber married Jane Bonython, widow of John Bancroft, who d
2 CONC ied in 1637, but Jane Bonython was born in 1573 and would have been way too old to have borne Thomas Barber's children. This theory was written up in The American Genealogist, Vol. 37, pg. 164, in 1961 by George E. McCracken and m
2 CONC ore or less disproves this possibility. It has also been said by some that Thomas may have married the daughter of one of the Dutch traders at Old Saybrook, or Hartford, and also that the one he married was "the first white woman 
2 CONC to land in Connecticut." There seems as yet no way of knowing who Jane was, an all too frequent problem in genealogy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1641, the lands in the locality were called by the Indian name, Massaco, and were apportioned among the colonists. Massaco became Simsbury in 1669. As an Indian fighter he evidenced a courage and sagacity which secured him the 
2 CONC confidence of the colonies and the fear and respect of the Indians. His bravery gained him honorable mention from Captain Mason and in return for this service in 1641, he was granted 600 acres of land in Massaco in the Western par
2 CONC t of Windsor. Life was apparently not completely peaceful for Thomas Barber. Court records in 1649 show that he was found liable for a debt to William Franklin. Thomas claimed that he had given the money to Thomas Ford to pay to F
2 CONC ranklin. Then in 1650 Sargeant Barber was fined 5 pounds and forced to surrender his rank, for the disorderly striking of Lt. Cooke in an argument over church matters. Thomas believed that the church had not right to interfere in 
2 CONC temporal matters. This penalty was later canceled when apparently Thomas apologized, "I was affected with a great evil and rash passionate carriage in striking the Lt."
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of Thomas Barber's death in 1662, he was making preparations to move to Northampton, Massachusetts. The records of Northampton town meeting on April 24, 1661 stated that it was voted and agreed for Thomas Barber of Win
2 CONC dsor to become an inhabitant of that town. Thomas Barber and his wife died at the same time of unknown causes and left four children still at home, ages ten through sixteen. Pnemonia, tuberculosis, and dysentery claimed more lives
2 CONC  in New England than any other illnesses. Yet there were also infections diseases like smallpox. Thomas Barber's will provided well for these children and left an estate appraised at over 132 pounds.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber was thoughtful, intelligent, and a God fearing man, and for the times exceedingly liberal in religious views. His probate record was taken on Oct. 20, 1662 by Benjamin Newbery and John Moore (Page 184, Probate Record
2 CONC s of Hartford, Connecticut and Court Records, page 187). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT It has long been known that Thomas1 Barber, with several others, was apprenticed to Francis Stiles, who was paid to bring them to Windsor CT. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Stiles was a carpenter and London freeman, so it seemed reasonable to search in the appropriate guild records in London. The following entry was found in the records of the Worshipful Company of Carpenters at the Guildhall
2 CONC , in the minutes of a meeting held on 18 December 1634: "Received of Francis Stiles for apprenticing Thomas Barber son of John Barber of Stamford in the County of Lincoln, yeoman, deceased, from St Thomas's day next for 9 years. 2
2 CONC s 2d". (which is 2 shillings, 2 pence).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the parish registers in Stamford and the adjacent parish of Uffington is the following information: [~ Family History Library, Salt Lake City, Films ~]
2 CONT
