2 CONC he ship "Christian" (John White, Master), which left London 16 Mar 1634 (Julian Calendar), and arrived 3 months later in Boston June 1635. Each of the passengers had a certificate which read in part: "with certificate from St. Mil
2 CONC dred, Bread Street, London, and having taken the oaths, to be transported to New England from London in the "Christian". [~ from the Public Record Office, Westminster Hall, London ~]
2 CONT
2 CONT After 10 days at Boston the Christian sailed up the Connecticut River to Windsor, arriving there on the first of July 1635. That same year Thomas was granted a lot of a few acres, extending from Mill Brook, near the old Warham gri
2 CONC stmill, north along both sides of Poquonock Avenue. The author's father was born on this same land, and my brother, sister, and I were brought up on this, the original land grant. After 330 years of continuous Barber ownership, th
2 CONC e land has now all been sold. The Pequot War in 1637, which according to the settlers was precipitated by the Pequot Indians and their continual harassment of the settlers, the friendly Mohegan Indians, and sometimes the Narragans
2 CONC etts, found Thomas a Sargeant, one of 30 soldiers from Windsor who were enrolled under Major Stoughton for 3 weeks and 2 days. Under the leadership of Captain John MASON, the May 26 1637 night attack on the Pequot fort was a compl
2 CONC ete surprise to the sleeping Pequots, and a large percentage of the tribe was massacred. Thomas Barber's bravery (he was inside the Pequot Fort at Mystic during the attack, and along with Edward Pattison, “having no time to reload
2 CONC  their muskets, slew seven fleeing Indians with axes and knives”), gained him honorable mention from Capt. Mason, and in return for this service, in 1641 he was granted 600 acres of land in Massaco, in the western part of Windsor.
2 CONC  Massaco became Simsbury in 1669. On March 28, 1637 Francis Stiles (master carpenter) was ordered to teach his servants, George Chapple, Thomas Cooper and Thomas Barber in the trade of carpentry. The year 1645 found Thomas still a
2 CONC n apprentice carpenter. Stiles apparently was slow to finish Thomas's apprenticeship, and needed a court order to force him to do so. Shortly after that Thomas was a free man. At the time of his death in 1662, Thomas may have been
2 CONC  making preparations to move to Northampton MA, where he was offered a home lot and 20 acres. Life was apparently not completely peaceful for Thomas1 BARBER. Court records in 1649 show that he was found liable for a debt to Willia
2 CONC m FRANKLIN. Thomas claimed that he had given the money to Thomas FORD to pay to Franklin. Then in 1650 Sargeant Barber was fined 5 pounds and forced to surrender his rank, for the disorderly striking of Lt. COOKE in an argument ov
2 CONC er church matters; Thomas believed that the church had no right to interfere in temporal matters. This penalty was later canceled when apparently Thomas apologized: "he is affected with his great evil and rash passionate carriage 
2 CONC in striking the Lt." Thomas left an estate appraised at over 132 pounds. A deep mystery surrounds Jane, wife of Thomas. He married her in 1640, but the written record by Matthew Grant gives only the name Jane or Joan. Two of Thoma
2 CONC s's sons married COGGIN ladies, but there is little sign that Jane was a Coggin, as some have suggested. One report (LDS record) has it that Jane COGGIN, b Bedfordshire ENG 1619 was daughter of John COGGIN, b in Bedford, Bedfordsh
2 CONC ire about 1593. It has also been suggested that Thomas BARBER married Jane BANCROFT, widow of John BANCROFT who died in 1637. Jane Bancroft had ties to Windsor thru her daughter Anna, b 1627, who married 1647 John GRIFFIN of Winds
2 CONC or and Simsbury, and her son John, born about 1620, who married in 1650 Hannah DUPPER and had a family in Windsor. But Jane BONYTHON who married John BANCROFT was born in 1573, and would have been way too old to have borne Thomas 
2 CONC BARBER’s children. This theory against the wife being Jane Bancroft was written up in The American Genealogist, Vol 37, p 164, in 1961 by George E McCracken and more or less disproved at that time. He points out that she would hav
2 CONC e had to have borne children for too long a time span - highly unlikely, and also she would have had 2 sons named John and 2 named Thomas - also unlikely. Another account has John BANCROFT born about 1596, died 1637, m. Jane about
2 CONC  1622. That would have meant she was born about 1606 or before, making her about 47 in 1653 when Thomas’s youngest child Josiah Barber was born - not impossible, but very unlikely. It has been said by some that Thomas may have mar
2 CONC ried the daughter of one of the Dutch traders at Old Saybrook, or Hartford, and also that the one he married was “the first white woman to land in Connecticut”. One of Francis STILES' sisters was named Jane, born 1605. She married
2 CONC  in England and presumably remained there. There was a Jane MORDEN or WORDEN, age 35 (in 1635), on the passenger list of the Christian; however I know nothing further about her. It seems she was too old to have borne all of Thomas
2 CONC 's children. There seems as yet no way of knowing who Jane was (an all too frequent problem in genealogy). [~ Windsor Hist Soc:Jay Mack Holbrook, 1992; 1909 Barber Gen; Barbour Index; Lyman Barber Gen; Stiles:Windsor; Lure of the 
2 CONC Litchfield Hills ~] ___
2 CONT
2 CONT It has long been known that Thomas1 Barber, with several others, was apprenticed to Francis Stiles, who was paid to bring them to Windsor CT.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Stiles was a carpenter and London freeman, so it seemed reasonable to search in the appropriate guild records in London. The following entry was found in the records of the Worshipful Company of Carpenters at the Guildhall
2 CONC , in the minutes of a meeting held on 18 December 1634: "Received of Francis Stiles for apprenticing Thomas Barber son of John Barber of Stamford in the County of Lincoln, yeoman, deceased, from St Thomas's day next for 9 years. 2
2 CONC s 2d". (which is 2 shillings, 2 pence). In the parish registers in Stamford and the adjacent parish of Uffington is the following information: [~ Family History Library, Salt Lake City, Films ~] St George Parish, Stamford, Lincoln
2 CONC  County Rebecca, daughter of John Barber, labourer, baptised 17 April 1609 Rebecca, daughter of John Barber, buried 6 Aug 1609 Sara, daughter of John Barber, labourer, baptised 16 Sept 1610 Thomas, son of John and Elizabeth Barber
2 CONC , baptised 25 Dec 1612 All Saints Parish, Stamford Elizabeth LUMLEY, daughter of Thomas LUMLEY, baptised 4 August 1597 (How related?) Elizabeth, daughter of John Barber, baptised 10 or 11 Nov 1616 John, son of John Barber, baptise
2 CONC d 4 Aug 1620 John Barber, buried 21 Apr 1620 Elizabeth Barber, widow, buried 8 Mar 1632 John Barber, son of Widow Barber, buried 22 Mar 1632 Uffington Parish, Lincoln County Elizabeth Barber m William FOWLER, 8 Jun 1590 (? related
2 CONC ) Margaret Barber m John PREESTE, 2 Oct 1607 (? a sister of JohnA) John Barber m Elizabeth LUMLEY, 17 Oct 1608 source: THE BARBERS OF CONNECTICUT
2 CONT
2 CONT FIRST THREE GENERATIONS, complete, Part 1 <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Sergeant, Indentured 28 Mar 1637.
2 CONT
2 CONT MILITARY 1st- Sergeant Thomas Barber, immigrated from England, and served as Sergeant in Pequot War- see description of battle pg 16 Barber Genealogy [Reference: Genealogy of Our Barber Family 1614-1965 by Lucy James COLE Flemming
2 CONC - Military Record of Barber (Our) Lineage]
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES Part of the Genealogy of the Barber Family (Handwritten by ?Col Levi Barber vs Capt Levi Barber) First Generation Thomas Barber of Mildred Bradstreet England aged 21 years, sailed from London England, in March 1635 in the sh
2 CONC ip 'Christian' for America after taking the oath of Allegiance and Subservience, resided in Dorchester Massachusetts afterwards in Windsor Connecticut, was engaged in teh Pequot war under Stoughton, his wife's name Jane, who with 
2 CONC her husband both died in 1662. Thomas and Jane had 6 children...
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy Mayberry BARBER Cole, Dictated by her father Capt Levi BARBER, and copied by her great grand daughter Linda Jean ENGLE Lackore Summer 2000: "The first settlers of Simsbury Connecticut came from Windsor Connecticut. A very lar
2 CONC ge proportion of the inhabitants as late as 1845 can trace their ancestry to that small flock who under the pastoral charge of the Rev Mr Warham left England in 1630 and after remaining a short time in Dorchester Massachusetts nea
2 CONC r Boston removed in the fall of 1635 and spring of 1636 to Windsor Connecticut."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Thomas Barber- born about 1614 in County of Bedfordshire England. He came to Windsor Connecticut with a party fitted out by Richard Saltonstall, under Francis Stiles, a master carpenter of London. The Saltonstall party sailed on 
2 CONC 16 Mar 1635 on the ship, 'Christian'. "Thomas Barber's residence was located 'upon an ancient road which runs SW from the revulet intersecting the Poquonnack road above the old mill.' "Thomas was a soldier with the rank of Sergean
2 CONC t in the Pequot War. He distinguished himself by his bravery in a number of fights with the Pequots and particularly taking of a fort, which the Indians considered inpregnable (description of battle on pg 16 of Barber Genealogy). 
2 CONC He was granted 600 acres of land in 1641 in locality called by Indian name 'Massaco'. "Records of town of Northampton Massachusetts: 'At a town meeting 24 Apr 1661 voted and agreed that he be made an inhabitant of Northampton with
2 CONC  plat of ground of 20 acres.' "He died 11 Sep 1662, married to Jane or Joan (surname not known) daughter of Dutch settler- said to have been first white woman to land in Connecticut. She died 10 Sep 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.geneal.net/446.htm <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> FOUNDING FATHER (1 of 7) of the COMMONWEALTH of Connecticut. Arrived in America March 16,1634 on the ship "Christian."Settled in Windsor,Conn. in 1635 wit
2 CONC h the Saltonstall party, under Francis Stiles. indentured servant from 1635-1642 for English nobleman sponsor to clear land, learn carpentry trade (building houses, furniture,etc.) in return granted/deeded acreage by the King's re
2 CONC presentative.
2 CONT
2 CONT The West Simsbury Historical Society, West Simsbury,CT. has 100s of documents relating to THE BARBER FOUNDING FATHERS of Connecticut. And maps to their burial plots. Back in 1987 I was able to locate burial stones of Lt. Thomas an
2 CONC d Samuel Barber flagstone headstones through contacting local funeral homes cemetery maps. There was a broken-off stone aside Lt. Thomas Barber's worn headstone, most likely the original grave marker for FOUNDING FATHER SGT. THOMA
2 CONC S BARBER! - Jerry Barber 1/3/2011. <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Sgt. THOMAS1 BARBER (JohnA) baptised at St George Parish, Stamford, Lincolnshire, England 25 Dec 1612; b probably 21 Dec 1612 (St Thomas’s Day); d Windsor CT
2 CONC  11 Sep 1662; m Windsor 7 Oct 1640 Jane ‑‑‑, b ; d Windsor 10 Sep 1662; r Windsor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before coming to New England Thomas was apprenticed on 18 Dec 1634 for a period of 9 years (until December 1643) under Francis STILES, a master carpenter from Millbrook, Bedfordshire, England. Stiles was contracted by Sir Richard 
2 CONC SALTONSTALL to bring apprentices to Windsor, and to build houses in America for Englishmen who were to follow. Thomas Barber at the age of 21 was among the twenty apprentices plus others who sailed with Stiles for New England in t
2 CONC he ship "Christian" (John White, Master), which left London 16 Mar 1634 (Julian Calendar), and arrived 3 months later in Boston June 1635. Each of the passengers had a certificate which read in part: "with certificate from St. Mil
2 CONC dred, Bread Street, London, and having taken the oaths, to be transported to New England from London in the "Christian". [~ from the Public Record Office, Westminster Hall, London ~] After 10 days at Boston the Christian sailed up
2 CONC  the Connecticut River to Windsor, arriving there on the first of July 1635. That same year Thomas was granted a lot of a few acres, extending from Mill Brook, near the old Warham gristmill, north along both sides of Poquonock Ave
2 CONC nue. The author's father was born on this same land, and my brother, sister, and I were brought up on this, the original land grant. After 330 years of continuous Barber ownership, the land has now all been sold. The Pequot War in
2 CONC  1637, which according to the settlers was precipitated by the Pequot Indians and their continual harassment of the settlers, the friendly Mohegan Indians, and sometimes the Narragansetts, found Thomas a Sargeant, one of 30 soldie
2 CONC rs from Windsor who were enrolled under Major Stoughton for 3 weeks and 2 days. Under the leadership of Captain John MASON, the May 26 1637 night attack on the Pequot fort was a complete surprise to the sleeping Pequots, and a lar
2 CONC ge percentage of the tribe was massacred. Thomas Barber's bravery (he was inside the Pequot Fort at Mystic during the attack, and along with Edward Pattison, “having no time to reload their muskets, slew seven fleeing Indians with
2 CONC  axes and knives”), gained him honorable mention from Capt. Mason, and in return for this service, in 1641 he was granted 600 acres of land in Massaco, in the western part of Windsor. Massaco became Simsbury in 1669. On March 28, 
2 CONC 1637 Francis Stiles (master carpenter) was ordered to teach his servants, George Chapple, Thomas Cooper and Thomas Barber in the trade of carpentry. The year 1645 found Thomas still an apprentice carpenter. Stiles apparently was s
2 CONC low to finish Thomas's apprenticeship, and needed a court order to force him to do so. Shortly after that Thomas was a free man. At the time of his death in 1662, Thomas may have been making preparations to move to Northampton MA,
2 CONC  where he was offered a home lot and 20 acres. Life was apparently not completely peaceful for Thomas1 BARBER. Court records in 1649 show that he was found liable for a debt to William FRANKLIN. Thomas claimed that he had given th
2 CONC e money to Thomas FORD to pay to Franklin. Then in 1650 Sargeant Barber was fined 5 pounds and forced to surrender his rank, for the disorderly striking of Lt. COOKE in an argument over church matters; Thomas believed that the chu
2 CONC rch had no right to interfere in temporal matters. This penalty was later canceled when apparently Thomas apologized: "he is affected with his great evil and rash passionate carriage in striking the Lt." Thomas left an estate appr
2 CONC aised at over 132 pounds. A deep mystery surrounds Jane, wife of Thomas. He married her in 1640, but the written record by Matthew Grant gives only the name Jane or Joan. Two of Thomas's sons married COGGIN ladies, but there is li
2 CONC ttle sign that Jane was a Coggin, as some have suggested. One report (LDS record) has it that Jane COGGIN, b Bedfordshire ENG 1619 was daughter of John COGGIN, b in Bedford, Bedfordshire about 1593. It has also been suggested that
2 CONC  Thomas BARBER married Jane BANCROFT, widow of John BANCROFT who died in 1637. Jane Bancroft had ties to Windsor thru her daughter Anna, b 1627, who married 1647 John GRIFFIN of Windsor and Simsbury, and her son John, born about 1
2 CONC 620, who married in 1650 Hannah DUPPER and had a family in Windsor. But Jane BONYTHON who married John BANCROFT was born in 1573, and would have been way too old to have borne Thomas BARBER’s children. This theory against the wife
2 CONC  being Jane Bancroft was written up in The American Genealogist, Vol 37, p 164, in 1961 by George E McCracken and more or less disproved at that time. He points out that she would have had to have borne children for too long a tim
2 CONC e span - highly unlikely, and also she would have had 2 sons named John and 2 named Thomas - also unlikely. Another account has John BANCROFT born about 1596, died 1637, m. Jane about 1622. That would have meant she was born about
2 CONC  1606 or before, making her about 47 in 1653 when Thomas’s youngest child Josiah Barber was born - not impossible, but very unlikely. It has been said by some that Thomas may have married the daughter of one of the Dutch traders a
2 CONC t Old Saybrook, or Hartford, and also that the one he married was “the first white woman to land in Connecticut”.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Francis STILES' sisters was named Jane, born 1605. She married in England and presumably remained there. There was a Jane MORDEN or WORDEN, age 35 (in 1635), on the passenger list of the Christian; however I know nothing fu
2 CONC rther about her. It seems she was too old to have borne all of Thomas's children.
2 CONT
2 CONT There seems as yet no way of knowing who Jane was (an all too frequent problem in genealogy). [~ Windsor Hist Soc:Jay Mack Holbrook, 1992; 1909 Barber Gen; Barbour Index; Lyman Barber Gen; Stiles:Windsor; Lure of the Litchfield Hi
2 CONC lls ~]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Thomas and Jane, born at Windsor:
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN2, baptised 24 Jul 1642; m/1 Bathsheba COGGIN; m/2 Mrs. Hannah (GARDNER) BANCROFT.
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMAS2, baptised 14 Jul 1644; m Mary PHELPS.
2 CONT
2 CONT SARAH2, baptised 19 Jul 1646; m Windsor 26 Nov 1663 Timothy HALE, b about 1642, d Suffield CT 26 Jul 1689, probably son of John and Dorothy (ROYLE) HEALD of Concord and Springfield MA. John HEALD came from Berwick-on-Tweed, Cheshi
2 CONC re Eng to Concord MA. It is also possible that Timothy was son of Thomas and Jane (LORD) HALE of Hartford. Timothy and Sarah lived at Windsor and Suffield. [~ Barbour Index; Hale,House;Corresp:Angela Cosby ~]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sarah and Timothy: i Sarah HALE3, b 9 Apr or Aug 1665; m John RISING, b Salem MA 1660, son James and Elizabeth (HINSDALE) RISING; r Suffield. They had 9 children. A son of Sarah and John, John RISING, was taken as a ch
2 CONC ild from Deerfield MA during the Deerfield Massacre in 1704, and brought to Canada. He married another English captive there in 1715, and they had a large family that married into the French community there.
2 CONT
