1 NAME Thomas /Barber/ II, of Simsbury
2 GIVN Thomas
2 SURN Barber
2 NSFX II, of Simsbury
2 _MARNM Barber
1 NAME //
2 GIVN
2 SURN Barber
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Simsbury Cemetery (Hop Meadow Cemetery, prior entry w/ dif birth yr)
2 ADDR
3 CITY Simsbury
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 14 JUL 1644
2 ADDR
3 CITY Windsor
3 STAE Connecticut Colony
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 MAY 1713
2 ADDR
3 CITY Simsbury
3 STAE Province of Connecticut
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 OCCU carpenter
1 TITL Lt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From [http://www.simsburyhistory.org/SimsHistory/FirstSettlers.html First Settlers of Simsbury]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber, an original patentee of Simsbury, was the son of Thomas Barber of Windsor who came from England with the Saltonstall party under Francis Stiles in 1635. Thomas Barber, Jr. probably learned the carpentry trade from h
2 CONC is father. We know that he built the first meeting house, church and gristmill in Simsbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barber received the commission of Lieutenant in the local militia. A famous story told about him is the incident of the drum warning. Apparently he noticed Indians surrounding the town and went onto his roof with a drum and beat o
2 CONC ut a warning that was heard by the militia company in Windsor who then marched to Simsbury's defense.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barber married Mary Phelps, the daughter of William Phelps, Sr. and Mary Dover Phelps, on December 17, 1663. Mary Phelps was born on March 2, 1644, in Windsor; when her husband died on May 10, 1713, she and five of their eight chi
2 CONC ldren shared his estate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber is buried in the Hopmeadow Cemetery near the site of the now lost meetinghouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Thomas and Mary Phelps Barber
2 CONT * 1. John (born 1 November 1, 1664; married Mary Holcomb; died 1 March 1, 1711/12)
2 CONT * 2. Marcy also called Mary (born January 11, 1666)
2 CONT * 3. Sarah (born July 12, 1669; married Lieutenant Andrew Robe) 
2 CONT * 4. Joanna (born 1670; married first Josiah Adkins; married second Benjamin Colt)
2 CONT * 5. Annie (born 1671; married Jonathan Higley died November 15, 1722)
2 CONT * 6. Thomas, Jr. (born October 7, 1672; married Abigail Buel; died 17 Jul 17, 1714)
2 CONT * 7. Samuel (born May 17, 1673; married Sarah Holcomb; died December 18, 1725) 
2 CONT * 8. Infant (born 1677)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT This Thomas did not have these children    AND The CT and MA Barbers are not the same man or family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Savage reported  [http://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Barber/6000000026178155442 Thomas] of Gloucster MA moved to Newbury in 1669 to marry Anne Chase d.. o f Aquila. Newbury vital records prove this. They had Thomas1672 and Elizabet
2 CONC h then  moved to Suffield CT to have Joseph and Benjamin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bad merge?
2 CONT
2 CONT Corrected May 2914.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ==more==
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: Carpenter
2 CONT
2 CONT     After Thomas and Mary were married, they lived in a house built by Thomas in Windsor, on a lot he purchased in 1664. In about 1670, he moved his family to Simsbury along with his brother, Samuel and family.
2 CONT
2 CONT     On March 3, 1676, during the King Phillip's War, Lieutenant Thomas Barber of the original military company of Simsbury was instrumental in saving the Simsbury residents, according to an old tradition. Some of Philip's Wampanoa
2 CONC g Indians threatened to attack the town. Thomas ascended the roof of his house, in the place called Hopmeadow, and beat an alarm on his drum. With the wind being favorable, the alarm was heard in Windsor, some seven or eight miles
2 CONC  away. Hearing the alarm, the Windsor militia quickly assembled and rushed to Simsbury, where they were able to safely evacuate the people of Simsbury to Windsor. The Indian, King Phillip, burned and destroyed everything on March 
2 CONC 26t, 1676. After several months the people of Simsbury returned to their community and began the task of rebuilding.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Once the town was rebuilt, life in Simsbury was due to change. Up to this point, the grain grown by the farmers had to be hauled to the Warham Gristmill in Windsor, which was not an easy task due to road conditions and attache
2 CONC s from Indians and wild animals. Thomas Barber had proven his leadership ability during the King Phillip's War and now his skills, along with those of 3 other men, were contracted by the town to build and run a sawmill and gristmi
2 CONC ll. The Simsbury gristmill was built in about 1680 and was still in operation until the early 1950's. Thomas Barber's carpentry skills, passed on from his father, were much in demand and he later built the first church in Simsbury
2 CONC  in 1683. He was hired by the town in 1671 to build a meeting house for public worship, but for 12 years there was a better argument over which side of the river it should be built. Finally 2 slips of paper were place in a hat and
2 CONC  one was drawn in favor of the west side of the river. Once it was completed, the new building was used for 60 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Thomas Barber married Mary Phelps and they eight children, John Barber, Mary Barber, Sarh Barber (Robe), Joanna Barber (Adkins) (Colt), Anne Barber (Higley), Thomas Barber, Samuel Barber, and and infant child who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT ______
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber, an original patentee of Simsbury, was the son of Thomas Barber of Windsor who came from England with the Saltonstall party under Francis Stiles in 1635. He probably learned his carpentry trade from his father. We kn
2 CONC ow that he built the first meeting house, church and gristmill
2 CONT
2 CONT in Simsbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received a commission of Lieutenant in the local militia. A famous story told about him is the incident of the drum warning. Apparently he noticed Indians surrounding the town and went onto his roof with a drum and beat out a w
2 CONC arning that was heard by the militia company in Windsor who then marched to Simsbury's defense.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Mary Phelps b. 2 Mar 1644 in Windsor, daughter of William Phelps Sr., Mary Dover, in 17 Dec 1663 and had 8 children. At the time of his death 10 May 1713 his wife and five children were living and shared in his estate. 
2 CONC He is buried in the Hopmeadow Cemetery near the sight of the now nonexistent meetinghouse.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT upon moving  from Windsor,CT to West Simsbury,CT. carpeneter by trade, built the first meeting house there.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Barber-1371
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber II (1644 - 1713)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lt. Thomas Barber [uncertain] II
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 14 Jul 1644 in Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut Colonymap
2 CONT ANCESTORS ancestors Son of Thomas Barber and Jane (Unknown) Barber Brother of John Barber, Sarah (Barber) Hale, Samuel Barber, Mercy (Barber) Norton and Josiah Barber Husband of Mary (Phelps) Barber — married 17 Dec 1663 in Windso
2 CONC r, Hartford, Connecticutmap DESCENDANTS descendants Father of John Barber, Mary Barber, Sarah (Barber) Robe, Anna Barber, Joanna (Barber) Colt, Thomas Barber III, Samuel Barber, Ann (Barber) Higley and Unnamed Barber Died 10 May 1
2 CONC 713 in Simsbury, Hartford, Connecticutmap Profile managers: Alva Crom private message [send private message] and Ed Poor private message [send private message] Profile last modified 18 Dec 2020 | Created 17 May 2012 This page has 
2 CONC been accessed 1,976 times.
2 CONT
2 CONT This profile is part of the Simsbury, Connecticut One Place Study. Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber was born on 14 July 1644 in Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut. His father Thomas Barber was born on 21 Dec 1612 in Saint George parish, Stamford, Lincolnshire, England, and baptized on 25 Dec 1612 in Saint George parish
2 CONC , Stamford, Lincolnshire, England). His mother Jane Coggins was born in 1619 in Bedford, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Barber was lieutenant of the first military company of Simsbury, and was known as Lieut. Thomas. He built the first saw-mill and grist-mill in Simsbury, also the first church, regarding the location of which there was some 
2 CONC contention among the settlers, which was finally decided by lot, and the location fixed in the southern end of Terry's Plains.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas wed Mary Phelps (bapt. 2 Mar 1644 in Windsor, Hartford, CT) in Dec 1663 in Windsor, Hartford, CT.[1] Thomas and Mary had at least three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Barber, b. 1 Nov 1664 in Windsor, Hartford, CT; MARY BARBER, b. 11 Jan 1666/1667 in Windsor, Hartford, CT; Thomas Barber, b. 7 Oct 1671 in Simsbury, Hartford, CT. Thomas Barber died on 10 March or May, 1701 at Simsbury. He is
2 CONC  buried in Hop Meadow Cemetery and stone can be viewed on Find A Grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lt. Thomas Barber Birth: 14 July 1644 Windsor, Hartford, CT. Death: 10 March 1701, Simsbury, Hartford, CT[2] Death: 10 May 1713, Place: Simsbury, Hartford, Connecticut[3] Marriage: 17 Dec 1663, CT. Military: Lieutenant of the firs
2 CONC t military company of Simsbury Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ Welles ↑ Stiles ↑ Willson See also:
2 CONT
2 CONT Phelps, Noah Amherst. History of Simsbury, Granby, and Canton, from 1642 to 1845 (Case, Tiffany and Burnham, Hartford, 1845) Page 168 Some Early Records and Documents of and Relating to the Town of Windsor, Connecticut, 1639-1703 
2 CONC (The Connecticut Historical Society, Hartford, 1930) Page 24 Willson, Lillian May. Barber Genealogy (John Barber White, Haverhill, Mass. Press of the Nichols Print., 1909) Stiles, Henry Reed The History and Genealogies of Ancient 
2 CONC Windsor, Connecticut (Hartford, CT., Case, Lockwood & Brainard, 1891) Sons of the American Revolution. A National Register of the Society, Sons of the American Revolution (A. H. Kellogg, 1902) Connecticut, Births and Christenings,
2 CONC  1649-1906 freepages. 19621550 Find A Grave. Birth: 14 Jul 1644 Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut Death: 10 May 1713 Simsbury, Hartford, Connecticut Hop Meadow Cemetery, Simsbury, Hartford County, Connecticut. wife: Mary Phelps paren
2 CONC ts: Thomas Barber & Jane Coggin FamilySearch., entry for Thomas BARBER. Welles, Edwin. Births, Marriages, and Deaths Returned from Hartford, Windsor, and Fairfield, and Entered in the Early Land Records of the Colony of Connecticu
2 CONC t (Case, Lockwood & Brainard Co., 1898) Windsor Marriages, Folio 45. Page 10 US and Intl Marriages. Thomas Barber, born 1644, married Mary Phelps in 1663 in CT. Family Data Collection. Lt Thomas Barber, husband of Mary Phelps. Son
2 CONC  of Sgt Thomas Barber and Joan Coggins. Born 14Jul1644 in Masace Area, CT. Died 10May1713 in Simsburyh CT. New England Marriages. Thomas Barber married Mary on 17Dec1661 in New England. CT Census. Thomas Barber lived in Windsor in
2 CONC  1669. CT Probate Records. Thomas Barber Sr. Date of Will: 21May1713. Text included. Mass Marriages. Thomas Barber married Anne Chase on 27 April1671 in Newbury. Possibly another Thomas or his second wife? Family Histories. "...Ma
2 CONC ry , born March 2, 1644. She married Thomas Barber, December 13, 1660. They removed to and settled at Simsbury..." The Great Migration. Thomas Barber, born 14Jul1644 in Windsor. 7 household members are listed.
2 CONT
