2 CONT www.phelprica/windsor-second-generation.aspsfamilyhistory.com/history/ame
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William Phelps went to New England in 1630 on the ship "Mary & John", with his second wife Anne Dover and six children. George Phelps, who may have been a brother of William Phelps, sailed on the same ship. They sailed from Plymou
2 CONC th, England on 20 Mar 1630 and arrived at Nantasket (later renamed Hull), Massachusetts on 30 May 1630. This company founded a settlement at Dorchester, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Phelps applied to be made a freeman on 19 Oct 1630 and was admitted 18 May 1631. He was deputy from Dorchester to the Massachusetts Bay General Court on 9 May 1632 and 6 May 1635. While in Dorchester he served also as cons
2 CONC table, selectman, and member of a boundary committee.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 Mar 1635/6, William Phelps was appointed by Massachusetts Bay as one of the commissioners for the new towns on the Connecticut River.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Mr. Warham and 60 members of the congregation in Dorchester moved to Connecticut to settle the new town of Windsor (at first also called Dorchester). William Phelps and his family and George Phelps were included in those movi
2 CONC ng to the new settlement, although it is probable that they made the move in the spring of 1636. He was second on the list of men who came from the Dorchester church to Windsor with Mr. Warham.
2 CONT
2 CONT William served in a number of positions after moving to Windsor, including deputy for Windsor to the Connecticut General Court for a number of years. He was one of the magistrates who participated in the adoption of a constitution
2 CONC  for the Connecticut Colony in 1638.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first residence in Windsor was in an area that was covered by water in the great flood of 1639, so he moved to an area in the Highlands which came to be known as Phelps Meadows, on a road running to Poquonock.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are records of other purchases and sales of lands by William Phelps in Windsor. A settlement deed which he signed on 22 Apr 1660, dividing his property among his family, was entered on the Windsor register on 26 Jul 1672.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT * "The English Origin of William Phelps of Dorchester, Mass. and Windsor, Conn., with Notes on His Marriages," by Myrtle Stevens Hyde in The American Genealogist, Vol. 65, Jul 1990 p. 161.
2 CONT
2 CONT * "The Phelps Family of America and their English Ancestors," by Oliver Seymour Phelps and Andrew T. Servin (Eagle Publishing, Pittsfield, Massachusetts, 1899) p. 88-9
2 CONT
2 CONT * "The Great Migration: Immigration to New England 1620 - 1633," by Robert Charles Anderson in New England Historic Genealogy Society, Boston, Massachusetts, 1995, Vol. 3, p. 1444-6
2 CONT
