2 CONT Sources for William PHELPS and his family: 1) The Great Migration Begins, Immigrants to New England 1620-1633, Vol. III, P-W, p. 1444-6 [GMB: 3, 1444-6]. Other sources were also mentioned here. 2) Vital records at Crewkerne Parish
2 CONC , Crewkerne, Somerset County, England. They were written in "Secretary's Hand", 3) Phelps & Servin, Vol. 1. pg 85--86. Note: The 2-volume book contains numerous errors and/or omissions. They must be verified with primary records. 
2 CONC  4) Dates were from various sources such as vital records, wills, probate, grants, etc. (see GMB to see other sources)  NOTE: Brackets [W_] indicate this person is a descendant of William and is numbered according to the Phelps & 
2 CONC Servin's 2 volumes.  5) Numerous articles in genealogy magazines have been written about William's origins and his family, including TAG, NEHGS, and others.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ Journey to America - Life in Dorchester, MA and Windsor, CT: William Phelps and his second wife Anne and four children William, Samuel, Nathaniel, and Joseph boarded the ship 
2 CONC Mary and John, owned by Roger Ludlow who was one of the assistants of the Massachusetts Bay Company, on March 20, 1630. They and other families, about 140 emigrants in all, were all recruited by the Rev. John White of Dorchester, 
2 CONC Dorset Co., England. They were from the "West Country" of England consisting of three counties Somerset, Dorset and Devon. Many came from the towns of Dorchester, and Bridport, Dorset, Crewkerene and Taunton, Somerset or Exeter, D
2 CONC evon. They were all part of the church led by the Rev. John Warham. Under the command of Capt. Squeb (or Squibb), they sailed from Plymouth, Devon Co., England. It arrived at Nantasket, near present-day Hull, Massachusetts on May 
2 CONC 30, 1630 without casualty. Soon, other ships arrived. This was a part of the Great Migration. They settled at Columbia Point, which the native Americans called "Mattaponnock". They renamed it Dorchester in 1630 in honor of Rev. Wh
2 CONC ite who organized the Dorchester Company. Today, Dorchester later became part of South Boston. It was annexed by the city of Boston in 1970.(From the Mary and John Clearing House, other historical accounts.)  [NOTE: Mary and John 
2 CONC in 1630 was not part of the Winthrop Fleet that came two weeks later after it arrived at the American shores. Mary and John brought emigrants from England to Massachusetts two more times, in 1633 and again in 1634. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1635 William moved his family to what is now Windsor, Hartford Co., Connecticut. It was formerly named Dorchester, but they renamed it to Windsor in 1637 under court order presided by William. He and many other men who founded 
2 CONC Dorchester were also founders and first settlers of Windsor. In other words, Mr. Warham's church moved to Windsor. They played various important roles in the community. These founding fathers' names are engraved on the Founder's M
2 CONC onument on the Palisado Green, near Strong's House (formerly known as Flier's House), where Windsor Historical Society is situated today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. William Phelps was regarded as an important figure in both Dorchester and Windsor. According to a noted historian Henry Reed Stiles, he "was one of the most prominent and highly respected men in the colony." (Note that William
2 CONC  Phelps was given the title "Mr". This title was given only to men with distinction, while the military titles were always used.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. William Phelps devoted most of his life serving. He served in varying governing capacities including judicial matters and held offices in early colonial government. 
2 CONT * On 19 Oct 1630, William Phelps applied to be made freeman. He was admitted on 18 May 1631. This allowed him to purchase land and to participate in juries. GMB 3:1444
2 CONT * On 9 Nov 1630, he was one of twelve jurors and he was selected as a foreman for the first grand jury trial in the New England Colony, on the death of Austin Bratcher.
2 CONT
2 CONT * On 27 Sep 1631, he was chosen Constable of Dorchester, MA, assigned to serve on committees given authority to settle land and boundary disputes, and given other key responsibilities in administering the affairs of the new town, 
2 CONC including serving on the General Court. 
2 CONT * He also was a Lot layer, Fence Viewer, and a Land Surveyor. He was twice a Deputy for Dorchester to Massachusetts Bay Colony in 1632 and in 1635. 
2 CONT * Between 1633 and 1635, William and a few others were appointed as members of several committees by the General Court. They helped lay out boundaries between Boston and Roxbury, Wessaguscus and Barecove, and Hingham and Weymouth.
2 CONC  He also surveyed Mount Wollaston in 1634. 
2 CONT * In 1634, William Phelps became one of three delegates from Dorchester to the first General Court in the colony. 
2 CONT * 5 May 1635, William Phelps was a member of the colony's General Court held in Newtown, now Charlestown, MA.
2 CONT * On 3 Mar 1635/6, appointed by the Massachusetts Bay Colony, William was one of the 7 commissioners to govern the new towns on the "The Great River" before emigrating to Windsor. He governed the new colony in Connecticut in its f
2 CONC irst year.
2 CONT * On 3 Mar 1636, the first legal committee was formed, consisting of William Phelps and 6 other men were authorized and given power to hear cases using witnesses, to evaluate, and to make impartial judicial decisions in Windsor. 
2 CONT *On 21 Feb 1637, William presided over the colony's General Court and ordered the town name change: "It is ordered y the plantacon called Dorchester shall bee called Windsor." 
2 CONT *On 1 May 1637, Mr. William Phelps, presiding over the committee, authorized them to start an offensive war against the Pequots. He helped organize the expedition to go after them. This conflict lasted two years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. William Phelps was a Magistrate , i.e., an old a term used for governor to govern a colony. By 1638, Massachusetts Colony admitted that the Connecticut Colony was out of its jurisdiction and it was practicing self-rule. The pe
2 CONC ople of Windsor, Wethersfield, and Hartford met in Hartford on 2 Jan 1639 and adopted the constitution for the Connecticut Colony. This document made it clear that it does not recognize authority superior to that delegated by the 
2 CONC people other than God. Mr. Roger Ludlow drew up the constitution, along with the assistance of Mr. William Phelps and other magistrates. 
2 CONT * The first government consisted of five magistrates (officers) of a legislative, judicial and executive character, chosen by the freemen of the colony, and the house of assistants chosen by the towns. This continued until 1665. 
2 CONT *Mr. William Phelps held of the Office of Magistrate from 1639 to 1645, making him the first Governor of Connecticut Colony. He again held the office from 1645 to 1649, and again from 1656 to 1662. He also served as an assistant, 
2 CONC equivalent to Lieutenant Governor. 
2 CONT * He was made a Deputy for Windsor to Connecticut General several times from 1645 to 1657.  Sources: P&S, Vol 1, pg. 72-80, GMB 3:1444-6, Dorchester, MA records, Massachusetts Colonial records, Connecticut State Library, Windsor r
2 CONC ecords, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Note regarding the terms Magistrate, assistant: A Magistrate was what they were called back then to govern the Colonies. William Phelps and the others all took turns governing the Colony. John Winthrop was the head Governor in Bo
2 CONC ston and they were still under the Kings watch.]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1635 William first lived in Windsor, in the lowland of "the Rivulet" [Farmington River] that flows into "the Great River" [Connecticut River]. He was granted a lot that was 100 feet wide, next to Bray Rossiter, west of the Rivu
2 CONC let. This land was flooded in 1638-9. Due to its tendency to flood, he moved to higher ground above the First Meadows further north in 1639. This was known as Phelps Meadows. It is situated on the most northerly lot on the west si
2 CONC de of the rivulet, on the east side of what is now East St leading north to Poquonock Road and north of west-east road Pleasant St. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Phelps purchased land from the Indians on more than one occasion. In a deed dated March 31, 1665, Phelps recorded that he had purchased a parcel of land about 30 years previously from Sehat, a Paquanick sachem. He was unable to pr
2 CONC ovide title and prove his previous payment, forcing him to buy the land again. He paid to Sehat’s descendant "Nassahegan, an Indian sachem" and his kinsmen "four trucking coats" and wampum. He had previously paid "two coats and 40
2 CONC  shillings in wampum for a third coat, and six bushels of Indian corn, and fifteen shillings in wampum for the fourth coat; and fifteen shillings in wampum is at six a penny." Many records of purchase and sale of land by Mr. Willi
2 CONC am Phelps are recorded in the Windsor land records. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Will and Testament of Mr. William Phelps,  or properly speaking, his Settlement Deed
2 CONT
2 CONT These presents testify, that I, William Phelps of Windsor, on Connecticut, in consideration of a marriage concluded between my son Timothy, on the one part, and Mary, the daughter of Edward Griswold, on the other part; have given 
2 CONC and granted, and by these presents do give and grant unto my son, that he, the said Timothy, shall jointly enjoin and possess, together with me, all my housing, lands and accommodations, as also all my estate, both real and person
2 CONC al, both within door and without, with all the property emoluments, products, and income of the same, during my material life: And my said son in to inhabit and dwell in my house, with me and my wife, in joint way; and that it sha
2 CONC ll continue during my material life; and if my wife shall survive me, she have and enjoy in a joint way with my son the estate for her maintainence as before expressed. But if my wife chooses to settle in any place and to leave th
2 CONC e house, then my son Timothy shall pay yearly to my wife, the sum of ten pounds during her material life, and in case I myself in my lifetime, or my wife after my decease, in her lifetime while she abides, to inhabit with my said 
2 CONC son Timothy, she see cause or desire it, I do reserve power for both myself and for her, after my decease to dispose a barrel or two of cider and some apples yearly without any harm to the premises, and likewise I do reserve like 
2 CONC liberty for myself and my wife, to dispose of my wearing apparel, and whom we shall meet to enjoy them after our decease. Also I do give full power of bequeathing the great brass pan at her decease; and my son Timothy is to carry 
2 CONC the improvements of the whole Estate, and to order and dispose of the stock, so far as the necessity of our subsistance shall require, and after my decease and the decease of my wife, my said son Timothy shall have and enjoy all m
2 CONC y whole Estate fore mentioned to him and his heirs forever; always provided that in case my said son Timothy shall die and leave no natural heirs begotten by him, that shall either not attain the age of twenty one years or marry, 
2 CONC then the one-half of my lands excepting the orchard and pasture down to the bridge, that goeth into the meadow; also the upper pasture by the house that shall belong to the house, shall return to William, the son of my son Samuel.
2 CONC  Also my son Timothy is to pay out of the Estate: Imprimis to discharge my daughter Mary, with that which is paid, the sum of thirty-four pounds, which is the full portion I allow her. To my son William twenty shillings, to Samuel
2 CONC  ten pounds, to Nathaniel fifteen pounds, to Joseph five pounds – these legacies to my sons to be discharged within two years of my decease.  In consideration of the premises we both have herunto set our hands this 22nd day of Apr
2 CONC il Anno Dom, 1660.
2 CONT
2 CONT Witnesses to the signatures:  Daniel Clark, William Phelps James Alford, Timothy Phelps
2 CONT
2 CONT Entered on the Windsor, Conn., Register, July 26th, 1672.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: Last Will and Testament of Mr. Phelps, from Windsor, in the County of Hartford and Colony of Connecticut in New England, The Phelps family of America and their English Ancestors, Phelps & Servin, Vol. I, p. 84-85/
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ William Phelps' Origins: Several assumptions and myths about Mr. William Phelps' birth ca.1593, origins and parentage are still being perpetuated today, in spite of discoveries since about
2 CONC  1982.
2 CONT
2 CONT The reasons for these errors is from information in the The Phelps Family in America and their English Ancestors, compiled by Phelps & Servin, a two-volume genealogy, was published in 1899 and considered 100% factual information. 
2 CONC Since 1987, discoveries including careful research by Robin Bush, Myrtle Stevens Hyde, Burton W. Spears and the Mary and John Clearing House , The Great Migration project by Robert Charles Anderson, the discovery of Crewkerne pari
2 CONC sh records and yDNA testing, have changed all that. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The major errors regarding William Phelps of Dorchester/Hull Massachusetts are as follows: 1. William Phelps born ca 1593 was from Tewkesbury. 
2 CONT *A William Phelps existed in Tewkesbury but is not genetically linked to the aforementioned William Phelps. More likely, William Phelps was baptized in Crewkerne or environs, including Yeovil and Dorset. 
2 CONT *William of Crewkerne was most likely born earlier than 1599, based on his eldest son.
2 CONT *William Phelps of Tewkesbury, baptized Aug 1599, had a son named Richard christened in Tewkesbury 26 Dec 1619. William of Crewkerne did not have a known son named Richard.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. George Phelps is William Phelps' brother. DNA testing has proven that George Phelps is not a genetic match to William Phelps born 1593 with the result that William and George can not be brothers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other facts: William and George did not live near each other in Windsor, name their children the same, did not have the same education level, nor have the same occupation. All of these known facts added up to them NOT being brothe
2 CONC rs. George adopted the surname Phelps though he was NOT born a Phelps from Crewkerne, England. Around 1990, this fact was published in the New England Gen. Society journal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. William and George sailed together on Mary and John at the same time.  William sailed on Mary and John with his wife and 4 children, George sailed on Recovery. George Phelps is mentioned in Great Migration series Vol 5, M-P, pg
2 CONC . 445-450. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. William's parents are William and Dorothy Phelps. William's parents are still UNKNOWN. No evidence has yet been uncovered. 
2 CONT *Widowed Dorothy Phelps, the mother of the William Phelps of Tewkesbury, named her brother-in-law Edward Phelps in her will. He in turn, in his 1636 will (when he was of Tewkesbury), "appointed as an overseer, William Phelps, almo
2 CONC st undoubtedly his nephew, and certainly not the William living in far away New England.'
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. William's wife's was Mary Ann Dover. There is no evidence of this. The two wives' names were conflated into Mary Ann Dover.  William Phelps had two wives named 
2 CONT **1) Mary unknown 
2 CONT **2) Anne Dover
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. William Phelps of Crewkerne had a brother named Richard Phelps. There is no evidence that supports this conclusion.
2 CONT
2 CONT The naming patterns used by Mr. William Phelps and his children did not include George and Richard.  Note that William's children named their children with names like William, Samuel, Nathaniel, Cornelius, Joseph, Timothy, Mary, a
2 CONC nd Sarah, but none of them were named George or Richard. Other names were introduced by the spouse's side
2 CONT
2 CONT Also note that middle names were not used until around 1800 or later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Phelps & Servin Phelps' two volumes The Phelps family in America and their English ancestors unfortunately cannot be considered as a primary source. While it is a valuable resource, it contains numerous errors and omissions. T
2 CONC hey are considered secondary source, meaning that other records must be used to verify the information recorded in these two volumes. These two volumes were compiled by people who were NOT trained researchers and they were not pro
2 CONC fessional genealogists. It was compiled primarily based on correspondence and belief.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several other genealogy books including Families of Ancient Wethersfield, Connecticut, Consisting of Volume II of the History of Ancient Wethersfield, Comprising the Present Towns of Wethersfield, Rocky Hill, and Newington; and of
2 CONC  Glastonbury Prior to its Incorporation in 1693, From Date of Earliest Settlement Until the Present Time, With Extensive Genealogies and Genealogical Notes on Their Families also contain numerous errors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ NOTE: Additional information on William's origins was written to her niece (me) in Sept. 2013 in a series of emails. And with her permission, this info can be found in Phelps Family 
2 CONC History and William Phelps (colonist). NOTE: This information must be fully acknowledged, not copied and pasted without reference. This credit goes to Margaret Phelps Swanson, certified genealogist and co-founder of “Phelps Connec
2 CONC tions." findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
