2 CONT * [http://www.familytreedna.com/public/GRISWOLD/default.aspx?section=yresults Haplogroup R1b1a2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Edward Griswold''' was born 26 Jul 1607 in Kennilworth England, and died 30 Aug 1691 in Windsor Hartford CT. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: not confirmed (see notes).  Possibly George Griswold (1574-1615) and his second wife, Dorothy James.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married (see notes)
2 CONT # Margaret (unknown) abt 1630 in Kennilworth England.  
2 CONT # Sarah 1672 in Windsor Hartford CT, widow of James Bemis 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Edward Griswold and Margaret  are:
2 CONT # Francis Griswold, born 1629; died Oct 1671. Married Sarah Post.
2 CONT # Sarah Griswold, born 1630.
2 CONT # George Griswold, born 1632; died 03 Sep 1704 in Simsbury CT.
2 CONT # John Griswold, born 1635; died 1642.
2 CONT # Anna Griswold, born 19 Aug 1642.
2 CONT # Mary Griswold, born 05 Oct 1644.
2 CONT # Deborah Griswold, born 28 Jun 1646. She married Samuel Buell 18 Nov 1662.
2 CONT # Joseph Griswold, born 22 Mar 1647/48; died 14 Nov 1716. He married Mary Gaylord 10 Jul 1670.
2 CONT # Samuel Griswold, born 18 Nov 1649; died 06 Jul 1672.
2 CONT # John Griswold, born 01 Aug 1652; died 07 Aug 1717. He married Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Griswold, born in England, 1607; married there about 1630, Margaret, whose parentage is unknown. Actual records of his birth and marriage have not been found.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 17 August 1639, Rev. Ephraim Huit arrived in Windsor, Connecticut with his company and immediately entered upon his labors assisting Rev. John Warham. Rev. Huit had been pastor at Knowle and Wroxall, Warwickshire, england, W
2 CONC roxall being a part of Kenilworth parish. He was a religious writer and he was censured by the Bishop of Worcester, which, no doubt, was the moving cause of his organizing his company and for his removal to England. Edward and his
2 CONC  brother Matthew Griswold were members of this company, and for this reason it had been thought that the records of Knowle and Wroxall would disclose some clue as to the parentage of our ancestors, but thus far they are silent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward seems to have been an intellectual type and he speedily became prominent in the affairs of the new community at Windsor, exceedingly active, and was frequently mentioned in colonial records. He served as deputy to the Gener
2 CONC al Court 18 August 1658 through 14 March 1660 and 15 May 1662 through 11 March 1663. In 1659 he built the Old Fort at Springfield for Mr. Pynchon. He was granted land at Poquonoc Plain but did not remove there until the title of t
2 CONC he Indians living there had expired in 1642. He was resident there with two other familes of John Bartlett and Thomas Holcomb, in 1649. His home stood near the highway at the top of the hill and had 29 1/2 acres bounded mostly sou
2 CONC th and west by Stony Brook and east by the Connecticut River. His sons George and Joseph inherited the homestead.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1663, with his son John, Edward removed to Hammonassett, later called Killingworth. The present Clinton, Connecticut, is the original Killingworth; Main Street is the identical ground where the first settlers took their home lo
2 CONC ts. These were surveyed in 1663 by Byron Rossiter of Guilford. Edward was one of the first settlers and doubtless suggested the name from Kenilworth Parish in England, and was the most prominent man in the new settlement, given mu
2 CONC ch credit for first organizing this community. He was its first deputy to the General Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the names entered as planters by the committee we find Edward Griswold, Samuel Beull, and Jonas Westover, and in 1669 these were recorded as freemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward was largely instrumental in organizing the first church and was its first deacon. He frequently served on important civil matters, his services, counsel and guidance evidently much sought after. He served on the committee t
2 CONC o establish a Latin school at New London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancient land records on file at the Office of the Secretary of State, Hartford, Connecticut, show land grants in favor of Edward; one of 200 acres; another of 100 acres given by the town of Killingworth; he was a large landholder 
2 CONC showing the spirit of those English settlers to accumulate large land holdings.
2 CONT
2 CONT ***
2 CONT
