2 CONT Lived in Kenilworth, Warwick, England in 1630.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT == Biography == 
2 CONT
2 CONT Category:Puritan_Great_Migration
2 CONT {{Puritan Great Migration}}
2 CONT ----
2 CONT == Origins ==
2 CONT That he was a son of one George Griswold is supported by a series of depositions given by him and other Griswolds, one of which directly says that his father's name was George:<ref>Genevieve Tylee Kiepura, "Griswold Ancestry in En
2 CONC gland," in ''The American Genealogist,'' 39:176-</ref># Testimony given 15 May 1684 by Edward Griswold, aged about 77, that he came to New England in 1639 [hence born ca. 1607], and by Matthew Griswold, aged about 64, to the same 
2 CONC effect [hence born ca 1620].# George Griswold, aged about 67, testified 9 May 1700 that in youth he lived with his father, Edward Griswold, in Kenilworth, Warwickshire,and had heard his father say that the house they lived in ther
2 CONC e belonged to Edward's brother Matthew, and he had seen a letter from Thomas Griswold of Kenilworth intimating that Thomas lived in said house... [proving that there was a third brother, Thomas]# On 19 Jan 1737/8, Capt. George Gri
2 CONC swold, aged about 72, and Mr. JohnGriswold, aged about 69, sons of George Griwsold, both of Windsor, Conn., testified that their grandfather's name was Edward Griswold, thattheir great-greatfather was George Griswold who had three
2 CONC  sons, the eldest named Edward, the second named Matthew and the third or youngestnamed Thomas, of whom the first two came to New England from "Killigsworth" in Warwickshire; also that said Edward's eldest son has always been repu
2 CONC ted to be Francis Griswold.
2 CONT # Margaret wife of Edward Griswold died 23 Aug 1670.# Will of John Riley named in his will of 13 May 1674 his brother JohnBelding, his brother Emanuel Buck and his cousin Michael Griswold [see ''The American Genealogist,'' 10:51-5
2 CONC 2] One candidate for George (as father of Edward) was the son of Henry Griswold and Dorothy James, but Henry and Dorothy did not marry until 1592, so their son George could not have been old enough to have been father of Edward bo
2 CONC rn 1607. The parish records of Wooten Wawen (a chapelry of Henley) included thebaptisms of Edward, son of George Griswold, 26 July 1607. This is believed to be the immigrant, although there is no Matthew or Michael in the index. T
2 CONC he 28 August 1615 burial of Dousabel, wife of George Griswold in the same parish records, suggests she was his mother, and that his younger brothers had a different mother. This same parish has a baptism of George, son of Roger Gr
2 CONC iswold, 6 Nov 1574, all together identifying a likely origin for Edward's father. : Griswold.  "The English Griswolds were an ancient county family established at Holihull, Warwickshire, England before 1400.  The were descended fr
2 CONC om John Griswold who came from Kenilworth about the middle of the fourteenth century, and settled in Holihull."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT === Birth ===Edward was baptized 26 JUL 1607 in Wooten Wawen, Warwickshire (near Kenilworth), England, the eldest of three <ref>see testimony (above) of 19 Jan 1737/8, by Capt. George Griswold, aged about 72</ref> sons of Griswold
2 CONC -216|George Griswold and Leigh-254|Dousabel Leigh (who may have married 1 July 1594 in Kenilworth, Warwickshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriage ===He married about 1628 Margaret _______. Her maiden name has not been confirmed; we know for certain that it was not Blencow.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Emigration to Windsor, Connecticut ===: Edward emigrated to Windsor, Connecticut (Wethersfield) with his half-brother Matthew in 1639.  Among the other passengers were Mr. William Whiting, Undertaker, and Rev. Ephraim Huet (or
2 CONC  Huitt or Hewett), past minister at Wroxall, Warwickshire, England, of the Puritan faith. Rev Huit, who had been harassed and silenced by Archbishop Laud, left his living at Kenilworth, Warwickshire and brought with him and his fa
2 CONC mily, "Warwickshire neighbours like Edward Griswold".<ref name="Frank">''Dorset Pilgrims: The story of West country Pilgrims who went to New England in the 17th Century'', Frank Thistlethwaite, 1989</ref>
2 CONT : Edward Griswold was a deputy to the General Court in 1658, 1660, 1662, & 1663.  In 1659 he built the Old Fort at Springfield.
2 CONT : He was granted land at Paquonoc (or Poquonoc) north of Windsor, but did not move there until after the title of the Indians was fully extinguished in 1642.  He was a resident there in 1649.  His sons George &Joseph inherited thi
2 CONC s homestead. :: "....Griswold immigrants, Matthew and Edward, whom we know were brothers, both came in 1639 and settled in Windsor, Connecticut.  Anotherbrother of Matthew and Edward remained in England, Thomas, as proved by the d
2 CONC eposition of George Griswold, son of Edward, in 1700...  
2 CONT
2 CONT === Among Founders of Killingworth, Connecticut ===: Deacon Edward Griswold was closely involved in the settling of Killingworth, Connecticut. 
2 CONT : In 1663, Edward and his son John moved to Hammonasset, later called Killingworth, probably from the the name Kenilworth Parish in Warwickshire, England of which Wroxall was a part.  Present Clinton, Connecticut, is the location 
2 CONC of Killingworth. : In 1669, Edward Griswold, and sons-in-law Samuel Buel and Jonas Westover were recorded as freeman in Killingworth.<ref>William Richard Cutter, ''New England Families Genealogical and Memorial''</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT === Death and Burial ===
2 CONT {{FindAGrave|11666989}} : Edward died or was buried 30 AUG 1691 in Killingworth, Hartford, Connecticut.: Burial 30 AUG 1691 Clinton Cemetery, Clinton, Middlesex, Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography Notes ===
2 CONT * [https://familysearch.org/pal:/MM9.2.1/9MGF-ZT6 FamilySearch ] reports that George Griswold, the father of Edward Griswold (b 1607, d 1691New London Ct.) was christened in Apr 1548, and  Edward's mother was named Frances.  
2 CONT
2 CONT : At Hartford Court, 13 Sep 1649.: "This Courte taking into consideracon the many dangers that the familyes of Thomas Holcombe, Edward Grisswold, John Barlitt, Francis Grisswold, and George Grisswold, all of Wyndsor, are in and ex
2 CONC posed unto, by reason of their remoate living from neighbors and nearenes to the Indians, in case they should all leave theire families together without any guard; doth free one souldger of the foremenconed families from training 
2 CONC uppon every training day; each family aforesaid to share hereinaccording to the number of souldgers that are in them:  provided thatman which tarryes at home stands about the aforesaid howses uppon hissentinell posture."    15 May
2 CONC  1662  Edward Griswold served on the Grand Jury of the General Court at Hartford<ref>[http://www.colonialct.uconn.edu/ Connecticut Colonial Records], Vol 1, p.196; p. 379</ref> : Names of the freemen of the Towne of Kennelworth.  
2 CONC Listed with listsfrom other town which were dated in the fall of 1669 although there was no date on the Kennelworth list.  Of the nineteen names, four were my direct ancestors:  Mr. Edward Griswold, Sam: Buel [married to Griswold'
2 CONC s daughter Deborah], John Meigs Senr, Andrew Warde [married to Meigs' daughter Tryall]<ref>[http://www.colonialct.uconn.edu/ ConnecticutColonial Records], Vol. 2, p.525</ref> '''Note:''' <p style="text-align: left;" dir="ltr">Chil
2 CONC dren of Edward are Sarah, b. 1631, George b. 1633, m Mary Holcomb, Francis b. 1634, Liddia b. 1637, Sarah b. 1638, m. Samuel Phelps and Nathaniel Pinney, Annb. 1642, Mary b. 1644, m. Timothy Phelps, Deborah, b. 1646, m. Samuel bue
2 CONC ll, Joseph b. 1647, m. Mary Gaylord, Samuel b. 1649, and John b. 1652</p>. 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
