1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hannah AKA Anna
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see: The Griswold family, England-America : Edward of Windsor, Connecticut, Matthew of Lyme, Connecticut, Michael of Wethersfield, Connecticut by Griswold, Glenn E; https://archive.org/details/griswoldfamilyen02gris/page/16
2 CONC  (Steven Ferry, February 8, 2019.) '''Hannah "Ann" Griswold''' was born 19 JUN 1642 in Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut, and died 21 MAY 1714 in Simsbury, Hartford, Connecticut, USA. She was the daughter of Edward Griswold and Marga
2 CONC ret.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Jonas "Jonah" Westover 19 NOV 1663 in Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut, son of Gabriel Westover and Joanne Gifford. He was born 4 MAY 1628 in Taunton, Somerset, England, and died 15 JAN 1708 in Simsbury, Hartford, Connec
2 CONC ticut.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Griswold-105
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah "Ann" Westover formerly Griswold
2 CONT
2 CONT Born before 19 Jun 1642 in Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of Edward Griswold and Margaret (Unknown) Griswold
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister of Francis Griswold, George Griswold, Sarah (Griswold) Pinney, Lydia Griswold, John Griswold, Mary (Griswold) Phelps, Deborah (Griswold) Buell, Joseph Griswold, Samuel Griswold and	John Griswold Sr
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of Jonas Westover — married 19 Nov 1663 in Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother of Jonah Westover, Margaret Westover, Hannah (Westover) Alvord, Elizabeth (Westover) Baker, Jane (Westover) Byington, Mary (Westover) Case, Sarah Westover, Jonathan Westover and Joanna (Westover) Wilton Died 3 May 1714 in S
2 CONC imsbury, Hartford, Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 7 Aug 2019 | Created 30 Nov 2010
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 19 Jun 1642 and christened 19 Jun 1642 in Windsor, Hartford County, Connecticut. Hannah Griswold was the daughter of Edward Griswold and Margaret Blencow. Note
2 CONT
2 CONT [Theurer gives 19 June as date of birth: all his dates may be Christening dates.] Marriage: 19 Nov 1663 at Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut [1] [2] Death 3 May 1714 Simsbury Center, Hartford, Connecticut.[1][2] [3] Will: Dated 1 Aug
2 CONC  1713, proved 7 Jun 1714. [4] Burial 21 May 1714 Windsor, Hartford, Conn. She married Jonah Westover Nov 19, 1663 in Windsor, Hartford Co, CT. The names Hannah and Anna are often confused. As a result the proof that Hannah wife of
2 CONC  Jonah Westover, and Anna daughter of Edward Griswold baptized in Windsor, are the same, is lacking. However, there doesn't appear to be another Anna or Hannah Griswold who could be the wife of Jonas Westover,[5] and the Westovers
2 CONC  did move to Killingworth at the same time as Edward and other members of the family. Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ 1.0 1.1 Lucius Barnes Barbour, Barbour Collection of Connecticut Vital Records (Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc.) ↑ 2.0 2.1 Jacobus, Donald Lines, The American Genealogist (Whole Number 67, vol 17, No 3, Jan 1941). ↑
2 CONC  Bates, Albert, Simsbury, Connecticut Births, Marriages & Deaths (1898. FHL 974.62/S4 V2b). ↑ Jacobus, Donald Lines, The Second Wife of Thomas Baker. (The American Genealogist, Vol 9, April 1933.). ↑ Glenn E. Griswold, The Griswol
2 CONC d Family, England-America (Middleboro, Massachusetts, 1935), viewed on Ancestry.com, page 23. Welles, Edwin. Births, Marriages, and Deaths Returned from Hartford, Windsor, and Fairfield, and Entered in the Early Land Records of th
2 CONC e Colony of Connecticut (Case, Lockwood & Brainard Co., 1898) Windsor Marriages, Folio 45. Page 10 Find A Grave: Memorial #79338163 Jordan, John W. Genealogical and Personal History of Western Pennsylvania (Lewis Historical Pub. C
2 CONC o., New York, 1915, Vol. 3. pg. 1707-1711)
2 CONT
2 CONT Acknowledgements
2 CONT
2 CONT Kay Tryon, firsthand knowledge.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Westovers of New England, 1649-1800, from "Gen. of the Westover Family of Missouri", by R.T. & O.F. Wester; with additional information compiled by D/M Erkfritz/Westover Repository 7905 Eston Rd. So. , Clarkston, MI 48348.
2 CONT
2 CONT Book - Vol.II Edward Griswold, p.23 -Anna, Hannah, Griswold, bapt. June 19, 1642 (Edward) 
2 CONT
2 CONT While at present we have no direct proof that the following record is correct, circumstances and the records thus far assembled, would tend to show the almost certian proof. Search has not revealed the birth of any Hannah Griswold
2 CONC  who could have been the wife of Jonas Westover. In view of the fact that Anna and Hannah were easily, and were very frequently confounded in the records, usualy by mistake in the sound of the name, it has been thought that the on
2 CONC ly person possible would be Ann or Anna, daughter of Edward. Hence all the records that have been found are placed here. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She married in Windsor, CT; Nov 19, 1663, Jonas Westover, son of John, who died in Simsbury Jan 15, 1708/9, farmer. Removed to Killingworth with Edward Griswold and Samuel Buell. Made freeman of Windso 1658; or Killingworth 1669. 
2 CONC Took his home lot next to Samuel Buell and near that of Edward. Members of the first church in Killingworth. Children recorded, first in Windsor, the others in Killingworth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There were others recorded in Simsbury, where he evidently resided later in life:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Jonas, born 20 Sep 1664
2 CONT # Margaret, born 19 Feb 1666
2 CONT # Hannah, born 8 Apr 1668; married Joseph Alvord
2 CONT # Elizabeth, b orn 3 May 1670
2 CONT # Jane, born 26 Mar 1672; married _____ Byington
2 CONT # Sarah, born ____, died 15 Aug 1718
2 CONT # Mary, born _____, married Samuel Chase
2 CONT # Jonathan
2 CONT # Joanna, b. ______, married Stephen Wilton
2 CONT # 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonas married Hannah Griswold in 1663. Hannah was born in Windsor 19 Aug 1642, daughter of Edward and Margaret Griswold, a prominent family in Windsor, Connecticut. After the birth of their first child they moved souhward to a new
2 CONC  settlement called Killingworth. There they lived fro about eleven years and then moved back to Simsbury (1675). Jonas and his family had been active members of the established First Church of Killingworth.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL - The will of Hannah of Symsbury, dated Aug. 1, 1713. In the name of God Amen, I Hannah Westover, fo Symsbury, in the county of Hartford, and Colony of Connecticut, relict of Jonah Westover, of the town County and Colony afor
2 CONC sd, deed, being weak and sick in body, yet of sound mind and judgment and of perfect memory, do dispose of what I have: First of all and principally, I give and bequeath my soul to God that gave it, and my body to Christian burial
2 CONC  and touching my wordly goods, I give in manner following: First, I give and bequeath to my six daughters, 40 shillings to each daughter, and the reminder if any be, to be equally divided to my daughter Hannah Alverd and my daught
2 CONC er Mary Case, Jane Byington and Johanna. Second: to Jonah my stack of rye. Third: I give also to my daughter Johanna my biggest pig, and the smaller pig I give to my son Jonathan. August the first 1713. This being my will and gtes
2 CONC tament I set my hand this first day of August, 1713. --Hannah Westover. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Not my research.....Ed
2 CONT
