1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Parentage 
2 CONT No reliable sources have been found to show that John Bissell was the son of Richard Bissell and Ann Margaret (Unknown) Bissell (1570-aft.1593).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Birth and Baptism
2 CONT '''John Bissell''', purportedly born or baptized on 30 October 1591 in England.[''citation needed''] His gravestone indirectly says he was born after 3 October 1591, and it is based on his age 86 at his death on 3 October 1677. He
2 CONC  died testate at Windsor, Connetcticut on 3 October 1677 (g.s., at age 86). 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriage
2 CONT John was married at least twice, likely three times. His wives are unknown. He first married in 1625/30 in England.[1] They had three children before coming to America, Mary, Thomas, and John, all born before 1630. They had Sam(ue
2 CONC l) in Plymouth Massachusetts in 1635, and Nathaniel and Joyce were born in Windsor, Connecticut, in 1640 and 1641. (Given that they named their first child Mary, John’s wife’s name may have been Mary... but she was not Elizabeth T
2 CONC hompson.) The name of each wife remains unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children  
2 CONT The children of John Bissell, Sr. and his unnamed first wife, the order uncertain, are as follows:
2 CONT *  i. Mary Bissell, b. circa 1631 in England, d. testate Sept. 11, 1689 at Windsor, Conn.; m. Apr. 12, 1649 at Windsor, Sgt. Jacob Drake, s. of John Drake & Elizabeth Rogers, b. circa 1624 in England. He d. testate at Windsor, Con
2 CONC n. Aug. 6, 1689. They had no known children.
2 CONT *  ii. Lieut., also referred to as Cornet, John Bissell, Jr., b. circa 1633 in England, d. intestate before Oct. 15, 1688 at an undefined place in the Province of New York while in the King's service; m. June 17, 1658 at Windsor, 
2 CONC Israel Mason of Saybrook, Conn., dau. of Maj. John Mason and his unnamed first wife, b. circa 1637-38, prob. at Windsor. She d. after Mar. 15, 1693/4. Nine children of the family.
2 CONT *  iii. Quartermaster Thomas Bissell, b. circa 1635 in England, d. testate at Windsor July 31, 1689; m. Oct. 11, 1655 at Windsor, Abigail Moore, dau. of Dea. John and Abigail Moore, b. June 16, 1639, at Windsor. She d. at Windsor 
2 CONC purportedly July 31, 1728. Twelve children of the family, three dying in infancy.
2 CONT * iv. Samuel Bissell, b. circa 1637 in England, d. testate at Windsor Dec. 3, 1700; m. 1) June 11, 1658 at Windsor, Abigail Holcomb, dau. of Thomas and Elizabeth Holcomb, bapt. Jan. 6, 1638/9 at Windsor, Conn. She d. at Windsor Au
2 CONC g. 17, 1688. Nine children of the family. Samuel m. 2) after Aug. 1688, Mary Buell, dau. of William Buell and Mary Post, and widow of Simon Mills, latter who d. intestate at Simsbury, Conn. July 6, 1683. There were no children by 
2 CONC this marriage, Mary having ten children by her first husband. She d. testate at Windsor, Conn. June 24, 1718.
2 CONT * v. Joyce Bissell, b. circa 1639 in England, d. after Sept. 8, 1689 when named in her sister Mary's will on the latter date; m. Nov. 17, 1665 at Windsor, Samuel Pinney, s. of Humphrey Pinney and Mary Hull, b. Mar. 30, 1635 at Dor
2 CONC chester, Mass. He d. after Dec. 13, 1689, when he was appointed one of four administrators of his sister-in-law Mary (Bissell) Drake's estate. Three children of the family.
2 CONT * vi. Nathaniel Bissell, b. Sept. 24 (bapt. Sept. 27), 1640 at Windsor, Conn, d. testate at Windsor Mar. 12, 1713/4; m. 1) Mindwell Moore, dau. of Dea. John and Abigail Moore, and sister of his brother Thomas Bissell's wife Abigai
2 CONC l, b. July 10, 1643, at Windsor. She d. at Windsor Nov. 24, 1682. Nine children of the family. Nathaniel m. 2) July 4, 1683 at Windsor, Dorothy Fitch, who d. at Windsor June 28, 1691, by whom he had two recorded children. He m. 3)
2 CONC  on an unknown date after June 1691, Deliverance Haines, formerly the wife of 1) John Rockwell of Windsor and 2) John Warner of Middletown, Conn. She d. intestate June 12, 1718, in Middletown, Conn. There were no children by this 
2 CONC marriage. Deliverance had seven children by her first two husbands.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bissell's first wife (Unknown) died on 21 May 1641 in her 45th year at Windsor, Connecticut. Their children were aged 14, 13, 11, 6, 1, and a newborn.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT John was married a second time before 18 January 1660 when he was listed as paying for a man and his wife for a church pew. His second wife (Unknown) died on 29 March 1665.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Immigration
2 CONT Henry Reed Stiles' ''The History of Ancient Windsor, Connecticut'' says in his following words on page 540:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Tradition asserts that this John, with a brother Thomas, came from Somersetshire, England, to Plymouth, in 1628. The latter died at Plymouth, or returned to England."
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a footnote on page 540 says, "This disagrees with the tradition in the family, which states 1635 as the date of the arrival, but with the statement of Farmer, and with all the known facts."
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Bissell either died at Plymouth Colony or returned to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Life in Windsor
2 CONT John Bissell was a founder of Windsor, Connecticut.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bissell and John Drake brought their families from Boston to settle in Windsor in the autumn of 1639. The Bissells lived next door to the Drakes, and they were the first families to settle at “Sequestered Meadow” where the or
2 CONC iginal ferry and the original Bissell Tavern were.
2 CONT
2 CONT John probably joined the Dorchester congregation in settling Windsor in 1635. He started up a ferry service to get cattle to the other side. Bissell’s Ferry was arguably the first ferry service in the United States before there ev
2 CONC en was a United States. That ferry connected Windsor and what is now South Windsor, north of Hartford.
2 CONT
2 CONT For thirty years there was no settlement on the east side of the river, the reason no doubt being, that the passage of the Connecticut was laborious in summer and difficult, or impossible, in winter; that the meadows on that side 
2 CONC of the river being lower, were subject to floods and, too, there were the Podunk Indians to be considered, who occupied the land on the east side of the river.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was admitted to the Windsor Church on 3 May 1640.[6] He served as a juryman at Hartford 1640-1643, 1645 and 1647. He later served as a deputy to the general court and attended 46 sessions of the General and Particular Court b
2 CONC efore the union of the Connecticut and New Haven colonies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1648, the Bissell family was granted a monopoly of a ferry (for 150 years) across the river between Windsor and the hamlet of East Windsor in the Town of South Windsor. There is a tradition in regard to this grant that is inter
2 CONC esting, if not founded upon fact, as Stiles claims. This tradition is, that John Bissell was sent by the Colony to England, in 1636, to purchase and bring back a supply of cattle as the previous winter had been so severe that many
2 CONC  of their cattle had died. Mr. Bissell returned with seventeen cows and a bull, and as an equivalent for his services, he was granted the monopoly of the ferry across the Connecticut River.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was an enlisted trooper of Windsor in 1657-8 and "was confirmed by the General Court, Quarter Master of the County Troop of Hartford County in 1677"s (the first troop of horse in the Colony)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1659. John built a house on the east side, which he gave to his son Nathaniel. John moved there in 1662, giving his Windsor home to his son John. The new house was just south of the mouth of the Scantic River on the east side o
2 CONC f the Connecticut River. It was garrisoned as the town fort during King Philip's War in 1675.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1662, John gave his ferry with the homestead to his son John, and with Nathaniel removed to below the mouth of the Scantic, on the east side, being the first family there.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1675, the Bissells had neighbors, and during King Philip's War, their house was fortified and garrisoned. 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Death and Burial
2 CONT John Bissell died on 3 October 1677 in his 86th year at the home of his son Nathaniel.[5] He is buried in Palisado Cemetery at Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut.[6] The memorial in the Palisado Cemetary reads: “Heare lyeth the body o
2 CONC f John Bissell deceased Oct. the 3rd, 1677 in the 86th year of his age.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT === Probate
2 CONT Manwaring's abstract of John Bissell's estate, citing Hartford Probate District vol. III, p. 194:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Will''': John Bissell made his will, dated Sept. 25, 1673. 
2 CONT * I John Bissell of Windsor doe make this my last Will & Testament: 
2 CONT * I give to my daughter Mary, the wife of Jacob Drake 10; 
2 CONT * to my daughter Joyce, wife of Samuel Pinney, 30. 
2 CONT * I give to my son John 50. 
2 CONT * The remainder of my estate after my just debts and funeral charges are paid, with 20 shillings apeice to each of my grand children naturally descending from my foure sons and two daughters, I bequeath to my four sons John, Thoma
2 CONC s, Samuel, and Nathaniel.
2 CONT * The remaynder of my estate to be equally divided. 
2 CONT * I appoint my sons John and Thomas Bissell to be executors. 
2 CONT * I desire Deacon John Moore and Daniel Clark to be supervisors......JOHN X BISSELL LS.
2 CONT
2 CONT Witness: John Moore sen., Daniel Clarke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Court Record, Page 165-6, December 1677: Will approved.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Inventory''': John Bissell of Windsor. "£ 520-16-03. Taken 22 October 1677, by Daniel Clarke, Benjamin Newbery, Return Strong."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Research Notes
2 CONT Stiles' first version of his "History of Ancient Windsor" (1859) contains a poor outline of the structure of the Bissell family of Windsor, Connecticut. He gave John Bissell only one wife in this version. Savage, Hinman, Cutter, a
2 CONC nd others also misrepresent the initial Bissell family structure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stiles' more familiar second version of his "Ancient Windsor," in two volumes (1891 and 1893), does not correct the errors made forty years earlier in 1859. He continued to give the immigrant only one wife in Vol. II (1893 Genealo
2 CONC gies). However, in Vol. I (1891 History) he added an obscure reference apparently contained in the 1662 deed to son Nathaniel of his housing at present-day East Windsor that proves the immigrant had two wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bissell, the immigrant, had six known children, all by his first wife who d. of record at Windsor, Connecticut. on May 21, 1641. According to the Matthew Grant Records, Grant was aware of only one child born to the immigrant 
2 CONC at Windsor, and as only John's youngest son is recorded at Windsor prior to the death of his first wife, there were no children by his unnamed second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some writers give the first wife the name of Mary, and still others claim she was Mary Drake. But neither variation has any proof, and it is quite likely Mary Drake has been confused with the married name of John Bissell's daughte
2 CONC r Mary (Bissell) Drake. On this point, in 1969, the eminent genealogist Donald Lines Jacobus wrote:
2 CONT
2 CONT >May I try to set the record straight once again? [The immigrant] John1 Bissell [Sr.] did indeed die in 1677; there is no authority that I know of for calling his wife Mary Drake. A nameless wife, perhaps the mother of his childre
2 CONC n, died in 1641; he thereafter had another nameless wife, whose death, as wife of John "Sr.", was recorded in 1665...(NEHGS Register, 123:278-279.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bissell's second wife's death was on the record at Windsor on Mar. 29, 1665 ["the wife of John Biffell senor dyed"]. According to Stiles' account, when John Bissell was in excess of 70 years old in 1662, he gave to youngest s
2 CONC on Nathaniel his housing at present-day East Windsor, in his following words:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"In part of his portion of his marriage," a part of his land and house "at Scantuck," on the E. side of the Conn. River with provision for the remainder at his father's death... Nathaniel doubtless resided on the river bank, belo
2 CONC w the Scantic, from the time of his marriage. This home was garrisoned at the time of King Philip's War. John Bissell, Senr, evidently died here, having made provision for his second wife, stipulating that Nathaniel should give "h
2 CONC is now present mother-in-law [i.e., stepmother], if she is willing and choose to have the use the parlor for her abode," it should be granted. (History of Ancient Windsor, 1891, Vol. I, p. 152.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The aforementioned death of the immigrant's second wife has been consistently confused as the date of death of the wife of John Bissell, Jr. This caused Stiles to give John junior an unnamed second wife as the mother of his younge
2 CONC st children. John Bissell, junior's wife, Israel Mason, outlived her husband. This error is more fully outlined in the Israel (Mason) Bissell memorial page.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest in which the immigrant John Bissell appears at Windsor is in the record of youngest son Nathaniel's birth at Windsor, on Sept. 24, 1640. The earliest in which he appears in the Connecticut Public Records is as a jurym
2 CONC an in Sept. 1641. He was a juryman for numerous sessions of the Particular Court during the mid-1640s until he was elected a deputy to the Connecitcut Court from Windsor in Sept. 1648. He served as a deputy from Windsor almost con
2 CONC tinuously until 1656.
2 CONT
2 CONT While some claim John held the title of Capt., he was relieved of the requirement for military training by the Connecticut Court in April 1645, when he was about 53 years old. Unless one wishes to attach the title of Capt. to a pu
2 CONC blic ferryman, there is no record of any military rank for the immigrant in the Windsor or Connecticut records. Also, none of his sons reached the rank of Capt. during their lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Jan. 5, 1641/2, Windsor was provided the right to operate a ferry across the Conn. River. However, not until Jan. 1648/9 was John Bissell, Sr. granted exclusive right to operate the Windsor ferry for the ensuing seven years (Co
2 CONC nn. PR, Vol. I, p. 174-5). This right was extended annually beginning in May 1656 until eldest son John Bissell, Jr. was granted exclusive rights to operate the ferry in Mar. 1657/8 for the ensuing 10 years. This was followed in M
2 CONC ay 1677 by the immigrant's youngest son, Nathaniel, granted the right of operation for the ensuing 7 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Bissell's Origins'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Frank Thistlethwaite, in ''Dorset Pilgrims: The Story of West Country Pilgrims who went to New England in the 17th Century'' (1989), says that John Bissell, Sr. "is supposed to have come from Somerset" (page 201). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Somerset is a West Country county that borders county Dorset in England. The founders of Dorchester, Massachusetts (1630), went overland to found Windsor, Connecticut in 1635, and completed the settlement by 1641. John Bissell was
2 CONC  first found in Windsor records in 1639. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Bissell's Wives'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Who were John Bissell’s Wives? 
2 CONT
2 CONT The three wives in question are: Mary Drake, Elizabeth Thompson, and Abigail Filley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Not''' Abigail Filley, who was born on 21 August 1658 and married Samuel Bissell, son of John the Emigrant, on 26 August 1680. Samuel Bissell was born on 5 April 1659 and died on 27 January 1684. Abigail then married second, Sa
2 CONC muel Tudor on 30 October 1685. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Not''' Elizabeth Thompson: her name came to be part of the LDS database as follows. There is a reference in Stile’s Volume 1 about the layout of the town and it refers to a book printed in 1870 entitled “The Golden Wedding of J
2 CONC ohn Bissell and Elizabeth Thompson” by Major Samuel W. Bartlett. This was assumed to be John Bissell, the immigrant. It was not. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That publication refers to this “Golden Wedding” on Page 89, Family 80, of the Stiles genealogies, where it shows Captain John Bissell, gr-gr-gr-grandson of John Bissell the immigrant (son of Hezekiah, John, Jerimiah, John, John )
2 CONC  Married Elizabeth Mcknight Thompson on December 12, 1820. Someone saw that reference only, and without finding that book, made an assumption and printed the information in the LDS database without documentation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The LDS database is a collection of “opinions” and is only a guide to more serious references. Too many people take the printed word as gospel truth and perpetuate myths by claiming that they are true. Then someone hears a true fa
2 CONC ct, and it becomes a fact! But who printed the original supposition? Someone who was trying to claim a connection, or someone who has seen the name connected with some other John B. and made an assumption? 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Not''' Mary Drake. The unreliable LDS database names Mary Drake, a wife of John Bissell. In Windsor, the Bissells lived next door to the Drakes, and they were the first families to settle “Sequestered Meadow”, which was the loc
2 CONC ation of the original ferry and the original Bissell Tavern. John Drake and John Bissell brought their families from Boston to settle Windsor in autumn of 1639. Mary, the daughter of John Drake, married John Gaylord. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There are many women named Mary Drake, born in England between 1580 and 1600, who would have been the right age to marry John Bissell but they were all married to others and did not emigrate to America at that time. So, there is n
2 CONC o actual data to support the LDS claim that Mary Drake was her name. 
2 CONT
2 CONT An April 1969 Article titled "Dorothy, Unidentified Wife of Edward Kellogg."[5] also calls John Bissell's wife Mary Drake. Donald Lines Jacobus, in the following October issue, refuted this statement. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "May I try to set the record straight once again? John1 Bissell did indeed die in 1677; there is no authority that I know of for calling his wife Mary Drake. A nameless wife, perhaps mother of his children, died in 1641; he therea
2 CONC fter had another nameless wife whose death, as wife of John ;Sr.;, was recorded in 1665. ..."[6] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion: the name of John Bissell, Sr.'s wives are unknown. Please do not add Mary, Elizabeth, or Abigail to John's profile without reliable primary or secondary evidence. 
2 CONT
