1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Samuel was born in 1698. He is the son of John Pettibone and Mary Bissell. He passed away in 1787.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Pettibone grew up in Simsbury and married Mary Porter when he was twenty-three and she was eighteen. He and his wife settled in Simsbury, and their ﬁrst nine children were born there. He became a lawyer and was admitted to 
2 CONC the bar in 1729. In 1737 Samuel Pettibone was one of forty-nine men who bought one right to a share of the so-called "Western Lands" offered by the Colony of Connecticut in a public sale by the Colony of lands it owned. He thus be
2 CONC came an original proprietor of the town of Goshen, Connecticut [Hibbard, History of Goshen, Connecticut, 32]. Samuel Pettibone’s new property was part of a huge area the ownership of which had been hotly contested for almost twent
2 CONC y years. The argument started in May 1719 when the General Assembly of Connecticut gave permission to a group of men who planned to form a new town west of Farmington to be called Bantam. In the same legislation, the Assembly stat
2 CONC ed that all the large tract of land lying northwardly, eastward, and westward of the colony line "shall lie for the further disposal of this Assembly...to the end that the said tract of land may be improved for the good of the col
2 CONC ony and be regularly settled" [Crissey, History of Norfolk, Litchfield County, Connecticut: 1744-1900, 36].The trouble was that the Colony had granted the same lands to the towns of Hartford and Windsor more than thirty years earl
2 CONC ier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Closer to home, Samuel bought for £19 one-half the land on which stood the iron works in Norfolk, Connecticut, "with 1/8 part of the iron works and the cole-house thereon standing, with the utensils thereof, and all the privileges
2 CONC  and appurtenances thereunto belonging" [Crissey, 229]. The proprietors of Norfolk having encouraged by land grants the building of a grist mill and then a sawmill, appointed a committee in 1757 to investigate the feasibility of a
2 CONC n iron works in Norfolk. Their efforts resulted in an operating forge by about 1770, the one in which Samuel invested. Iron had first been manufactured in Salisbury, Connecticut, thirty years before, and the industry spread to oth
2 CONC er towns in western Connecticut, including Norfolk, Colebrook, and Litchfield. Iron ore was brought to the forges from Salisbury, often in leathern sacks on horseback. Bar iron became an accepted medium of exchange in the area, an
2 CONC d promissory notes were frequently made payable in iron rather than in money. In addition to his business activities, Samuel Pettibone was also active in the practice of law. He was the first King’s Attorney for the County of Litc
2 CONC hﬁeld, appointed in 1751. He was elected Representative to the General Assembly from Goshen for four consecutive years, from 1759-1762 [Hibbard, 343], and was occasionally commissioned as agent by other towns, as in 1762 when the 
2 CONC town meeting of Norfolk voted that Captain Samuel Pettibone of Goshen be agent for the Norfolk petition asking the assembly for liberty to hold a lottery to raise £100 for making and repairing public highways in Norfolk [Crissey, 
2 CONC 21]. Samuel married his second wife in 1766, when sixty-eight. The date of his third marriage is unknown, but his third wife survived him and he bequeathed to her his entire estate except for a small monetary bequest to his younge
2 CONC st daughter, Arm. At his death at the age of eighty-nine, his four surviving sons lived in Vermont, Massachusetts, and New York, and apparently only his daughter Anne, "widow of Asee Gillet," according to Samuel’s will, still kept
2 CONC  in touch with her father. Eleven years after his death 139 acres of his original grant in New Haven, Vermont, were still listed in his name and were sold to satisfy unpaid taxes of one cent an acre [Town Records of New Haven, Ver
2 CONC mont, 1:1].
