1 NAME Richard /Treat/
2 GIVN Richard
2 SURN Treat
2 _MARNM Treat
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 14 MAY 1694
2 ADDR
3 CITY Glastonbury
3 STAE Connecticut
1 DEAT
2 DATE 31 MAY 1759
2 ADDR
3 CITY Sheffield
3 STAE Massachusetts
1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rev. Richard Treat undertook efforts to convert the Central Connecticut indians in the 1730's to the Christian religion and English lifestyle, focusing on the Wangunk, Hockanum and and Farmington Indian communities
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1734 he was working as a missionary to the Wangunks. He was raised on his father's farm at Nayaug  in Glastonbury, His father, Thomas, was a skilled interpreter who served as a depurty to the General Court and as a lieutenant d
2 CONC uring Queen Anne's War. Thomas Treat's written will stipulated that his son, Richard, receive a college education and this request was carried out. After graduating from Yale College in 1719, Rev. Richard Treat preached to Wangunk
2 CONC  community members from Dec. 1734 until June 1735. He undertook this task with the encouragement of ministers in Central Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT He instructed a group of about 12-14 children in the English language and religion on a weekly basis. He abandoned his efforts after about four months, discouraged by slow progress, impending family obligations, and an apparent la
2 CONC ck of missionary society support.  However, later that summer he attended a Wangunk "Great dance" in an attempt to assess their population on behalf of Gov. Talcott and missionary society commissioners.  Rev. Treat wrote a stateme
2 CONC nt about his experiences with the Wangunk in 1737. This information is considered to be an excellent ethnographic source for historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT The society Rev. Treat was appealing to for advice and a source of funding was the Society for the Propogation of the Bible, the missionary arm of the Church of England.  The SPG in New England, directed by commissioners at Boston
2 CONC , promoted the conversion of Connecticut's Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the 1770's the Christian faith had become a powerful tool for social networkng among many Indians of Southern New England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wangunk Ethnohistory: A Case Study of a Connecticut River Indian Community
2 CONT
2 CONT https://scholarworks.wm.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5225&context=etd
2 CONT
2 CONT Link to Rev. Treat's work within this source material:\ Appendix D
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wangunk Ethnohistory: A Case Study of a Connecticut River Indian Community
2 CONT
2 CONT Society for the Propagation of the Bible:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.oxfordbibliographies.com/view/document/obo-9780199730414/obo-9780199730414-0067.xml
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ouramericanrevolution.org/index.cfm/page/view/m0162
2 CONT
