3 CITY Lyme
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 JAN 1820
2 ADDR
3 CITY Troy
3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 TITL Captain
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Charles Selden'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/44869488/charles-selden Find A Grave Memorial ID # 44869488]
2 CONT
2 CONT Member, New York State Assembly, Rensselaer County, 1803-04; Member, New York State Senate Eastern District, 1807-15. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://politicalgraveyard.com/families/37968.html Gale-Selden family of Galesburg, Illinois]
2 CONT
2 CONT REVOLUTIONARY WAR VETERAN:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Selden is documented and identified, on Rep. No. 118, House of Representatives, 22nd Congress 1st Session Jan.3,1832, as "Capt. and A.D.C." [Aid De Camp] / Alan Herbert Catlin.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Citation information:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Massachusetts Society of the  Cincinnati Profiles (Online database: NewEnglandAncestors.org, 2004.)
2 CONT
2 CONT CHARLES SELDEN: He was born at Lyme, Connecticut, November 23, 1755-, died in Troy, New York, January 1, 1820. Charles was a son of Col. Samuel and Elizabeth (Ely) Selden. He married at Colchester, Conn, in 1786 to Abigail Warren 
2 CONC Jones. She died before 1820. Charles attended Yale and received his AB in 1777. Commissioned in the Continental Army immediately after graduation.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following sketch for his service in the Revolutionary War appears in Massachusetts Soldiers and Sailors of the Revolutionary' War (13:977).
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Selden, Lieutenant, Col. Henry Jackson's Regt.; Continental Army pay accounts for service from July 1, 1777, to Dec. 31, 1779; also, Lieutenant, Col. Lee's Regt; return for clothing allowed agreeable to order of General Co
2 CONC urt of March 13, 1778; also, Lieut. Col. William S. Smith's (late Col. Lee's) Regt.; return of field, staff, and commissioned officers, dated Pawtuxet, Sept. 19, 1778; also, 2d Lieutenant, Capt Daniel Lyman's co., Col. Jackson's R
2 CONC egt commanded by Lieut Col. William S. Smith; pay rolls for Oct. and Nov., 1778; also, 1st Lieutenant, Capt. William North's (5th) co., Col, Jackson's Regt.; muster roll of field, staff, and commissioned officers for April, 1779, 
2 CONC dated Pawtuxet; appointed July 1, 1777; reported promoted March 5, 1779; also, Lieutenant, same Co. and Regt.; pay roll for July, 1779, sworn to at Camp Providence; also, same Regt.; return of field, staff, and commissioned office
2 CONC rs for clothing, dated Camp Castle Island, Sept, 23, 1779; also, Capt. North's co., Col. Jackson's Regt.; pay roll for Oct., 1779; also, 2d Lieutenant, same Regt; regimental return made up to Dec. 31, 1779, dated Camp at Providenc
2 CONC e; reported as belonging to Connecticut; also, Lieutenant, 4th Mass. Regt. commanded by Col. Henry Jackson; return of effectives, dated Feb, 21, 1783; reported at the lines; also, same Regt.; returns of effectives between March 23
2 CONC , 1783, and April 11, 1783, dated Cantonment Mass. Hutts; reported on furlough; also, same Regt.; return of effectives, dated Camp Mass. Line,. May 2, 1783; also, same Regt; return of officers of 1st Mass. brigade, showing dates o
2 CONC f commissions; commissioned March 1778,
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was retained in Colonel Henry Jackson's Continental regiment and served as Regimental Adjutant thereof 16 June 1783 until 20 June 1784 when he was discharged. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war he went into business in Lansingburg (near Troy), New York, with his college classmate, Samuel Cogswell, under the firm name of Cogswell & Selden. This venture failed in two years, but Lieutenant Selden later became 
2 CONC a prosperous and influential merchant nonetheless. He was appointed a Regent of the State University of New York in 1803 and served until 1817. Charles was elected a New York State Senator for the term 1808 through 1811. His will 
2 CONC bequeathed his estate in equal halves to his surving children, William Jones Selden and Harriet Selden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was an Original Member of the Massachusetts Society of the Cincinnati from 1783 until 1820. He was granted a Bounty Land Warrant 1 March 1800. His obituary appeared in The Columbian Centennial 12 January 1820.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Troy, New York by: George Baker Anderson, says that in 1801, as the merchants of Troy were in need of a bank for their own community, when the Legislature granted permission for the organization of such a bank, they inc
2 CONC orporated The Farmers' Bank, and Charles Selden was named in the charter as one of the first directors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Charles and Abigail:
2 CONT
2 CONT    i.    Eliza, b.1790.  She married Henry Vail
2 CONT    ii.   Charles Lord, b.1795
2 CONT    iii.  William Jones. b. 1798
2 CONT    iv.  Harriet, b. 1800. She married in 1820 to Rev. George Washington Gale, D.D. founder of Galesburg and   Knox College in 1835.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
2 CONT
2 CONT Conn. VRs - Barbour Collection: Lyme, Connecticut Births, Marriages, Deaths 1667-1852. (Connecticut State Library, 1919), P. 176
2 CONT
2 CONT Franklin Bowditch Dexter, "Biographical Sketches of the Graduates of Yale College, with Annals of the College History" - Vol. III: May 1763 - July, 1778. (New York. Henry Holt and Company; 1903) p. 706.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rensselaer County, New York, Surrogate's Records, Will Book 6:322
2 CONT
2 CONT Virgil D. White, "Genealogical Abstracts of Revolutionary War Pension Files", (Waynesboro, TN., National Historical Publishing Co., 1992) 3:3066 (BLW # 1911-200).
