2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Introduction
2 CONT
2 CONT After reading the entries in Savage's Genealogical Dictionary for the husbands of Elizabeth, I started to look for supporting evidence and discovered that two FASG's had written an article explaining all this and citing the approp
2 CONC riate deeds and probate records.[1] Following is a synopsis.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cast of Characters
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth _______, the wife and mother.
2 CONT Husband (1) Thomas Barlow, b. c. 1630; died in Boston 23 Oct 1661, cooper : Barlow Children b. in Boston:
2 CONT Elizabeth Barlow b. 13 Nov 1657; d. 17 April 1659
2 CONT Sarah Barlow b. 18 July 1659, d. unm. near April 1678
2 CONT Husband (2) John Coombs b. c. 1632; m. Elizabeth Barlow 24 Feb 1661/2; [3]died May 1668 Boston, cooper :Coombs Children b. in Boston:
2 CONT Elizabeth Coombs, b. 30 Nov 1662; d. Plympton after 14 Oct 1723; m. 12 Jan 1687/8 Eleazer Cushman, her cousin.
2 CONT John Coombs, b. 20 July 1664; d. Boston Jan-Feb 1709/10; m. Elizabeth (Ballentine) (Greenland) Yellings, widow
2 CONT Mary Coombs b. 28 Nov 1666; d. 2 July 1728 Plympton; m. 18 Dec 1689 Benjamin Eaton
2 CONT Husband (3) John Warren, died Boston 1677: Warren Child
2 CONT Nathaniel Warren b. 27 May 1670; living 31 Aug 1691, 21 years of age. He deeded his share or right in the estate of his half sister Sarah Barlow to his half brother John Coombs[4]
2 CONT The Property
2 CONT
2 CONT This begins with a piece of land in Boston and it's various "owners." For our purposes, starting with Bartholomew Barlow, who left the property to his son Thomas Barlow in 1657. Thomas died in 1661. Administration of his estate wa
2 CONC s given to his widow, Elizabeth _____ "in behalf of herself and the childe." Elizabeth and Thomas had two children: daughter Elizabeth died young, so the child mentioned was Sarah Barlow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth, now Barlow, remarried to John Coombs, 24 Feb 1661/2 in Boston. Because Sarah Barlow was still a minor, use of her estate was granted to her mother and because John Coombs was Elizabeth's husband he had use of the proper
2 CONC ty, which evidence shows he did not improve. When Coombs died in 1668, his small estate was left to the widow and the Coombs children, while the remainder of the Barlow estate was left to Sarah Barlow until she came of age, at whi
2 CONC ch point her mother would have her thirds. Elizabeth and John Coombs had three children who all lived to maturity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth, now Coombs, remarried a third time to John Warren (also previously married). They had a son Nathaniel Warren. Elizabeth Warren died at Boston Jan 1671/2. She left no will. The inventory of her personal estate was valued
2 CONC  at £33/14/6. Her husband John Warren presented a memorandum of goods that she wished bestowed on her children Sarah Barlow, Elizabeth, Mary and John Coombs, and Nathaniel Warren. Sarah Barlow was still underage (14) so the origin
2 CONC al Barlow property was allowed to be improved by John Warren until Sarah's majority. Warren died and for a short time his third wife had the use and improvement of the property.
2 CONT
2 CONT After John Warren's death, in 1677, Daniel Turrell Sr. and John Coney, principals, and John Williams and Joseph Royal, the uncle, sureties, were named guardian of Sarah Barlow during her minority. In 1680, the same were appointed 
2 CONC guardians of the Coombs children. Nathaniel remained with his stepmother, who remarried, but at age 15 he chose his uncle Joseph Royal to be his guardian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth _______'s children by all three marriages called Joseph Royal uncle at some point in the records, which has led to the possibility that she was originally Elizabeth Royal. More later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Barlow died in 1678. Her estate was granted by the court to her four half siblings. Elizabeth Coombs, spinster of Plymouth, sold her brother John Coombs her share in 1687, recorded in 1717. Mary Coombs, residing in Charlesto
2 CONC wn, (probably living with her uncle), sold her share to Joseph Royal in 1689. John Coombs, evidence shows, had purchased Nathaniel Warren's share, which he subsequently sold to Joseph Royal also. John retained his own share, but e
2 CONC ventually it became the property of Joseph Royal's son-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT  ?Royall?
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth (___?___) (Barlow) (Coombs) Warren, had children by three husbands. Children by all three husbands called Joseph Royal an uncle. This would indicate that Joseph was her brother or brother-in-law (the husband of Elizabeth
2 CONC 's sister). Joseph was married to a woman named Mary, whose surname is also unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Ryall was the son of William Royall and Phebe Green. William and Phebe Green had no child named Elizabeth.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidence indicates that Elizabeth was the sister of Mary (_______), the wife of Joseph Royall. Their surname is unknown and their parentage is unknown. Elizabeth was probably born between 1634 and 1639, based on the birth of her f
2 CONC irst child in 1657. There is a possibility she was born in Massachusetts, but could have been born in England also.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ Mrs John E Barclay and Rachel E Barclay, "Barlow, Coombs and Warren of Boston," TAG, 46: 3 (July 1970) , p 129 ff. link AmericanAncestors
2 CONT ↑ Anne B
2 CONT ↑ History of the Town of Hingham, Massachusetts (The Town of Hingham, Massachusetts, 1893) Vol II, p.138
2 CONT ↑ cites Suffolk Co. Deeds 31:260, 32:90]
2 CONT ↑ Anderson, Robert Charles. The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England 1620-1633, Volumes I-III. (Boston: New England Historic Genealogical Society. 1995).William Ryall pp 1611, 1612 link at American Ancestors
2 CONT Roser, Susan E. Mayflower Births and Deaths: From the Files of George Ernest Bowman at the Massachusetts Society of Mayflower Descendants. Volumes 1 & 2. Baltimore, MD: Genealogical Publishing Company, Inc., 1992. vol 2 p. 200. "T
2 CONC homas Barlow & Elizabeth ______" does not support Ryall/Royall
2 CONT Clemens, William Montgomery, 1860-1931. American Marriage Records Before 1699. Pompton Lakes, N.J.: The Biblio company, 1926. p. 63 "Combes, John and Elizabeth Barlow (widow of Thomas, widow of Thomas 24 Feb 1661 Boston, Mass." p.
2 CONC  30 Barlow to Combes
2 CONT U.S., New England Marriages Prior to 1700 @ Ancestry = Torrey’s New England Marriages Prior to 1700. (Online database. AmericanAncestors.org. New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2008.) Originally published as: New England M
2 CONC arriages Prior to 1700. (Boston, Mass.: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2015.)
2 CONT BARLOW, Thomas (-1661) & Elizabeth ?ROYALL/[BENSON], m/2 John COOMBES 1662, m/3 John WARREN; by 1657; Boston {Boston VR 30; Benson (1920) 15; Royall 10-11; Fallass 178; Sv. 1:117, 439; Reg. 11:39}
2 CONT COOMES, John (-1668) & Elizabeth (ROYALL?) BARLOW, or BENSON, w Thomas, m/3 John WARREN 1669±; 24 Feb 1661, at Boston, 1662?; Hingham {Coombs; Sv. 1:117, 439, 453; Fallass; Bos. Trans. 24 Jun 1918 ,886}
2 CONT Note: Regarding Torrey references to Benson: John Benson mentioned a daughter Combs (not deceased) in his will dated 1678. Elizabeth of this profile was deceased by 1678, so is clearly not his daughter.
2 CONT WARREN, John (John HAYWARD m 1684+) & 2/wf Elizabeth (?ROYALL) (BARLOW) [COOMBS] (-1672?), w Thomas, w John; by 1674; Boston {Royall 10-11; Sv. 1:117, 439; Fallass 178; Reg. 64:355; GDMNH 721}
