2 CONT Sources are as follows:
2 CONT "Hopkins of VA and their related families" published in 1931.
2 CONT Mary Hope West in the DAR and published in VA Magazine of History and
2 CONT Biography 1945. 1994-1997 Dr. Rogers Chambers and Robert T. Muse in
2 CONT "A Southern Legacy;Descendants of John Muse l @ ll. Web page by Mitchell
2 CONT K.Muse http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~mkm2295/HOMEPAGE.htm
2 CONT and Ancestry.com by Mitchell K. Muse.
1 NOTE John Mewes OF VIRGINIA
2 CONT
2 CONT Who was John Muse/Mewes? This question has been pursued by many but only some sought the answers and then made this material public knowledge.
2 CONT PUBLISHED AND UNPUBLISHED RESOURCES
2 CONT In the early 1930's Walter Lee Hopkins sought the answers on his family surname "Hopkins " and their related families and because the Muse surname was related he sought out those answers as well. In 1931 he published his facts and
2 CONC  findings on Muse's of Virginia and their descendants along with his descendants in book called " Hopkins of Virginia and Related Families". Published in 1931. This is one of the first recorded and published references I have foun
2 CONC d and it has with stood the test of time.
2 CONT In the 1940s Mary Hope West and Juliet Fauntleroy pursued this question and then compiled an unpublished manuscript that now resides in the archives of the Daughters of the American Revolution in Washington DC. In 1945 they publis
2 CONC hed a summation of their findings the "Virginia Magazine of History and Biography". In three different issues from April 1945 to October 1945 they published a descending tree of the ancestors of John Muse Sr. Where "Hopkins of Vir
2 CONC ginia and Related Families" only dealt with a certain line of descendants of John Sr. West and Fauntleroy expanded on this to include all they could find. The works of Walter Hopkins and West and Fauntleroy have with stood the scr
2 CONC utiny of time and the information they have provided has been paramount to our research.
2 CONT In 1994 and 1997 Dr. Roger David Chambers in collaboration with Lou Andrews Murphy and R obert T. Muse researched and compiled information on John Sr. and his descendants in an unpublished manuscript called "A Southern Legacy: Des
2 CONC cendants of John Muse of Virginia I & II" In these manuscript they took the works of the first two and conducted more research on their findings and then researched later generations and added them as well. How ever this research 
2 CONC focused mainly on North Carolina, Tennessee and their migration west. Like the other two references they provided research they collected from others or conducted them self to include references and finding of facts. The contents 
2 CONC of this research however only touched on the Muse's of Kentucky that lived in around the Pulaski County Kentucky in the early 1800's and 1900's, it never went into any great depth or detail on the subject. All of these works have 
2 CONC stood the test of time and are used by many researchers like us in trying to answer who was John Muse Sr. and where do we fit in? I am not going to try and express word for word what the above references have said, but will try to
2 CONC  sum up their findings and facts where it is most important or necessary. Wehave listed these same resources and others under Richard Sr. as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT MUSE'S OF KENTUCKY
2 CONT Who where the Muse's of the Pulaski County area of Kentucky? What was their relationshi p to John Muse Sr.? Where did the Muse surname come from? The above references state that th e Mewes surname originated from early England or 
2 CONC France and once these descendants settled i n the Colonies the name slowly transformed to the spelling most commonly used today, Muse. D uring our research we also found in the early 1800's to 1900's a family of Meaux's livingi n 
2 CONC Mercer County Kentucky and it was apparent based on the census record studies we did of Ken tucky and Missouri that some these Meaux's transformed the spelling of their surname to Muse . Like most families they originated from Vir
2 CONC ginia in the late 1700's. We found no evidenc e to suggest that these Meaux's (French pronunciation "Me O") have any connections with Joh n Sr. and his descendants in Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT COUNTRY OF ORIGIN OF John SR.
2 CONT Many attempts have been made to connect John Sr. to the shores of England but today thi s has not been successful. The closest that anyone has gotten was research done by a genealo gical research firm that identified a John Mewes 
2 CONC who[Lorene Rambo.ged]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes from Greg Howearth:
2 CONT
2 CONT Rappahannock Co VA Deeds 1668-72, pp. 15, 181 & 232, in Essex Co;
2 CONT and Records of Old Rappahannock Co VA, Vol 4, p. 54 & 92; per West &
2 CONT Fauntleroy:
2 CONT "On 18 Sep 1668 Thomas Green and Richard Wharton of Rappahannock
2 CONT Co., each deeded to John Mewes 50 acres of land. On 3 Jun 1679, John
2 CONT Mews conveys to Ignatius White for the term of 99 yeas, a tract of
2 CONT land adjoining on the land of Roger Cloathwork, and containing 100
2 CONT acres. July 1683, George Jones of Rappahannock Co., Gent. conveys to
2 CONT John Mewse, for 6000 lbs tobacco 108 acres of land."
2 CONT
2 CONT Northern Neck Grant Book No 3, p. 2; per West and Fauntleroy:
2 CONT "31 Mar 1692 John Muse made entry of 360 acres part in
2 CONT Rappahannock Co now Richmond Co and part in Westmoreland Co upon a
2 CONT survey dated 15 Oct 1691, on which survey there is an endorsement
2 CONT dated 16 Oct 1699 signed by Nathaniel Pope, then Clerk _____ alledging
2 CONT that he (Muse) hath been in possession of the land since the date of
2 CONT the survey and said Muse having assigned over his right in writing to
2 CONT his son John Muse ______ beginning at a certain stumped gum in the
2 CONT main swamp of Rappahannock Creek _____ touching the land of Edwin
2 CONT Thacker and John Bowen, decd. This patent John Mewes assigned to his
2 CONT son, John Mewes, Jr., on 7 Jun 1703."
2 CONT
2 CONT See Virginia Magazine of History and Bigraphy; Vol 53, No 2, Apr
2 CONT 1945.
2 CONT
2 CONT * * * * * * * * * *
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes from HOPKINS OF VIRGINIA:
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time this book was written, there was no evidence of the
2 CONT date of arrival of this family in Virginia, nor of their point of
2 CONT origin in England. "In the Isle of Wight, England, the family is
2 CONT found as far back as the 15th century. In Kingston Church, built in
2 CONT the 13th century, is a small brass inscribed: 'Mr. Richard Meweys
2 CONT whych deceassyd the iii day of March in the yere of our Lord God
2 CONT MCCCCC and XXXV.' He is represented as wearing a long gown liberally
2 CONT trimmed with fur. A small plate on one side of the figure is engraved
2 CONT with four sons similarly clad, and on the other side is a shield of
2 CONT the Mewes arms--Paly of 6 on a chief 3 crosses pattee." The author
2 CONT was certain that the name is originated in France. "On the other
2 CONT hand, Thomas Muse, Jr., of Westmoreland Co., Virginia, who wrote the
2 CONT will of Elizabeth Muse, widow, in 1734, spells the word daughter as if
2 CONT pronounced 'dafter,' which, it is said, is a north of England
2 CONT colloquialism."
2 CONT
2 CONT Earliest records of the Muse family in Virginia: On Sept. 1, 1666 (D.
2 CONT & W. B. I, p. 316, West'd Co.) Robert Muse was a witness to a deed
2 CONT from Thomas Wilsford to his son Andrew Wilsford, and on July 4, 1683,
2 CONT George Jones deeded George Mewes 680 and John Muse 180 acres. (Records
2 CONT of Old Rappa Co. Vol. 1680-1688). In 1707 Michael Mewes is named in
2 CONT the will of John Elliott of Westmoreland Co. On Mar. 26 (D. & W. B.
2 CONT 5, p. 415, West'd Co.), Nicholas Mewes of West'd Co., deeds Brooks
2 CONT Allington (or Abbington) of same Co., 50 acres of land, which he,
2 CONT Nicholas Mewes purchased from Brooks Allington on Feb. 1, 1708. On
2 CONT Dec. 7, 1720 (D. & W. B. 7, p. 538, Richmond Co., Va.), Catherine Mews
2 CONT gives bond as Admx. of the estate of Ann Lewis, dec'd.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 7, 1703, John Muse, Sr., assigned his son, John Muse, Jr., a
2 CONT patent to 360 acres of land lying partly in Richmond and Westmoreland
2 CONT Counties, upon which he, John Muse, Sr., entered and had surveyed Mar.
2 CONT 31, 1692. (Northern Neck Grant Book No. 3, p. 2).
2 CONT
2 CONT On Oct. 30, 1707, Thomas Muse, of West'd Co., and John Muse, Jr., of
2 CONT Richmond Co., Va., were granted 265 acres in West'd Co. On Nov. 10,
2 CONT 1711, Nicholas Muse of West'd Co., received a grant of land of 179
2 CONT acres in West'd Co. on Holling Branch on Great Rappahannock Creek,
2 CONT between the counties of Richmond and West'd. (Northern Neck Grant
2 CONT Book No. 4, p. 60).
2 CONT
2 CONT "Later records prove conclusively that John Muse, Jr., and Thomas
2 CONT Muse, Sr., were brothers, and sons of John Muse, Sr., who was born in
2 CONT 1633 and died in 1723 at the age of 90 years. On the 7th of
2 CONT September, 1715, Edward Barrow, Nicho A. Smith, John Tayloe and James
2 CONT Ingo, were ordered to take the depositions of John Mews and William
2 CONT Carter, which they did, and on Oct. 5, 1715, presented them in Court.
2 CONT In the deposition of John Muse, Sr., he stated that he was then
2 CONT Eight-two years of age. (Miscellaneous Records and Depositions, p.
2 CONT 93, Richmond Co., Va.).
2 CONT
2 CONT "Robert, George and Michael Muse may have been brothers of John Muse,
2 CONT Sr., but from the records it appears that the latter was the only one
2 CONT who left male issue."
2 CONT
2 CONT WILL OF John MUSE, SR., dated April 5, 1723, prob. Oct 8, 1723, as
2 CONT follows, omitting the preamble:
2 CONT "My last will and testament is that all debts and funeral
2 CONT expenses be fully paid and discharged. My will is to give to Thomas
2 CONT Muse my son one shilling my daughter Jane Pritchett one shilling my
2 CONT daughter Ann Willson one shilling my daughter Mary Quisenberry one
2 CONT shilling and all the rest I give to my daughter-in-law Ann Muse, as
2 CONT Witness my hand this 5 day of April 1723--John Muse mar. (Seal) Robert
2 CONT Sanford, George Dunbar, Westmoreland. ss I.
2 CONT At a court held for the said County the 25th day of September
2 CONT 1723--The last will and testament of John Muse deceased was presented
2 CONT into Court by Ann Muse his daughter-in-law who made oath thereto and
2 CONT being proved by the oath of Robert Sanford one of the witnesses
2 CONT thereto is admitted to record. And for that no Exr. is ordained in
2 CONT the sid will upon mocon of the said Ann (as greatest creditor) and her
2 CONT performing what is usual in such case certificate is granted her for
2 CONT obtaining lettere of admicon on the said decedts. estaet with the will
2 CONT annexed in due form--
2 CONT
2 CONT INVENTORY OF ESTATE, John Muse, 26 Nov 1723, recorded 4 Dec 1723,
2 CONT presented by Ann Muse, Westmoreland Co., VA, Records & Inventories,
2 CONT 1723-1746. The estate was appraised by Humphey Pope, Thomas King, and
2 CONT Matthew Boan.
2 CONT
2 CONT (From Greg Howearth relative to the will of John Muse: WILL: VA
2 CONT Westmoreland Co. Will Book, GHL 975.524W761 Mesa? "Evidently moved
2 CONT to Westmoreland Co VA after the death of second wife and lived with
2 CONT John, Jr. and Ann, which is probably the reason Ann got the whole of
2 CONT his estate.")
2 CONT
2 CONT From Virginia Colonial Abstracts, Volume 1, Richmond County Records
2 CONT 1692-1704:
2 CONT p. 280: p. 20-A Misc. Records: John Muse is listed as owed $62 in
2 CONT probate papers of Wm. Brockenbrough, Dec. On p. 303 John Muse is
2 CONT listed as paid the $62.
2 CONT p. 302: p. 73-A Misc. Records: Both John Muse and John Muse, Jr.,
2 CONT are listed as signers of a report of land survey.
2 CONT
2 CONT From letter from Bob Muse dated 10 Dec. 1997:
2 CONT John Muse Sr.'s will was written 5 April 1723, probated 25
2 CONT September 1723, and recorded 8 October 1723. Thusly, he probably
2 CONT died September of 1723.
2 CONT
2 CONT "A Southern Legacy" lists a number of documents naming John Muse, RDC
2 CONT 11.
2 CONT
2 CONT That John Muse was born in 1633 is documented by: 5 Oct 1715,
2 CONT "Deposition of John Mews & William Lewis" referencing "John Mewes Aged
2 CONT Eighty two years. (From "Southern Legacy")
2 CONT Christy Flynn lists date of death as 8 Oct 1723. Jeanne Muse and Rev.
2 CONT McLeod agree on 5 April 1723. See note on will. Bob Muse surmises
2 CONT that based on the date of will, he probably died in September of 1723.
