2 CONT ===Property and Positions===
2 CONT The original patent was in the name of grandfather, Peter Ransone (as "Peter Ranson" on 2 September 1652, and as "Mr. Peter Ranson" on 9 June 1653). The land lay on the east side of the North River in Mobjack Bay, beginning by the
2 CONC  creek and "dividing this from land of Richard Dudley, Sly, to Isle of Wight Cr."[6] This property consisted of 1100 acres which was left to his three sons: the 400 acres "having on it Madam Ransone's house" Capt James Ransone (fa
2 CONC ther), another 350 acres which were William Ransone's (uncle) and a portion for George Ransone (uncle).
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter's portion was "300 acres bounding on Capt. Dudley's great pine." On 25 Feb 1714, Peter Ransone, and Dorothy, his wife, of Kingston Parish, Gloucester County, conveyed for 4500 lbs of tobacco, "to Thomas Waring and Elizabeth,
2 CONC  his wife, all their right of dower in the tract of 300 acres in St Anne's Parish, Essex County" (formerly belonged to Thomas Gouldman) (Page 179).
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, the property was a combined tract consisting of the original 1100 acres, an additional 400 acres called "Bald Eagle Neck, and 30 acres patented by Capt. James Ransone. This was surveyed on 8 September 1745 by John French fo
2 CONC r George Ransone. The plat showed four divisions, but the wording is unclear as to which division was Peter's portion. "3) George Ransone's part, Peter Ransone's house; 4) 300 acres bounding on Capt. Dudley's great pine." Apparent
2 CONC ly he received the fourth portion, because on 25 Feb 1714, Peter Ransone, and Dorothy, his wife, of Kingston Parish, Gloucester County, conveyed for 4500 lbs of tobacco, "to Thomas Waring and Elizabeth, his wife, all their right o
2 CONC f dower in the tract of 300 acres in St Anne's Parish, Essex County"
2 CONT
