1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT James Ransom I was a colonial Virginia resident whose life spanned the administrative centers of the Tidewater region. Born in 1704 in Kingston Parish, Gloucester County, to Peter Ransom and Dorothy North, he established his life 
2 CONC in the colony during the early 18th century. He married Giselle (or Grissell) Gwathmey, and together they settled in the Lawnes Creek area of Surry County. His will, dated May 19, 1740, and proved on October 15, 1740, serves as th
2 CONC e primary documentary evidence for his immediate family and remains the foundational record for researchers of the Ransome lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Peter Ransom (c. 1670–after 1759)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Dorothy North
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Birth: 1704, Kingston Parish, Gloucester County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 1740, Lawnes Creek, Surry County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and Issue===
2 CONT Spouse: Giselle (Grissell) Gwathmey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children (in order as documented in the 1740 Will):
2 CONT 1. James Ransom II (c. 1726–1798): Named as a son and executor in his father's will; he later resided in North Carolina.
2 CONT 2. Gwathney (Gwathmey) Ransom (c. 1728–1790): Named as a son in his father's will.
2 CONT 3. Mary Ransom (c. 1730–unknown): Named as a daughter in his father's will.
2 CONT 4. Catherine Ransom (c. 1732–unknown): Named as a daughter in his father's will.
2 CONT 5. Elizabeth Ransom (c. 1735–unknown): Named as a daughter in his father's will.
2 CONT (Note: While some family records include Charles Ransome (c. 1725–1794) as a son of James Ransom I, he is not explicitly named in the 1740 Surry County will. Consequently, professional genealogical research often treats Charles as
2 CONC  a separate branch or unlinked descendant within the broader Ransome network.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Documented Facts & Sources===
2 CONT Probate Evidence: The primary source for the family structure is the Will of James Ransom, made May 19, 1740, and recorded October 15, 1740, in Surry County, Virginia (Surry Co., VA Wills 1730–1800, by Lyndon H. Hart).
2 CONT
2 CONT Geographic Context: His life reflects the movement of colonial families from the older parishes of Gloucester County toward the expanding settlement areas along the James River in Surry County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage Verification: Colonial Virginia records verify his descent from the early emigrant Peter Ransom, establishing his place within the established Tidewater gentry.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Updated: 06/30/2026
2 CONT
2 CONT —-
2 CONT James Ransom (1), son of Peter Ransom, was a member of the Virginia House of Burgesses, 1690-93-97.
2 CONT He was a vestryman in Kingston Parrish, Episcopal Church. He belonged to the Colonial Militia with Mathew Page and Peter Beverly.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Had 7 children with Giselle.
