1 TITL Brigadier General
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_M._Goggin
2 CONT
2 CONT James Monroe Goggin (October 23, 1820 – October 10, 1889) was an American businessman and soldier. He served as an officer in the Confederate States Army during the American Civil War, and very briefly as a general in 1864.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life and Texas
2 CONT
2 CONT Goggin was born in 1820 in Bedford County, Virginia, and was married to Elizabeth Nelson Page. Goggin entered the United States Military Academy in West Point on July 1, 1838, but resigned prior to graduating sometime before 1842.
2 CONC  After leaving West Point, Goggin relocated to Texas, where he joined the Army of Texas as a first lieutenant. Goggin also began purchasing real estate in Waller County while in Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1848, Goggin was special agent for the United States Postal Service, establishing offices and postal routes in northern California and southern parts of Oregon. Goggin then moved to Memphis, Tennessee, where he began working as
2 CONC  a cotton broker. He was engaged in this business up until the beginning of the American Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Civil War service
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1861, Goggin chose to follow the Confederate cause. After moving to Virginia, he entered the Confederate States Army as a major on July 1, and was assigned to lead a battalion in the 32nd Virginia Infantry regiment. Goggin and 
2 CONC the 32nd served under Maj. Gen. John B. Magruder in 1861 and into 1862 on the Virginia Peninsula. Goggin joined the First Corps, Army of Northern Virginia that April, serving on the staff of Maj. Gen. Lafayette McLaws as his assis
2 CONC tant adjutant general. He served in this capacity during all of the First Corps' engagements, in both the Army of Northern Virginia and the Army of Tennessee, until the spring of 1864. During the Battle of Fort Sanders in November
2 CONC  1863, Goggin carried a message from McLaws to Lt. Gen. James Longstreet, informing him that the attack was beginning to fail. When McLaws was replaced with Maj. Gen. Joseph B. Kershaw that May, Goggin served as his assistant adju
2 CONC tant general as well. His service in this post was highly praised by Goggin's superiors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Goggin's only noteworthy Civil War combat occurred in the Valley Campaigns of 1864. During the Battle of Cedar Creek on October 13, Goggin was given temporarily command of Brig. Gen. James Conner's Brigade. Conner had lost his lef
2 CONC t leg in combat earlier that day, and Kershaw assigned Goggin to lead his brigade in the battle at Cedar Creek. On December 4 he was appointed a "special" brigadier general, but this was canceled soon afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT No longer a brigadier, Goggin returned to the staff of Kershaw in December 1864, once again as assistant adjutant general. Following the Battle of Sayler's Creek during the 1865 Appomattox Campaign, Goggin, Kershaw, and the rest o
2 CONC f the staff were captured on April 6, and they were paroled from that location.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Postbellum
2 CONT
2 CONT After the American Civil War ended in 1865, Goggin returned to Texas, initially in the state's Waller County, and then to Austin. He died at the age of 68 in Austin, and was buried in the city's Oakwood Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT ========================
2 CONT
