1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sidney Rigdon Burton was born January 8, 1838, in Clay County, Missouri.  He was the son of John Newton Burton and Susan McCord, daughter of James McCord and Elizabeth Barnet.  The Burton's were of English stock wh
2 CONC o had originally settled in Virginia and became wealthy land and slave owners.  The loss of his first wife, Sally Allred, led John Newton to dispose of his Virginia property with the exception of one slave, Black John, who refused
2 CONC  to leave his master.  Leaving his baby daughter, Leona with her grandparents, he traveled over the South, finally going to Kentucky where he met and married Susan McCord.  The couple then went to Missouri and settled in Clay Coun
2 CONC ty. Here two boys, William Parley and Sidney Rigdon Burton were born to them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They acquired a plantation and John Newton Burton became superintendent of schools.  He died at the age of forty from pneumonia contracted through exposure while riding horseback between the settlements in the capacity of superint
2 CONC endent.  His son, Sidney R., was left without a father at the age of fifteen months.  Susan, her boy and Black John were among those taken to Nauvoo under the leadership of Brigham Young, President of the Quorum of Twelve Apostles
2 CONC , at the time of the imprisonment of Joseph Smith in Missouri.  
2 CONT
2 CONT While there, she became a plural wife of Joseph Lee Robinson in the Nauvoo Temple, January 11, 1846, after which they immediately made plans to go west with the Saints.  The couple made a trip back to Missouri to dispose of Susan’
2 CONC s property in order to procure oxen, cattle and other necessary supplies to aid in their journey west.  As the word spread that the Mormons were leaving Nauvoo, Gentiles began flocking in, lured by the prospect of obtaining proper
2 CONC ty at extremely low prices.  The sight of the preparations being made to leave by the Saints seemed to allay somewhat the severity of the mob persecution.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The main body of the Saints had already gone and on the tenth day of June 1846, Sue and her family crossed the Mississippi River on their first lap of their journey west.  Their party consisted of Susan, her husband, Joseph Lee, h
2 CONC er two boys, and the girl Leona and the faithful Black John who felt that it was his duty to assist her in raising her two boys.  The grandparents of Leona Burton strongly objected to her leaving on such a journey, having already 
2 CONC decided on a marriage partner for her.  In order to bring her to the party, Black John swam the Missouri River at night.  She married William Allred at the age of sixteen and later settled in Beaver, Utah. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As they traveled through Iowa, one had the measles and a little girl and Black John became crippled from some disease.  However, the roads were good, the scenery beautiful and the cripples soon became well.  They reached Mount Pis
2 CONC gah where they stopped for a time.  The water here was impure and some of the party became ill with chills and fever.  They started on again August 12, and reached Council Bluffs in September. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They went on to a place called Cutler Park near this river.  Here they could be close to a settlement, build homes and get supplies, wood and good water.  They first lived in tents but soon hauled logs and built a two room cabin. 
2 CONC  A log house was also built for a woman and her family whose husband was with the Mormon Battalion. The homes were located on the west side of the river at a point between Council Bluffs and Council Point.  They built a council ho
2 CONC use, a school, hired a teacher, and soon had a school going.  
2 CONT
2 CONT At these winter quarter, November 16, 1846, a baby girl was born to Susan McCord Burton Robinson.  She was named Susan Aseneth.  She passed away to the great sorrow of her parents, on March 4, 1847.  After leaving their winter qua
2 CONC rters, they traveled westward experiencing great difficulty in crossing rivers and streams one of which was the Platt River.  Black John had gone ahead of the party and had corn raised when they arrived in Utah.  That, and the sup
2 CONC plies they had with them, had to provide them until further crops could be raised.  
2 CONT
2 CONT They settled in Dry Canyon, now known as Farmington.  On October 5, 1848 another daughter was born to them and was given the name of Mary Jane Robinson.  This winter, food became so scarce that after taking stock of all the availa
2 CONC ble food, they decided it would be necessary to ration their food and allow each person one-half pound food per day. It was very hard at first but they gradually became accustomed to it. In the spring they planted their crops agai
2 CONC n and after the crops had sprouted, occurred the celebrated miracle when the seagulls came in answer to the prayers of the Saints and consumed the crickets, thus saving a portion of the crops. The Saints were forced to continue wi
2 CONC th their rations until harvest time, supplementing them with roots and herbs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emigrants passing through on their way to California, brought help to the people at a critical time through their trade of provisions such as clothing, tools, cattle, wagons, and money.  After a period of hard work they were able 
2 CONC to get settled quite comfortably and began to prosper.  At about this time, Joseph Lee Robinson was called upon to join a company of Saints going to Iron County to settle it in accordance with the wishes of Brigham Young who was a
2 CONC nxious to begin the colonization of this area.  The members of the company were told that after the colonization was fairly on its way, they would be free to come back to their former homes or do whatever else they wished.  Mr. Ro
2 CONC binson felt it was his duty to obey the call and consented to go. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The company was under the leadership of George Albert Smith.  He was told to prepare provisions, tools, farm equipment and also to select one of his families to go with him.  Susan agreed to go since it seemed that she was the mos
2 CONC t suitable having two boys who could be of great assistance, as well as the former slave, Black John.  A large wagon was loaded with potatoes, ham, shorts, a stove, etc. and hitched to four yoke of oxen. Black John and the boys dr
2 CONC ove or rode in the wagon.  Susan and Joseph Lee rode in a carriage.  They started December 10, 1850, George A. Smith and company going ahead. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon ascending the long hill between Salt Lake and Utah County, the oxen refused to pull the heavily loaded wagon despite the effort of the boys and Black John to aid them. Mr. Robinson was forced to hitch his $1,000 team up with 
2 CONC the oxen to get the load up the hill. The weather was very cold and it was necessary to keep fires going at night in order to keep the potatoes from freezing.  They built roads and forded streams all of which delayed their progres
2 CONC s considerably.  They stopped at Corn Creek, smoothed a piece of wire grassland, and had a New Year’s dance.  They spent a Sunday at Beaver Creek where they held an enjoyable meeting and then proceeded onward to Buckhorn Springs w
2 CONC here they found some of the company.  President Smith’s company was in the rear to the north of them. Suddenly the heard a cannon boom, greatly alarmed, and thinking it signified an Indian attack, they hurriedly formed the wagons 
2 CONC into a circle. Mr. Robinson suggested that this shot could be a salute to celebrate their entry into the Parowan Valley. They rode back to the main company and found that it was true. Their first camping sited was in Center Creek,
2 CONC  January 13, 1851. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They found plenty of grass for the animals, plenty of good cedar and pinion pines and no snow. Farther up in the canyon were good stands of pine.  Parowan was built on the north side of Center Creek.  The soil was red and looked i
2 CONC nfertile but the people optimistically thought that in good time, good crops of wheat and vegetables could be raised. There were also reports of iron deposits in the mountains farther to the south. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sidney R. Burton grew up in Parowan.  He had walked and driven cattle from Beaver to Parowan at the age of thirteen.  Even though young, he as always able to take a man’s part, taking his turn at standing guard at night and doing 
2 CONC many other things usually considered an adult’s responsibility.  The family stood the trip well.  One cold seemed to be the only ailment suffered. January 17, an election was held.  Buffalo robes were spread on the ground with tab
2 CONC le cloths over them. Ox yokes lined the sides for seats.  Dinner was served and later in the evening a dance was held.  Land was surveyed, a town laid out, ponds and canals built, a school started and in the spring, crops planted.
2 CONC   Joseph Lee Robinson claimed to have laid the first house foundation south of Provo. He stayed with his family until August 22, 1851; then accompanied George A. Smith back to Salt Lake, using his fine team and carriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was able to send a letter back to his wife while enroute telling her to be of good cheer and to give her expected child the name of Solomon.  The child born August 27, became ill in December of a blood infection but recovered a
2 CONC fter being administered to.  There is no record of Susan Burton Robinson every returning to Salt Lake.  She spent the rest of her life in Parowan, suffering many years with rheumatism.  Sidney took care of her well until her death
2 CONC  and burial in Parowan, April 18, 1872.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sidney’s first employer was John Hyatt, for whom he worked a year after arriving in Parowan.  He worked hard at any labor he could get and in a short time he had a small farm and some horses and cattle.  He had a special love and 
2 CONC understanding for the Indians.  He learned to speak their language and was their friend through many years of close association. They him “Potaticbates” meaning “very good man”; they loved him in return and came to him for advice.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT A patriarchal blessing given to him as a young man by Elisha Groves, promised that the time would come when the Lamanites would shout aloud his praises. In 1855, at the April Conference, Brigham Young called a company of thirty me
2 CONC n to the Las Vegas Mission to settle the place, make friends with the Indians and build a fort for the protection of travelers to and from California.  When the company from the northern settlements, under command of William Bring
2 CONC hurst of Springville, arrived in Parowan in May, William C. Mitchell, Sidney R. Burton, and John Steele joined them. They arrived in Las Vegas June 14, 1855. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Steele surveyed a fort 150 feet square, a large corral, thirty garden lots and fifteen five acre field lots. T hey built adobe homes in the fort.  John Steele returned to Parowan in November of 1855 since he was First Council
2 CONC or to President Calvin Smith who had died.  He later returned to Las Vegas at the request of Brigham Young.  This time he discovered lead mines with the aid of Indians.  A sample sent Brigham Young proved to be 90% lead.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sidney R. Burton served as an Indian Scout and did much to make friends with the Indians, teaching them to farm and be self-supporting.  He served as an interpreter to William C. Mitchell who was doing a fine job teaching them the
2 CONC  principles of the gospel.  A good number of the Indians joined the Mormon Church at this time.  Upon one occasion, an Indian informed Sidney R. that some of them were planning on coming into the fort on a certain day to the get t
2 CONC he layout of it in order to make an attack at a later date.  When the Indians arrived for their reconnaissance, the entire personnel of the fort were out having a target practice.  The attack never materialized. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1857, the men were released from their missions and returned to their homes in Parowan.  On March 22, 1859, Sidney R. Burton married Ann Maria Fish, the ceremony being performed by Amasa M. Lyman.  To them were bo
2 CONC rn the following children – Sidney, Julia, Hannah, Frank, Edward, and William Marian. He built a comfortable home on Main Street where many social gatherings were held.  He always encouraged his children to bring their friends hom
2 CONC e for entertainment.  Relatives from Salt Lake City, Beaver, etc. were always made welcome.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1861, Sidney was called to go with a company of men to go Moenkopi, Arizona to bring back the remains of George A. Smith who had been killed by an Indian.  An attempt had already been made by Jacob Hamblin who, with some of his
2 CONC  men, had gone to Leeds, Utah to get assistance. Fear of the Indians made the men there refuse to accompany him so he continued on the Parowan. There he was able to recruit the men he needed. Sidney took with him a beautiful hand-
2 CONC embroidered shirt made by his mother. Upon arrival at their destination, the accused Indian agreed to surrender the remains of the body in return for the silk shirt. As the group rode to the spot, the Indian and Sidney were riding
2 CONC  parallel to each other, each holding onto the shirt. The remains were recovered and the old Indian received the shirt. Sidney was a member of the Militia and served in all the Indian Wars. Nevertheless, he was a friend to the Ind
2 CONC ians. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There is just one incident on record where he showed severity with them.  It was the custom of the Indians to go to the various homes for food.  A group of them stopped at Sidney’s house and while his mother was preparing food for
2 CONC  them, she saw one of the Indians stealthily take a hat belonging to his brother William from a hook on the wall and hide it under his blanket.  She was very frightened but said nothing until Sidney came home.  He immediately went
2 CONC  in search of the Indian.  He found him astride his horse and proudly wearing the hat.  Sidney walked up to the Indian and began using his quirt on him.  The severity of the blows drew blood and brought the Indian Chief in a hurry
2 CONC  for an explanation.  After hearing it, he remarked that any man who had stolen a hat from Sidney’s squaw and frightened her deserve a whipping and gave Sidney permission to whip him again if he so desired. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon another occasion after having most of his cattle and horses stolen, he followed the trail up Little Creek Canyon and came back with payment for the herd. However, he always tried to be kind and especially to see that the Indi
2 CONC ans had food. One group of Indians came to see him not knowing of his death. They called at the home of his son Edward and wanted to have their horses fed. Edward told them to put them in the corral because there was no room in th
2 CONC e barn as his own horses were there. The Indians informed him that his father would have taken his horses out of the barn and allowed them to put their own in. Sidney’s hospitality extended to everyone – the writer of this article
2 CONC  still hears expressions of his extreme kindness, generosity and thoughtfulness. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One woman tells of falling on the ice in front of his home and being helped up and brought into his house to recover, although a total stranger to him.  People remember his kindness to them as children and his assistance to widows
2 CONC  at times of trouble.  He had no desire to live in the limelight – was humble and content to work hard.  A letter written from the iron mines and dated December 26, 1889, is somewhat indicative of his character.  It is as follows:
2 CONC   Dear Anna, I received your more than welcome letter. I was glad to hear you were all well. I didn’t have time to write to you at that time. We very seldom have a chance to send a letter to the station as we are working six or se
2 CONC ven miles from the road. Del and I have worked 16 days. The 24th and 25th were very cold here and we could not work. Expect to come home before the First of February. I hope you take care of yourself and be of good cheer, for ther
2 CONC e are better days for you and me. Tell our children their father will bring them some money when he comes. Dear Anna, I would like to write more but the man who is going to take this letter is waiting. From your husband, S.R. Burt
2 CONC on.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Sidney had an effective way of expressing himself.  At one time he and some of their neighbors had gone to a tent meeting to hear a protestant minister give a sermon. Upon being asked how he liked the sermon, he replied, “Well, th
2 CONC at man was a pretty good preacher, but the Mormons have been fed on wheat too long to be content with chaff.” Upon hearing his daughter express dissatisfaction with their poverty and lack of advantages in Parowan, he packed her of
2 CONC f to Salt Lake City to visit wealthy relatives for two weeks. She returned to her home thoroughly thankful for the simple, moral and hospitable life she enjoyed there. He encouraged young couples to plan a nice wedding for remembr
2 CONC ance instead of eloping. He was willing to go to any length to keep a boy from spending one night in jail. He could use profanity with the best of them. Upon hearing his grandson emulate him, he complained, “A child shouldn't be l
2 CONC et swear like that.” His daughter-in-law replied calmly, “All right Grandpa, we’ll give you the job of training him up. In addition to his notable work among the Indians, Sidney made six trips back to the Missouri River with ox te
2 CONC ams to bring immigrants and freight; each trip required six months’ time. He served two years on the city council and as Mayor of Parowan. It was said that his word was as good as his bond and that his counsel was valuable. One ma
2 CONC n, disregarding his advice to place drags on the back of his wagon when returning with heavy loads of wood from the mountains, had his wagon brake give way, his load lost and his son killed. Sidney believed in education and saw th
2 CONC at his children attended the best schools available – which in those days was the Presbyterian school. He loved music and some of his children were talented in that respect. He was morally upright and taught his philosophy to his 
2 CONC children by example. He lived to be 59 years of age, died May 28, 1897 and is buried in Parowan. The epitaph on his headstone at the cemetery reads as follows: “He was a noble pioneer and a friend to the friendless.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mission to Las Vegas:  In 1855, at the April Conference, Brigham Young called a company of 30 men to go to the Las Vegas mission to settle the place, make friends with the Indians, and build a fort for the protection of travelers 
2 CONC to and from California.  When the company from the northern settlements, under the command of William Bringhurst of Springville, arrived in Parowan.  In May, William C. Mitchell, Sidney R. Burton, and John Steele joined them.  The
2 CONC y arrived in Las Vegas June 14, 1855.  John Steele surveyed a fort 150 feet square, a large corral, 30 garden lots, and 15 5-acre field lots.  They built adobe homes in the old fort.  Sidney R. Burton served as an Indian scout, an
2 CONC d did much to make friends with the Indians, teaching them to farm and be self-supporting.  He served as Interpreter to William C. Mitchell, who was doing a fine job teaching them the principles of the gospel.  A good number of th
2 CONC e Indians joined the Mormon Church at this time.  Upon one occasion, an Indian informed Sidney R. that some of them were planning to come into the fort on a certain day to get the layout of it in order to make an attack at a later
2 CONC  date.  When the Indians arrived for their reconnaissance, the entire personnel of the fort were out having a target practice. The attack never materialized. In the spring of 1857, the men were released from their missions and ret
2 CONC urned to their homes in Parowan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1861, Sidney was called to go with a company of men to Moenkopi, Arizona to bring back the remains of George A. Smith (son of the Apostle George A. Smith) who had been killed by an Indian.  An attempt had already been made by J
2 CONC acob Hamblin who, with some of his men, had gone to Leeds, Utah, to get assistance.  Fear of the Indians made the men there refuse to accompany him, so he continued on to Parowan.  There, he was able to recruit the men he needed. 
2 CONC  Sidney took with him a beautiful, hand-embroidered shirt made by his mother.  Upon arrival at their destination, the accused Indian agreed to surrender the remains of the body in return for the silk shirt.  As the group rode to t
2 CONC he spot, the Indian and Sidney were riding parallel to each other, each holding on to the shirt.  The remains were recovered, and the old Indian received the shirt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Letter to his wife, Anna, from the Iron Mines west of Cedar City, Dec 26 1889:  Dear Anna, I received your more than welcome letter.  I was glad to hear you were all well.  I didn't have time to write you at that time.  We very se
2 CONC ldom have a chance to send a letter to the station as we are working 6 or 7 miles from the road.  Dell and I have worked 16 days.  The 24th and 25th were very cold here and we could not work.  Expect to come home before the 1st of
2 CONC  February.  I hope you will take care of yourself and be of good cheer, for there are better days for you and me.  Tell our children their father will bring them some money when he comes.  Dear Anna, I would like to write more but
2 CONC  the man who is going to take this letter is waiting.  From your husband, S. R. Burton.
2 CONT
