1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John M. Callaway by Donna F. Morgan from: Callaway Family Association - US Immigrant Joseph Callaway File, @http://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=cfa%2Djoseph&id=I1721
2 CONT
2 CONT 1850 Bedford Co., VA census shows three children living with this family, probably John's brother's children: Richard Callaway age 19, John M. Callaway age 18 and Betty Callaway age 14. On the 1860 Bedford Co., VA census John is l
2 CONC iving with Silas Early age 53. Lucinda appears to have died by this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lynchburg Virginian 7 December 1861, p.2 c.4 "DIED. "At his residence in Bedford county, on the 30th of October, 1861, JOHN CALLAWAY, in the 54th year of his age, after a painful, but brief illness, leaving three children, and man
2 CONC y warm friends, who were devotedly attached to him. Nor did they love him without a cause, his friendships were ardent, because they were genuine. "The writer of these lines knew him intimately more than twenty-five years. No man 
2 CONC better sustained the character of an honest man. As husband he was kind and indulgent—as father, he was affectionate and truthful—as master, he was tender and humane—and to the poor and distressed, his aid and advice were only to 
2 CONC be asked, his liberal hand was ever open. Such as these have many times shared his liberality and his bounty. Nor was it to be seen that he did it, but it was from his benevolent heart they sprang. He tilled his promises and oblig
2 CONC ations promptly and truthfully. He possessed to an extent far greater than is usual in our frail natures, virtues of head and heart, which could but render him beloved in a large circle of relations and friends. "The community in 
2 CONC which he lived will long feel the loss they have realized in the loss of this good man. Now in this season of dire and heavy affliction, when sorrows deep and irrepressible rend their hearts to the banishing of every peaceful sens
2 CONC ation. May his children, bereft of the dear one who possessed so large a share in their affections, & whose presence they deem essential to their enjoyment, whose familiar voice they will no more hear, whose cheering and animating
2 CONC  face, wrapped in grave clothes, goes back ashes to ashes and dust to dust.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sun beams depart, Flowers yield up their perfumed breath, But Memory keeps their beauties fresh, Long, long after death."
2 CONT
2 CONT "To whom can his children go for consolation? Who can alleviate their distress? Or whisper consoling words to their hearts, over-burdened with anguish, but to "Him who has a balm for every wound," Jesus Christ. His promises are fu
2 CONC ll of soothing and tenderness always. 
2 CONT
2 CONT And this obit - Lynchburg Virginian, 7 November 1861, p.3 c.1 "DIED. "At his residence in Bedford county, on Wednesday, the 30th inst., JNO M. CALLAWAY, in the 54th year of his age. Thus do we record the death of one around whom c
2 CONC lustered the tender stories of friendship and affection. A house is desolate, while society mourns the loss of him, whose heart was the home of the brightest, truest virtues that adorn man's nature--winning the friendship and good
2 CONC  will of all who came within the circle of his acquaintance, by his whole souled genial nature, and frank sincerity, he was the friend of all. True and faithful in every relation of life. As a father and friend, many will testify 
2 CONC to the generosity of his heart, possessed of an abundance of this worlds goods himself. The poor and needy ever found in him a friend. Upright and just in every action, we feel that from our midst, has passed away one, who in all 
2 CONC his dealings with his fellow man, was guided by the promptings of his own noble nature. This feeble tribute to his memory is paid by one, who enjoyed his friendship and esteemed his virtues, and who cherished the hope that in his 
2 CONC last moments, unseen by mortal eye, bright Angels were around him, and on their snowy pinions bore the disembodied spirit home." "A FRIEND"
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
