1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S.A.M._Wood
2 CONT
2 CONT Sterling Alexander Martin Wood (March 17, 1823 – January 26, 1891) commonly referred to as S.A.M. Wood, was an American lawyer and newspaper editor from Alabama. He served as a Confederate general during the American Civil War unt
2 CONC il 1863, and resumed practicing law, served as a state legislator, and later taught law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life and career
2 CONT
2 CONT Wood was born in Florence, Alabama, in the spring of 1823. He attended St. Joseph College in Kentucky in 1841, and then moved to Tennessee and became a lawyer there. In 1851 Wood returned to Alabama, where he served in the state l
2 CONC egislature. Wood also held the position of "solicitor" of Alabama's fourth judicial court from 1851 to 1857. He was editor of Florence's Gazette newspaper in 1860, during which he actively supported John C. Breckinridge's unsucces
2 CONC sful bid for President of the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Civil War
2 CONT
2 CONT Early service
2 CONT
2 CONT Wood chose to follow his home state of Alabama and the Confederate cause, and entered the state forces as a captain in the Alabama state forces as part of the "Florence Guard" on April 3, 1861.[2] He was elected colonel of the 7th
2 CONC  Alabama Infantry Regiment on May 18, and Wood and his regiment then served in Pensacola, Florida.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wood was then given brigade command in the Western Theater in October 1861, joining the Army of Central Kentucky. He was promoted to brigadier general on January 7, 1862. Wood then commanded a brigade in the Army of Mississippi du
2 CONC ring the Battle of Shiloh in Tennessee on April 6, 1862, and was wounded when his horse dragged him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perryville 
2 CONT
2 CONT Buckner's attack at PerryvilleWood's most notable Confederate service came on October 8, 1862, when he and his brigade fought at Perryville, Kentucky, in the Battle of Perryville. His brigade was part of Maj. Gen. Simon B. Buckner
2 CONC 's division in Maj. Gen. William J. Hardee's II Corps and participated in Buckner's attack on a Union position. The Confederates desired to force the Federals back and cut off their escape route at the Dixville Crossroads, effecti
2 CONC vely surrounding them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Union infantry and an artillery battery posted on a hill close to the Benton Road shot up Wood's men and forced them back to fall back. Wood reformed his brigade at the base of the hill and renewed the assault. The Federal guns ra
2 CONC n low on ammunition and withdrew, and the Confederate attack pushed the Union infantry back towards the crossroads. After the charge Wood's men withdrew and were replaced by Brig. Gen. St. John Richardson Liddell's reserve brigade
2 CONC . In this fight Wood was wounded in the head and would be out of action until November, by which the Army of Mississippi was now called the Army of Tennessee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Remaining campaigns
2 CONT
2 CONT Wood resumed command of his brigade on November 20, 1862, and fought in the Army of Tennessee's campaigns during the rest of 1862 and into 1863, including the bloody Battle of Stones River at year's end, throughout the Tullahoma C
2 CONC ampaign in the summer of 1863 and the Battle of Chickamauga that September. However Wood resigned his commission on October 17, just prior to the Battles for Chattanooga in November.
2 CONT
2 CONT Postbellum
2 CONT
2 CONT Wood resumed his law practice following his resignation from the Confederate Army, and would be pardoned by the U.S. Government after the war on November 4, 1865. He then served in again in the Alabama Legislature, and was a profe
2 CONC ssor of law at the University of Alabama from 1889 to 1890.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wood also was the attorney for the Alabama Great Southern Railway from its beginnings in 1877 until his death. In early in 1891 at the age of 67, Wood died and was buried in Tuscaloosa, Alabama.[
