1 NAME James Early /Lindsay/
2 GIVN James Early
2 SURN Lindsay
2 _MARNM Lindsay
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Saint Johns Episcopal Church Cemetery, Kingsville, Baltimore County, Maryland, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 22 SEP 1836
2 PLAC Greensboro, Guilford County, North Carolina, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 2 FEB 1892
2 PLAC Baltimore, Baltimore County, Maryland, USA
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 TITL Dr
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} James Early Lindsay was the third of eight children born to Jeduthan Harper Lindsay (1806-1881) and his wife Martha Strange (c. 1809-1867) of Greensborough (now Greensboro), Guilford County, NC. One of his great gr
2 CONC andfathers, Judge Robert Lindsay (c.1735-1801) was a member of the first independent NC Legislature in 1777 and 1778, and another was Lt. Col. Jeduthan Harper, a Soldier of the Revolution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT James was graduated from the University of North Carolina in 1857, and trained as a doctor. He was an assistant surgeon in the US Navy at the time War broke out in 1861, and was apparently arrested and confined at Fort Warren, Bos
2 CONC ton when the attempted to resign his commission and come home. He was imprisoned from October, 1861 to January, 1862. Eventually he was exchanged and served as an assistant surgeon on the CSS Virginia (more commonly known as the M
2 CONC errimac), the Confederate ironclad ship that met the Monitor in 1862. He was with the crew of the Virginia when it sank at Drewry's Bluff, Va., and later was sent to Europe to await assignment to another ship, but the war ended be
2 CONC fore the ships were finished. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the War, James went to Baltimore, Maryland. A web site, "Medicine in Maryland, 1752-1920," states that, "The second period of the Washington University Medical College began in 1867 when a group of Confederate physicians uni
2 CONC ted to form a medical school with the expressed purpose to 'arrest the tide that tended Northward, and offer to the young men of the South congenial homes.' The chief instigator was Edward Warren, who had distinguished himself as 
2 CONC Surgeon-General of North Carolina during the Civil War and who had returned to Baltimore to resume his chair as Professor of Materia Medica and Therapeutics at the University of Maryland. Conditions had necessitated that his chair
2 CONC  be filled before his return, so when he did not succeed in regaining his old position, he joined with a number of physicians, most of whom were newcomers to Baltimore, to found an new school…" It seems likely to me that James Ear
2 CONC ly Lindsay would have been one of the original Confederate physicians. He was listed in the Baltimore City Directory in 1867. He was listed as a professor and Dean of the Faculty in 1877. by 1890, he was a professor at Baltimore M
2 CONC edical College, which had been founded in 1881. I don't know if he was one of the founders or not. (His cousin, Dr. Isaac James Madison Lindsay, 1804-1854, had founded a medical school in NC in the late 1820s.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT James Early Lindsay married Miss Charlotte E. "Lottie" Gittings (1842-1933) and they had two daughters, Charlotte and Margaret. He died in Baltimore on 2 Feb. 1892, and was buried at St. John's Episcopal Church, Kingsville, Baltim
2 CONC ore County, Maryland. (His family were all Presbyterians; perhaps he became an Episcopalian at the time of his marriage.) Mrs. Lindsay survived him by more than 40 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Obituary: Dr. James E. Lindsay, No. 9 East Read street, died yesterday. He had been in poor health for two years, and was afflicted with spinal meningitis, the result of a fall. Dr. Lindsay was educated at Chapel Hill, N. C., and
2 CONC  Jefferson College, Philadelphia. He served in the United States Navy until the beginning of the civil war, when he joined the Confederate Navy and served throughout the conflict. After the war he came to Baltimore. For eight year
2 CONC s he was dean of the Maryland University, and at the time of his death was professor of chemistry in the Maryland Dental College. He married a daughter of Dr. David S. Gittings, who with two children survives him. Dr. Lindsay was 
2 CONC in his fifty-sixth year. He was a native of North Carolina."  Monday, Feb. 8, ,1892 (presumably from the Baltimore Sun or some other paper in Baltimore, thanks to Lynn McGee).* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/30722
2 CONC 709/james-early-lindsay Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Feb 7 2020, 1:48:37 UTC''
