1 NAME James Winright /Flanagan/ U.S. Senator
2 GIVN James Winright
2 SURN Flanagan
2 NSFX U.S. Senator
2 _MARNM Flanagan
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Flanagan Family Cem. Henderson, Rusk Co, TX21
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 5 SEP 1805
2 ADDR
3 CITY Gordonsville
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 28 SEP 1887
2 ADDR
3 CITY Longview
3 STAE Texas
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_W._Flanagan
2 CONT
2 CONT James Winright Flanagan (September 5, 1805 – September 28, 1887) was an American merchant, lawyer, and farmer from Henderson, Texas. He served as Lieutenant Governor of Texas in 1869 and 1870, and then represented Texas in the Uni
2 CONC ted States Senate from 1870 to 1875.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT James was born to Charles and Elizabeth (Saunders) Flanagan in Albemarle County near Gordonsville, Virginia. Before his tenth birthday, the family moved to Boonesboro, Kentucky. As a young man he moved to Cloverport, Kentucky, on 
2 CONC the Ohio River and became a prosperous merchant. He also read law and was admitted to the Kentucky bar in 1825. He married Polly Moorman in 1826 and the couple had several children before moving to Henderson, Texas, in 1844.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Flanagan established himself in Henderson by opening a store. He bought a farm, speculated in land, and practiced law. Politically, he was a Whig and an active supporter of Sam Houston. He later became a moderate Republican. Flana
2 CONC gan served in the Texas House of Representatives (1851–1852) and the Texas State Senate (1855–1858).
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Civil War came to Texas, Flanagan was a Unionist, but he withdrew to his farm and lived quietly. He returned to active politics during the Reconstruction. He served as a delegate to both Constitutional Conventions. The fi
2 CONC rst, in 1866, produced a state constitution that was rejected by the Radical Republicans in the U.S. Congress. The second, in 1868–1869, was successful.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the new Constitution, Flanagan was elected Lieutenant Governor. He served there only until 1870, when Texas was readmitted to the Union. The legislature named him, along with Morgan Hamilton, to the U.S. Senate. He served on
2 CONC e term as a Senator, until 1875 when he was replaced by the Democrat Samuel Maxey. In the Senate he was a supporter of the Grant Administration.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT After his Senate term, Flanagan took up residence on one of his farms near Longview, Texas. He married again (he was widowed twice and married three times), this time to Elizabeth Lane. The three marriages produced a total of elev
2 CONC en children. One of Flanagan’s children was David Webster Flanagan who also served as Lieutenant Governor in Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flanagan died on his farm in Longview in 1887 and was buried next to his first wife, Polly, in their family graveyard in Henderson, Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
