1 NOTE John is descended from Henry I, King of France 1031-1060 and Anne
2 CONT of Kiev (Russia).  (Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists, 1988, p 191
2 CONT and Your Family Tree, 1929, p 309)
2 CONT      When John left Boston he bought land at Taunton, but did not
2 CONT settle there and came to Windsor, CT before 1639.  He died while
2 CONT "driving a cart loaded with corn to carry from his house to his son
2 CONT Jacob's; the cattle being two oxen, and his mare.  In the highway,
2 CONT against John Griffin's, something scared the cattle, and they set a
2 CONT running, and he laboring to stop them by taking hold on the mare, was thrown down on his face, and the cart-wheel went over him, brake one of his legs, and bruised his bodye so that he was taken up dead, being carried to his daugh
2 CONC ter's house, had life come again, but dyed in a short time..."  (Stiles, Vol 2, pp 177-178)
2 CONT
2 CONT came over in the Winthrop fleet
2 CONT
2 CONT Church records.
2 CONT
2 CONT 14 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonial and Revolutionary Lineage, Vol 1, p. 172.  John Drake, son of William and Philippa Denys Drake, was born at Wiscomb, County Devon, England, about 1590 or 1600 and died 8/17/1659, the result of an injury [ run over by an o
2 CONC x cart wheel].  The exact date of John Drake's arrival in New England is not known, but he was listed among those to become freemen, according to the "Boston Record Commissioners' Report," 10/19/1630.  His name does not appear on 
2 CONC later lists in Boston and details of his life for the next few years have not been found.  He was one of the 46 purchasers of Taunton, Mass in 1637, and was in Windsor early in 1639. John Drake received a grant of 14 acres, a home
2 CONC  lot with meadow adjoining from the plantation at Windsor, CT, 1/26/1640.  He was a member of the jury in the Particular Court, in June, 1643, and for 8 sessions afterward.  The inventory of his estate was taken 9/14/1659 and amou
2 CONC nted to L324-13-00.  John Drake married, probably in England, Elizabeth Rogers, who died at the home of her son Jacob, 10/7/1681.  It is said that she was one hundred years old at the time of her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCU came over in the Winthrop fleet
1 NOTE RFN: 19762
2 CONT Immigrant ancestor, Came to Boston in 1630.
1 NOTE !Information on the Drake families found in the Books on the Mary and John
2 CONT Passengers.  Vols I-IX. Also in the New England Registry vol II.
2 CONT He died as a result of being accidently run over by a cart whe he was hauling
2 CONT corn.
