2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 1st 9 Nov 1671 Windsor, Hartford, Connecticut,
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 2nd about 1696.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Veteran of the King Phillips War. In October 1685, Sir Edmund Andreus demanded the Connecticut charter for England. John was at the meeting at Hartford, Connecticut where the charter was taken and hid in the great oak tree. Soon a
2 CONC fter this John Higley was commissioned by Colonies Governor Robert Treat as an officer of the Militia. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Veteran of the King Phillips War.
2 CONT In October 1685, Sir Edmund Andreus demanded the Connecticut charter for England. John was at the meeting at Hartford, Connecticut where the charter was taken and hid in the great oak tree. Soon after this John Higley was commissi
2 CONC oned by Colonies Governor Robert Treat as an officer of the Militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIO excerpts from the "Early Settlers of West Simsbury", Items of History of the Higley Family, by Abiel Brow, Esq, published 1856.
2 CONT - John Higley, Esq., was among the early settlers in Simsbury. The place where he and his numerous descendants lived in Simsbury, was north-west from Tariffville. It was formerly called Higley town after the name of its inhabitant
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Hannah, daughter of John and Hannah Drake, and granddaughter of Deacon John and Hannah Moore, married 1671.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was honored with many of the highest offices in the gift of his fellow-citizens, being the first appointed justice of the peace, and soon after, judge of the county court. He represented the town at the General Assembly, for my
2 CONC  sessions, subsequent to the year 1698.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the first military captain, being chosen in 1698, and office at that time of great dignity.
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Inscription'''=
2 CONT Capt John Higly died August 25, 1714 aged 66 years
2 CONT ----
