2 CONC ice of John Drake by choice.  It was at about this time, he having come to manhood, that he and Hannah Drake, the eldest of John Drake's daughters, plighted their troth.  But in those days it was not a simple matter to get married
2 CONC .  Though they certainly had the blessings of the Drake household, there was still the mater of John's own mother in England and the unsatisfied master glove maker. With the same Higley fortitude and perserverance that he passed t
2 CONC o future generations of Higley's, John undertook the 51 day voyage back to England, Landing safely he proceeded to Frimely and soon stood once more upon his mother's threshold.  After a five year absence, the tall, strong man on h
2 CONC er door step did not resemble her son and Katherine Higley had no clue as to who it was who greeted her and handed her a letter.  The letter contained his declaration of true and honest heart and she wept as she read it.  Glancing
2 CONC  at the stranger before her, she caught her breath as maternal in stinct moved her.  Advancing to his side she parted his hair and discovered a scar from a fall John had taken down some stairs when he wa ten.  "John, you rogue!  I
2 CONC s that you?"  she exclaimed and soundly cuffed his ear.  Of course, after the happy reunion she gave John her blessing and after making satisfactory arrangements with the glove maker, he was once more on a ship to America to claim
2 CONC  his bride.  After their marriage the young couple took up residence on the eastern shore of the Connecticut River and raised a large family.  Their children were John, Johathan, Elizabeth, Katherine, Brewster, Hannah, Joseph, Sam
2 CONC uel and Mindwell.  John prospered and became one of Connecticut's leading citizens.  In 1671 he was elected Constable for Windsor.  It was also that year that he erected a warehouse and began his mercantile career that was to make
2 CONC  him one of the wealthiest men in Connecticut, dealing primarily in sugar, molasses and rum between the colonies and Bermuda and Jamica.  By 1683 he held the additional office of Customs Officer and his fortune was growing rapidly
2 CONC .  He decided to move the family to Simsbury, Connecticut, about nine miles from Windsor, where he purchased 94 acres plus some additional adjacent land.  Here he erected a colonial home, much above the standard of the time, and m
2 CONC oved burgeoning family.  As John purchased more adjacent land over the years and extended his holdings for more than four miles along the river, the region began to be called Higleytown and was known as that for more than 150 year
2 CONC s.  One of the most interesting stories concerning John Higley revolves around the political affairs of the colony.  For in 1685 King James II of England came to power and promptly required the colonies to surrender their charters
2 CONC  in order to establish dominions under a Crown appointed governor-general.  On 31, October 1687, Sir Edmund Andros arrived in Hartford, with a company of soldiers, while the General Assembly was in session.  John Higley was presen
2 CONC t at the assembly.  Andros demanded the assembly surrender their charter.  After heated debate that lasted until night-fall, the charter was brought into the chamber and laid upon the table in front of Andros.  Suddenly the lights
2 CONC  were extinguished, leaving the chamber in pitch dark, and the charter was spirited away.  John Higley, as tradition goes, was given the charter by Captain John Wadsworth, the chief actor in the saving of the Charter, and sped awa
2 CONC y through the night to Higleytown.  There the charter stayed for six weeks until it found its way to the famous Charter Oak in Hartford.  We find John soon after given a commission as ensigh in the Connecticut militia which was, a
2 CONC t the time, the highest ranking officer in Simsbury.  In 1689 he was elected to the General Assemhbly.  John was devastated in 1694 by the death of his wife Hannah.  She passed away on August 4 of that year at the age of 41.  John
2 CONC  did not resume his seat at the General Assembly again until 1698.  These three years and the year 1703 were the only years he did not serve the assembly from 1687 until 1711.  In 1696 John remarried Sarah Strong Bissel, who is ou
2 CONC r ancestor.  She was a widow with two children of  her own.  In spite of having now a family of eleven children, Sarah and John had a large family of their own:  Sarah, Nathaniel, Josiah (our ancestor), Abigail, Susanna and Isaac.
2 CONC   And you wonder why there are so many Higley's?  In 1698 John, who was by then a Lieutenant in the militia, was promoted by the General Assembly to the rank of Captian and given his own company.  It is by the title "Captain" that
2 CONC  John has come down to tradition.  John continued to prosper and was engaged in numerous business and public pursuits up until the day he died, August 25, 1714.  His estate showed his tremendous increase since the day he ran away 
2 CONC from home to a strange new world to found a distinguished bloodline in America.  SOURCE: abbreviation:  The Higleys and their Ancestry, 1963.SOURCE NOTES:  English Origins of the "Mary & John" Passengers by Ann Natalie Hansen, con
2 CONC tinued: 1985 source: abbreviation:  History of the Descendents of Elder John Strong of Northhampton, Mass.
