2 CONT ===Records
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/memories/memory/120379640?cid=mem_copy
2 CONT
2 CONT Trumbull Bible
2 CONT This record was published in the National Genealogical Society
2 CONT Quarterly, Vol 15, No. 1, March, 1927, p 10.
2 CONT
2 CONT SURNAMES:
2 CONT TRUMBULL
2 CONT WOODWORTH
2 CONT PHELPS
2 CONT
2 CONT BIBLE RECORD OF REV. BENJAMIN TRUMBULL (CONN.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Record noted by Mrs. Carrie W. Avery from a Bible in possession of Mrs. Ansell Woodworth, of Modesto, 
2 CONT  California, who is a great-grandaughter of Rev. Benjamin Trumbull (1735-1820). 
2 CONT
2 CONT This Bible is said to have been carried through the Revolution by Benjamin Trumbull, who was a chaplain in the army and is said to have used it as a pillow.
2 CONT
2 CONT On fly leaf: "Benjamin Trumbull's Bible bought Anno 1760" "Edinburgh, printed by Adrian Watkins,
2 CONT His Majesty's Printer MDCCLV." Family Record:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Benjamin Trumbull was born at Hebron, December 19th, 1735, Old Stile, was graduated at Yale
2 CONT College, Sept. 12, 1759. He was married to Mrs. Martha Phelps, December 20th, 1760, and the 21st of
2 CONT the same month was ordained to the pastorial office in the church of North Haven.
2 CONT --------
2 CONT And died Feb. 2nd, 1820, in the 85 year of his age, and in the 60th year of his ministry.
2 CONT Rev. Benjamin Trumbull fied February 2nd, 1820, aged 84 years."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Martha Trumbull
2 CONT Died June 21, 1825,
2 CONT aged 92 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Aaron Woodward
2 CONT Died February 25th, 1840,
2 CONT aged 79 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Martha Woodward
2 CONT Died December 10th, 1851,
2 CONT aged 88 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Mrs. Martha Woodward was the daughter of Rev. Benj. Trumbull. See Trumbull's History of Connecticut.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From < [https://historicbuildingsct.com/rev-benjamin-trumbull-house-1761/#google_vignette Rev. Benjamin Trumbull House (1761)] >
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Trumbull’s house, which faces the Green in North Haven, is discussed in Vol. 1 of J. L. Rockey’s History of New Haven County (1892):
2 CONT
2 CONT >It was in the summer of the latter year [1760] that he came to North Haven church, and November 14th was ordained as its pastor. The following year he purchased a tract of land of Joseph Pierpont, and began the erection of a dwel
2 CONC ling house upon it. The old mansion is still standing and in excellent repair. It is the property of Hon. Ezra Stiles, who has occupied it something more than 60 years. As a historic point, there is none greater in the town. The g
2 CONC reat double doors were ever ajar. Over its threshold were ceaselessly trooping scores of busy feet. Ministers, messengers, committees, referees and strangers made it a religious caravansary and rested in its shadow. Hither came du
2 CONC ring the revolutionary war aids and officers with despatches, and later eminent historians and theologists tarried within its walls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thorpe also attests to the many visitors Rev. Trumbull entertained at the house:
2 CONT
2 CONT >The great double doors of this hospitable mansion were ever ajar. Over the threshold tradition tells us, were ceaselessly trooping many busy feet, and its owner soon became widely known. Ministers and messengers journeying to and
2 CONC  fro to religious gatherings, took roundabout roads to call on this rising divine. Referees, committees, consociations, came to test his judgment and his wife’s hospitality, both exhaustless. As he came in later years to be still 
2 CONC more widely celebrated, the calibre of his visitors increased. Many an eminent man visiting Yale college thought his mission far from complete until he had ridden out to North Haven and Visited “Dr. Trumbull.”
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000207858554825&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
