1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Family Connection''': Her husband, Levin Backus, was a Revolutionary War soldier; his records often place the family's activities and residence firmly in the Gilead parish of Hebron.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Levin Backus''' (also recorded as Living Backus) served as a soldier from Connecticut during the Revolutionary War. His service is primarily associated with the Hebron area, where he was a resident.
2 CONT '''Service Details'''
2 CONT While specific individual muster roll dates can vary by enlistment period, his service typically includes:
2 CONT Unit: He served in the Connecticut Militia and is often linked to the Lexington Alarm or subsequent calls for state troops.
2 CONT '''Residence during Service:''' Hebron, Tolland County, Connecticut.
2 CONT Rank: Generally listed as a Private or soldier in local military lists.
2 CONT '''
2 CONT Key Records for Verification'''
2 CONT You can find further specific details, such as his specific company and duration of service, in the following primary sources:
2 CONT The Record of Connecticut Men in the Military and Naval Service During the War of the Revolution: This is the definitive 1889 volume (often called "Johnston’s Record") that lists every documented Connecticut soldier.
2 CONT Connecticut Revolutionary War Military Lists (1775–1783): Available on Ancestry, this database provides unit info and residence for over 13,000 Connecticut men.
2 CONT SAR Patriot Research System: This free database often contains biographical sketches and grave registry information for soldiers like Levin.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Historical Context'''
2 CONT Hebron was a significant center for patriot activity, and Levin’s family (including the Posts) were deeply embedded in the local militia. His service would have likely involved defending the Connecticut coast or participating in c
2 CONC ampaigns in the Northern Department (New York/New Jersey)
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical and Family Context'''
2 CONT Marriage:''' He married Ann Post Barker on February 14, 1771, in Hebron.
2 CONT Revolutionary War Service: He is recognized as a patriot for his service in the Connecticut Militia. His name appears in the Record of Connecticut Men in the Military and Naval Service During the War of the Revolution, where he is
2 CONC  documented alongside other men from the Hebron area.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: His children included Ann, Levin Jr., Ezra, and Abner Backus, many of whom remained in the Tolland County area.
2 CONT
2 CONT His life is well-documented in the Barbour Collection of Connecticut Town Vital Records, which serves as a primary source for the birth, marriage, and death dates of early Connecticut settlers.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Burial Information'''
2 CONT While there is no specific modern headstone record for Levin, historical data strongly suggests he is buried in the Gilead Cemetery in Hebron.
2 CONT '''Historical Documentation:''' The Hebron Historic Properties Commission notes that 31 Revolutionary War veterans are buried in Gilead Cemetery.
2 CONT '''Family Ties:''' Levin lived in the Gilead parish of Hebron, and his wife’s family, the Posts, were instrumental in establishing the cemetery; Jordan Post was one of the three men who originally acquired the land for the "Buryin
2 CONC g Place" in 1750.
2 CONT Regiment: 12th Regiment of Militia, which was primarily composed of men from the towns of Hebron, Lebanon, and Colchester.
2 CONT Company: He is specifically documented in Captain Worthy Waters' Company.
2 CONT Lexington Alarm (1775): Levin was one of the men who "marched for the relief of Boston" during the Lexington Alarm in April 1775. His name is found on the muster rolls of the Hebron company that responded to this first call to arm
2 CONC s.
2 CONT Subsequent Service: Following the initial alarm, he continued his service in the state militia, which was responsible for defending the Connecticut coast and providing reinforcements to the Continental Army when needed. 
2 CONT Ancestry.com
2 CONT Ancestry.com
2 CONT  +3
2 CONT Official Records
2 CONT His service is officially recognized and recorded in the definitive volume Record of Connecticut Men in the Military and Naval Service During the War of the Revolution, edited by Henry P. Johnston. This record is the primary proof
2 CONC  used by the SAR and DAR to verify his status as a patriot.
