0 @N6000000002339097527@ NOTE By the time he was seven, both Seth's parents had died.  When his motherdied, he (along with his siblings Fred, Lola, and Martha) was sent to the Soldiers' Orphans' Home in Davenport, IA.  While there, he received a good education
1 CONC .  At age 16 he went to work on the Pangborn farm near Bayard, IA with his brother Fred.  When Fred got married, Seth went to California for 3 or 4 years.  He was there at the time of the San Francisco earthquake, April 18, 1906. 
1 CONC  He worked in lumber camps, fruit farms, and finally was the "meat cook" in a restaurant.  He returned to Bayard and farmed with Fred until 1915 when they bought a farm east of Montedvideo, near the Olson farm.  He married Ella Ol
1 CONC son in 1919 and in 1920, Seth and Ella rented a nearby farm.  They lived on the rented farm until 1943 when they purchased a farm in Kandiyohi County, near Willmar, MN.  His son, Chuck, worked the farm when Seth was no longer able
1 CONC  to do so himself.  Seth was active in Farmers Union activities, cooperative elevation and oil station, and was secretary of the local group.  He was an avid reader and enjoyed raising colts.  He had a beautiful singing voice and 
1 CONC sang with a male quartet to sell war bonds.  He died of a hear attack in 1958.[CRONE Family-1.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [CRONE Family-1.FTW.GED.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT By the time he was seven, both Seth's parents had died.  When his motherdied, he (along with his siblings Fred, Lola, and Martha) was sent to the Soldiers' Orphans' Home in Davenport, IA.  While there, he received a good education
1 CONC .  At age 16 he went to work on the Pangborn farm near Bayard, IA with his brother Fred.  When Fred got married, Seth went to California for 3 or 4 years.  He was there at the time of the San Francisco earthquake, April 18, 1906. 
1 CONC  He worked in lumber camps, fruit farms, and finally was the "meat cook" in a restaurant.  He returned to Bayard and farmed with Fred until 1915 when they bought a farm east of Montedvideo, near the Olson farm.  He married Ella Ol
1 CONC son in 1919 and in 1920, Seth and Ella rented a nearby farm.  They lived on the rented farm until 1943 when they purchased a farm in Kandiyohi County, near Willmar, MN.  His son, Chuck, worked the farm when Seth was no longer able
1 CONC  to do so himself.  Seth was active in Farmers Union activities, cooperative elevation and oil station, and was secretary of the local group.  He was an avid reader and enjoyed raising colts.  He had a beautiful singing voice and 
1 CONC sang with a male quartet to sell war bonds.  He died of a hear attack in 1958.
0 @N6000000002339106010@ NOTE By the time she was only three, both Mattie's parents had died.  When hermother died, she (along with her siblings Fred, Lola, and Seth) was sent to the Soldiers' Orphans' Home in Davenport, IA.  When she was 16 she went to live w
1 CONC ith her uncle, Henry Phelps, and then went to New York City to be with her sister Maude.  Later she lived for two years with her brother Fred and his wife Nina near Bayard, IA.  She graduated with a two-year high school diploma an
1 CONC d went to Des Moines, IA to work.  She married Clyde Short.  During WWII, she worked for the government in munitions in Des Moines.  After Mattie's sister Maude died, Mattie and Clyde moved to Coronado, CA where Mattie had lived. 
1 CONC  Clyde died there and after his death, Mattie moved back to Iowa.She died in a nursing home in Sigourney, IA.[CRONE Family-1.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [CRONE Family-1.FTW.GED.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT By the time she was only three, both Mattie's parents had died.  When hermother died, she (along with her siblings Fred, Lola, and Seth) was sent to the Soldiers' Orphans' Home in Davenport, IA.  When she was 16 she went to live w
1 CONC ith her uncle, Henry Phelps, and then went to New York City to be with her sister Maude.  Later she lived for two years with her brother Fred and his wife Nina near Bayard, IA.  She graduated with a two-year high school diploma an
1 CONC d went to Des Moines, IA to work.  She married Clyde Short.  During WWII, she worked for the government in munitions in Des Moines.  After Mattie's sister Maude died, Mattie and Clyde moved to Coronado, CA where Mattie had lived. 
1 CONC  Clyde died there and after his death, Mattie moved back to Iowa.She died in a nursing home in Sigourney, IA.
0 @N6000000001720853112@ NOTE All seven sons of Augustine Leftwich were officers in the Revolutionary War.
1 CONT Two were captains, one was a major, two were colonels, and two, Joel and Jabez
1 CONT were generals.
0 @N6000000002312233068@ NOTE [lewis.FTW.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT From Elizabeth Adams' notes:
1 CONT
1 CONT The story goes that one day Colonel Leftwich picked up a strange looking leaf in front of the Hare factory and was told by a druggist friend that it was an olive leaf used by the Italians to wrap a medicinal plant called licorice.
1 CONC  After a few experiments, Colonel Leftwich and Hare came up with a secret formula used for flavoring the chewing tobacco. It met with great success all over the nation. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Joseph C Robert in his "Story of Tobacco in America" says, "In Lynchburg, a small town of unusual wealth known as 'Tobacco City', there was a Colonel Augustine Leftwich described in 1830 by Mrs. Anne Royall as ' . . . a stout, nob
1 CONC le figure and a very gentlemanlike man, his complexion dark, with an arched brow, and fine, full hazel eyes; his manners are polished and familiar.'"
