0 @N6000000000682146010@ NOTE Line 13706 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   DEAT SOUR Descendants of John Porter of Windsor, CT.  1635-9 compiled by Henry Porter
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 13707 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   DEAT SOUR Descendants of John Porter of Windsor, CT.  1635-9 compiled by Henry Porter CONT Andrews
1 CONT
1 CONT Dorothy Guidette:  b. ca 1615 Bedfordshire (BDF), ENG m. 10/07/1640
1 CONT d 11-Sep-1662, Simsbury, CT
1 CONT     ___________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 13712 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE Bob Caulfield  <ajrotc01@email.badkreuznach.army.mil>
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas BARBER, immigrant anc, came to NE in ship "Christian" Mar 16, 1634, settled in Windsor, CT.  Soldier in Pequot fight.
1 CONT Married Oct 7 1640 Jane (also  called Joan)___ who died 10 Sep 1662. Thomas died 11 Sep 1662. He  & Jane had 6 kids; Mary was #5 born 12 Oct 1651. She married (1) 8 Jul 1669 John GILLETT and (2) 20 Jun 1683 George NORTON of Spring
1 CONC field.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Descendants of John Porter: immigrated to NE on "Christian" in 1635.  He served in Pequot War, removed to Simsbury, CT, and d. 1652.
1 CONT
1 CONT           _____________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 13723 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE From: william.bagwill@mwtelcom.com (William Bagwill) Date: Sun, 12 Feb 95 15:26:00 -0500
1 CONT
1 CONT Subject: BARBER,Thomas #1751 b 1614 in London, d 1662.
1 CONT          married Jane ??? #1752 7 Oct 1640 at Windsor,Conn.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas and Jane are around my tenth great grandparents on a maternal side.  This information is from some family papers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas and William arrived on "Christior [or Christian]", apprenticed to Francis Stiles, a carpenter.  Landed at Plymouth in 1635, fifteen years after the colonies founding.  They went to Windsor, Connecticut for 10 years.  In 164
1 CONC 5, Thomas moved to West Semsbury in 1645.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas landed at Plymouth Rock, and around twenty one years old.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas and William apprenticed themselves to Francis Stiles.
1 CONT Francis was a carpenter and a neighbor headed for America.  The brothers and some friends traveled on the 'Chrisior' in 1635.
1 CONT This fifteen years after the Pilgrims landed at Plymouth Rock.
1 CONT The two brothers and Francis Styles went to Connecticut and Windsor.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Pequod War two years later may indicate the brothers fought in it, as Captain John Mason of Windsor led a company of soldiers against the Pequod.
1 CONT
1 CONT The bothers lived at Windsor for some ten years.  In 1645, Thomas moved to West Semsbury, some distance west of Windsor, and built the efforts church house in that town.  He had now mastered his trade of carpentering.  Just how lo
1 CONC ng he  lived at West Semsbury is not known.  It probably was after the war with the Mohican Indians, who occupied the western  part of Connecticut and Massachusetts, when the indian menace to the west was removed, that migrated to
1 CONC  the valley of the Hudson River and settled in Saratoga County, New York.  Here the family remained for many years.
1 CONT
1 CONT The descendants of William Barber, I do not know.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas married Jane ??? Phelps. Connecticut.   He had a son named Thomas Jr, born in 1644 at Windsor and married Mary Phelps.
1 CONT Thomas Jr. died in 1701 at the age of 57.
