0 @N6000000001540786105@ NOTE He came to Connecticut, 1639, at the time of the second visit of George Fenwick
1 CONT when many other settlers came. He was attorney for a Mr. St. Nicholas, of
1 CONT Warwickshire, who had a house built for him at Windsor and a tract ofland
1 CONT impaled, as had also Sir Richard Saltonstall. There were many other prominent
1 CONT Puritans in Warwickshire intending to settle in the colonies, when a change in
1 CONT the political conditions in England caused them to stay there. Rev. Ephraim
1 CONT Hewett and the Wylys family were two others from Warwickshire. Griswold had a
1 CONT grant of land at Poquonock to which he removed in 1649, when his house was the
1 CONT outpost of the colony. It was on the site of the Eliphalet S. Ladd house,
1 CONT having the Tunxis river on the south and west.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was active in public affairs. In 1650 he helped build the fort at
1 CONT Springfield for Pyncheon. He was a deputy to the general court from Windsor in
1 CONT 1656; and every season but one afterward until the new charter was granted. He
1 CONT was a prominent settler of Homonosett or West Saybrook, whither about1663 he
1 CONT removed with his younger children, deeding to his sons George and Joseph his
1 CONT Windsor property, reserving a small annuity. * The settlement was organized as
1 CONT a town in 1667 and received the name of his English birthplace and home.
1 CONT Kenilworth, which became strangely perverted in the spelling to Killingworth,
1 CONT and is now Clinton,Connecticut. He was the first deputy from the town
1 CONT magistrate and deputy for more than twenty years.1662- 1688/9, and was
1 CONT succeeded in office by his son John. the colonial records show him tohave
1 CONT been an active and influential member of the legislature, accomplishing much
1 CONT good. He had the pleasure of meeting his own son Francis and brother mathew in
1 CONT office, and there has scarcly been a time since when the family has not been
1 CONT represented in the legislature of the province and state. In 1678 he was on
1 CONT the committe to establish a Latin school at new London; he was deaconof the
1 CONT Kilingworth church; he died there in 1691 aged eighty -four years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward Griswold, was one of two brothers who came to New England in 1639, in a
1 CONT vessel sent out by Mr. William Whitney.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was deputy to the general court in 1658 and justice of the peace before
1 CONT 1663.
1 CONT
1 CONT * In that year the settlement of the plantation of Hamonassett began,and was
1 CONT afterward called Kenilworth. From the place of Griswold's birth.
1 CONT
1 CONT !NOTE: The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, Ancestral File (R); ;
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1987, June 1998, data as of 5 January 1998; ; , Family History
1 CONT Library, 35 N West Temple Street, Salt Lake City, UT 84150 USA
1 CONT
1 CONT !NOTE: Cutter, A Genealogical History of Ct.; ; Vol. 1; p. 224; ;
1 CONT
1 CONT !BIRTH: Cutter, A Genealogical History of Ct.; ; Vol. 1; 224/521; ;
1 CONT
1 CONT !BIRTH: Families Of Early Hartford CT; ; Hartford , Hartford Co. CT; p. 29; ,
1 CONT Spec. Collect, Albq. Nm
1 CONT
1 CONT !MARRIAGE:Families Of Early Hartford CT; ; Hartford , Hartford Co. CT; p. 29;
1 CONT , Spec. Collect, Albq. Nm
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Edward Griswold came to America at the time of the second visit of Mr. George Fenwick, at which date, also, came a large number of newsettlers to the Conn. settlement. It was at a time when many of the gentry of England and we
1 CONC althy persons connected with the Warwick Patentwere intending removal hither; but the breaking out of the Scotch Rebellion compelled King Charles to call a Parliament, and they stayed athome to carry on their struggle with the Kin
1 CONC g and Archbishop Laud. Mr. Griswold undoubtedly came in the interest of some of these patentees. He was attorney for Mr. St. Nicholas of Warwickshire, who had a house built in Windsor, and also a tract of land "impaled" (fenced), 
1 CONC as had Sir Richard Saltonstall. The Rev. Ephraim Hit, who came, also in 1639, was from the same parish, as, also, the Wyllys family, who settledat Hartford.
1 CONT
1 CONT His first location in Windsor is not known; but he had a grant of land in Poquonock, to which re removed, in 1649, accompanied by a few families, who there found an "outpost" settlement. His residence at Poquonock was on the site 
1 CONC of the present dwelling of the heirs of the lateEliphalet S. Ladd, and who, on the female side, are Griswold descendants. The spot is a beautiful knoll which overlooks the brook on the west, and the Tunxis River on the south and e
1 CONC ast. As soon as he had fairly established his home, he began to take that active part i public matters which was natural to a man of his character. In 1650 he built the "Old Fort" at Springfield for Mr. Pyncheon; in 1656 he was a 
1 CONC deputyfrom Windsor to the General Court, and continued, with the exception of one session, to represent the town until the reception of the charter from King Charles. At this time he was the principal promoter of a new settlement 
1 CONC authorized by the court, called Hommonoscett, which layimmediately west of Saybrook, and to which, about 1663, he removed with his younger children, deeding to his sons, George and Joseph, who remained behind, his Windsor lands, r
1 CONC eserving a small life annuity therefrom. The settlement was organized as a town in 1667 and received,probably from him, the name of his old English home Kenilworth, afterwards corrupted to Killingworth, and now known as clinton. H
1 CONC e was the first deputy from Kenilworth and continued to be its magistrate and representative for more than 20 years, 1662 to 1678-89, and 2was succeeded by his son John.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Colonial Records show him to have been a very active, influentialmember of the legislature -- pre-eminently one of those men who, in the first half-century, did so much to make the small colony of Connecticut so important a fa
1 CONC ctor in American affairs. As a member of Sessions, he had the pleasure of meeting with his brother Matthew and his ownson Francis; and there has, since that time, rarely been an Assembly of Conn., in which some of their lineal des
1 CONC cendants have not been members. He was frequently a commissioner; and in 1678, was on a committeefor
1 CONT establishing a Latin school in New London, and was first deacon of the Kenilworth church.
0 @N6000000003927581665@ NOTE "Griswold, Edward, Windsor. b. at Kenilworth, in Warwicksh. 1607, as tradit. says, came, it is always said, with Rev. Ephraim Huit, tho. it is certain. wrong, for his s. George testif. that he was b. 1638, and in his youthful year
1 CONC s 'with his f. and uncle Matthew at Kenilworth, Eng. Bef. com. to W. he had Francis, George, John, and Sarah, prob. all b. in Eng. and he had at Windsor, Ann, b. 19 June 1642, in wh. yr. John d. there; Mary, bapt. 13 Oct 1644; Deb
1 CONC orah, 28 June 1646; Joseph b. 12 Mar. 16548; Samuel, bapt. 18 Nov. 1649; John again, 1 Aug. 1652; rem. at 1664 to Killingworth, so named ac. the com. pronunciat. of his native place. He was rep. 1658-61 for W. and often for K. His
1 CONC  s. Francis, and br. Matthew, as well as hims. were reps. in one Court. His s. Samuel d. 1673, and he d. 1691. Sarah m. 1650 Samuel Phelps; Mary m. 19 May 1661, Timothy Phelps; and Deborah m. 13 Nov. 1662 Samuel Buell. Francis, Ca
1 CONC mbridge 1639, perhaps br. of the predced. perhaps cous. by w. Mary had Mary, b. 28 Oct 1639; Hannah, 3 Feb. 1643, d. at two mos., and Hannah, again, 4 Mar. 1645; was..." (Savage, 2-17) 
1 CONT
1 CONT EDWARD and Mathew Griswold were b. at Kenilworth, in Warwickshire, England. According to a deposition in the State records of Hartford, Edward was b. in 1607. They emigrated to this country about 1640. Edward brought with him a wi
1 CONC fe, Margaret, and four children. He settled at Windsor and was Representative from that place 1658-61. In 1664 he removed to Killingsworth as one of the leaders in the settlement of that place and was its first Representative, and
1 CONC , no doubt, gave name to the place, Killingsworth, answering to the popular pronunciation of his native place in England. In 1678. when the County Court took the conditions of the schools into consideration, he represented Killing
1 CONC swortb in a committee of six " to see what could be done towards establising a Latin school at New Haven." His son, Francis, brother, Matthew, and himself were Representatives in one court. He d. in 1691. Nothing can be learned of
1 CONC  his wife, except that her name was Margaret. 
1 CONT *** 
1 CONT An old English family; had seats at various points: Solihul prior to 1400, Lyme and a chief seat at Melbourne Hall near Kenilworth, Warwickshire. Visitation of County of Warwick 1619 gives ... thirteen generations of Greswolds. Ed
1 CONC ward Griswold was a descendant of Sir Matthew Griswold. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Services: "Came to America with Rev. Ephraim Hewitt and party in 1639, at the time of Fenwick's second visit." Lawyer. Deputy from Windsor, Conn., 1656-1658-1660). Deputy from Killingworth (Clinton) 1662-1667-1687-1689. Later Depu
1 CONC ty from Windsor. Magistrate. Deacon of 1st Church at Killingworth. Granted land in Poquonock in 1649. Removed there later from Windsor. 
0 @N6000000000479082507@ NOTE (Research):1840. Edward GRISWOLD was born 1607 in Sloihull, Kenilworth, Warwickshire, England and was christened 26 Jul 1607 in Wooten, Warwickshire, England. He died 30 Aug 1691 in Killingworth, Hartford, Connecticut and was buri
1 CONC ed 30 Aug 1691 in Clinton Cemetery, Clinton, Middlesex, Connecticut. Edward married Margaret BLENCOW about 1630 in of, Warwickshire, England.
