0 @N6000000001540786171@ NOTE Biograhical Record of Tolland and Windham Counties, Connecticut Page 75:
1 CONT "Francis Griswold, was in Windsor in 1649 and he was one of the firstsettlers at Windham, erecting the first sawmill in town.  By trade hewas a blacksmith."
1 CONT
1 CONT New London County, Connecticut: A Modern History Page 160:
1 CONT "16. Lieut Francis Griswold, 1659,(date of list) Born 1622; died 1671, Represente the town in the General Court in eleven sessions. Home-lot on West Town street."
1 CONT
1 CONT Pease, John. It has generally been supposed by the Pease family, thatthe first of the name settled at Enfield about 1683; yet Miss Caulkins, in her History of Norwich, has reported John Pease as located at Norwich at a much earlie
1 CONC r period, with his name and lot registered in the Town Plat, as a proprietor in the N. W. extremity of the settlement, with John Tracy, John Baldwin, Jonathan Royce, Robert Allyn, Francis Griswold, Nehemiah Smith and Thomas Howard
1 CONC . John Calkins, Hugh Calkins, Ensign William Backus, Richard Egerton, Thomas Post and John Gager. Upon the opposite side of the street she locates, with no river land attached to their homelots, Samuel and William Hide; upon the r
1 CONC iver, Morgan Bowers, Robert Wade, John Birchard, John Post, Thomas Bingham and Thomas Waterman; around the Plain, Gen. Mason and Rev. James Fitch. After which she gives with like particularity the locations of Lieut. Thomas Tracy,
1 CONC  John Bradford, C. Huntington, Thomas Adgate, John Holmsted (or Olmsted,) Stephen Backus, Thomas Bliss and John Renolds. T. Leffingwell, J. Reed, R. Wallis and Richard Hendys, as the first planters of Norwich. Mr. Pease must have 
1 CONC been located in Norwich as early as 1660, as a town book was then commenced, and from that it appears the contract which had been made with John Elderkin in '54, to erect a corn-mill for the town of Mohegan, was now understood to 
1 CONC be erected either "on the land of John Pease, or at Norman's Acre," before Nov. '61. Mr. Pease was afterwards found at New London. Farmer says, John Pease was a member and Captain of the Ar. Co. in '61. He might havereturned to th
1 CONC e Plymouth Colony, and from thence removed to Enfield, or he might have removed direct from Norwich or New London to Enfield in '83. As the name and age of the man appears to be the same as that of John Pease who had resided at Sa
1 CONC lem, there is little doubt he was the same man. He was a good surveyor and a gentleman of education.
1 CONT
1 CONT REFERENCE: 306
1 CONT SOURCE: JANA WELLMAN ULRICH
1 CONT Francis did not settle in Killingworth, Conn, with his father, choosing
1 CONT instead the neighboring town of Saybrook, Connecticut and then the new
1 CONT plantation of Norwich, Connecticut in which he was an initial
1 CONT proprietor. A map of the initial land allotment at Norwich, 1660, shows
1 CONT that the home lots were laid out along the river as follows: startingat
1 CONT outer Northwest lot and moving Southeast:
1 CONT --- J. TRACY, BALDWIN, ROYCE, ALLYN, Francis GRISWOLD, J. CALKINS, H.
1 CONT CALKINS, EDGERTON, T. POST, GAGER, WADE, BIRCHARD, BOWERS, W. HYDE, J.
1 CONT POST, BINGHAM, WATERMAN, MASON, T. TRACY (northeast of Mason, off the
1 CONT river).
1 CONT Griswold says that many writers claim Francis' wife was a TRACY, but
1 CONT there are no records to support same. A fact he disputes based on the
1 CONT lack of any record of a Mary TRACY. Whoever hisspouse, the Saybrook
1 CONT records "Children of Francis GRISELL" - Sara b. 28 March 1653, Josephb.
1 CONT 4 June 1655, d. the latter end of July the same year; Mary b. 26 August
1 CONT 1656; Hanna b. 11 December 1658"
1 CONT The Norwich records cite the births of Deborah, Lydia (includes her
1 CONT death), Samuel, Margaret and Lydia II and the marriages of Sarah, Mary,
1 CONT Hannah, Deborah, Margaret and Samuel. These records refer to Francis as
1 CONT Lieutenant Griswold and indicate that he died Oct 1671 of "apparent
1 CONT disease" leaving seven children including an 18 day old infant. Thomas
1 CONT ADGATE and John POST, Sr., were appointed guardians.
1 CONT Like his father, Francis was apparently quite public spirited, having
1 CONT served as deputy to the General Court from Norwich, 1660-1671. In 1662
1 CONT he was appointed tothe Court of Commissions and served As Lieutenant of
1 CONT the trainband.
1 CONT Griswold's genealogy includes a letter from Benjamin BREWSTER to his
1 CONT Wetherill brother-in-law which mentions Francis in connection with the
1 CONT sale of a cow. Theletter contained no punctuation, which has been added
1 CONT for clarity, but misspelling left as is:
1 CONT "Loveing Brother Wetherill
1 CONT My love comended to you with mysister. if George and you dele for
1 CONT his cow
1 CONT then yo must make a very puntall barginn with him for he is trading
1 CONT with
1 CONT severall for hur. goodman GRISSILL expects the cow and therefore
1 CONT if you dele,
1 CONT make aright with him and when he comes up let him make delevere of
1 CONT the cow to me for you and dele thus for prise that what the buccher
1 CONT will give that you will give, and you dele then. send me word what
1 CONT your mind is whether I shall hur or not and take butter you may have
1 CONT some. now rest
1 CONT Your assured loveing Brother Benjamin Brewster
1 CONT ULRICH"s SOURCES: S.L. Lawson. The Griswold Family, England, Griswold,
1 CONT Glenn E. 1935 - History of Norwich, Ct., to year 1866, History of the
1 CONT Crane Family by Ellery Bucknell Crane, 1900, Tolland and Windham, Ct.
1 CONT Biographies - 1903 Saybrook, Ct. Vital Records, Norwich, Ct. Vital
1 CONT Records, Commonwealth Records of New Haven, County, Ct. 1902
1 CONT SOURCE: INFO. PACKAGE FROM MARY BACKENS
1 CONT Francis was at Cambridge, Mass. in 1639.
1 CONT SOURCE: COLONIAL FAMILIES OF THE USA VOLUME 5 (ancestry.com)
1 CONT Wm Backus and Lieutenant Francis Griswold were among the patentees ofthe
1 CONT town of Norwich. He settled in Saybrook, 1655-56, and was one of the
1 CONT first proprietors of Norwich, 1660, taking an active part in the affairs
1 CONT of the plantation and from 1661 inclusive to 1671 wasa Deputy to the
1 CONT General Court.
1 CONT
