1 CONT The family of John, who came to Windsor, is the only one of the name known to have come to this country.  He settled in Windsor before 1640, received a grant in 1648 or 1649 from the Connecticut Court, of the monopoly of a ferry a
1 CONC cross the Connecticut River located on the east side of the river, near the wharf of the Quarry Company.  Evidence seems to point to the fact that Bissell built on the east side of the river as early as 1659-60.  In 1662, he gave 
1 CONC his homestead with the ferry in Old Windsor to his son John, and remained with his son Nathaniel on the east side, below the mouth of the Scantic;  being, probably, the first family to actually reside on the east side.  Thirteen y
1 CONC ears later, during King Philip's War, the Bissells had neighbors and their house was fortified and garrisoned. 
0 @N6000000003495155876@ NOTE
1 CONT Common ancestor of Willard Pope & Fanny Laura Shaw (7th cousins)  John came to Massachusetts in 1630, and relocated to Windsor,  Connecticut about 1638. He had four sons, Thomas (Lt.), Samuel, John II, and Nathaniel, all mentioned
1 CONC  in his will, as well as  daughter Mary who married Jacob Drake, and Joyce who married Samuel Pinney. John Moore was supervisor of the will. He was the father of Nathaniel`s wife, Mindwell. Married in England.
0 @N6000000003495159258@ NOTE
1 CONT Common ancestor of Willard Pope & Fanny Laura Shaw (7th cousins)  John came to Massachusetts in 1630, and relocated to Windsor,  Connecticut about 1638. He had four sons, Thomas (Lt.), Samuel, John II, and Nathaniel, all mentioned
1 CONC  in his will, as well as  daughter Mary who married Jacob Drake, and Joyce who married Samuel Pinney. John Moore was supervisor of the will. He was the father of Nathaniel`s wife, Mindwell. Married in England.
0 @N6000000004066710903@ NOTE
1 CONT Believed to have been of Huguenot origin and to have fled with the numbers who left France after the slaughter of St. Bartholomew’s Day and to have taken an English name to cover his nationality.  According to family tradition he 
1 CONC came from Plymouth England to Dorchester Mass. and was among the colony that reached Windsor Conn. in the autumn of 1635.  In consequence of the severe loss of cattle that winter he was in the spring dispatched to England for a su
1 CONC pply and returned with 19 cows and a bull, receiving therefor in addition to his apportionment of land the ferry across the Connecticut river for his services.  According to Farmer he came from England with Rev Ephrai Hewitt in 16
1 CONC 39 directly to Windsor, and that he was a member fo  his church in England.  His name appears on the Windsor church record under date of May 3, 1640 and is second on the list of church members left by Rev. Rowland.  He died a tthe
1 CONC  residence of his son Nathaniel on the east side of the River.  His wife died in Windsor in May 21, 1641.  A 2nd wife died March 29, 1665.
0 @N6000000004066710905@ NOTE
1 CONT Arrived Plymouth, Massachusetts, 1628
0 @N6000000000337410253@ NOTE Stiles, Ancient Windsor II, p. 77;  777 - he had a homestead on the west side of the Connecticut River.  With his brother Nathaniel, owned a saw-mill (1669) on the east side;  kept the ferry from 1658-1668.
1 CONT
1 CONT Manwaring, Vol 1, p. 406
0 @N6000000004066710848@ NOTE
1 CONT early at Bolton, where he had land set out to him in the first apportionment of town lands, 1722;  was selectman in 1721 and for many subsequent years.  He was established and confirmed to be Lieutenant of the company or trainband
1 CONC  in the town of Bolton in Cot. 1722;  later he was Captain, but after his appointment to a Justice of the Peace in May 1739, he was more frequently designated Esq.  He represented the town in the Assembly and was in many ways a pr
1 CONC ominent man.  His home-lot in Botonw was at Quarryville and he and his siefe were buried there.
