0 @N6000000002248119140@ NOTE A.B., N.C., 1661, A.M.; Tutor and Fellow, H.C. 1663-1666; sett. Windsor, Conn., 1667-1680; sett Hatfield, Mass., 1682 - 1685; physician and minister.
0 @N6000000001657844192@ NOTE
1 CONT Rev. Peter Bulkeley, born 31 January 1582 in Odell, Bedfordshire, England, died 9 March 1659 at Concord, Middlesex Co., Massachusetts; married Grace Chetwood, died 21 April 1669 in New London, New London Co., Connecticut, daughter
1 CONC  of Sir Richard Chetwood and Dorothy Needham. 
1 CONT         Peter was educated at St. Johns College, Cambridge University in England. He succeeded to the Rectorship of Odel parish where he served for 21 years. After years of persecution for nonconformity he took his family and a la
1 CONC rge contingent of followers to America in 1635 "Of the concern my renewed soul has for the free worship of the L ord Christ and for planting of evangelical churches in the which to exercise that worship."  They landed in Massachus
1 CONC etts Bay Colony and a year later he founded the town of Concord described thusly: "The little town was built under the protection of a sunny bank, and all the houses faced on one long straight street. Here, with close cut hair and
1 CONC  clad with scrupulous care in the strict habit of the Puritan divine, the Reverend grave and godly Mr. Bulkley might be seen on the rounds of his daily duty, going from house to house to instruct the young and watching with pastor
1 CONC al care over the elders of his flock ... to the end of his life he continued to write Latin verse at which he had a compentently good stroke."  During this time he wrote The Gospel Covenant, which was the first religious book of i
1 CONC mportance written in New England (Jacobus), and one of the first American books to be printed. 
1 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~legends/bulkeley.html
0 @N6000000003635731841@ NOTE BIOGRAPHY: St. John's College, Cambridge 1608 M.A.; 1635 ("Ros's Pedigree") 1636 ("Dict Am Biog") with 2nd wife and children [of 1st wife?] in "Susan and Ellen" to Cambridge. Mass; first setler & minister in Concord "The Bulkeley 
1 CONC Family..." by R. W. Collins : St. John's College, Cambridge B.A. 1605, M.A. 1608; rector of All Saints Chruch, Odell. Sailed to Mass on the "Susan & Ellen" 1635
1 CONT
1 CONT COLVER31.TXT Peter was born in England. Peter's name was also spelled Bulkley. He married as his second wife, Grace Chetwood in England and moved to Concord, Mass. During the trans-Atlantic sailing to North America Mrs. Peter (Gra
1 CONC ce) Bulkeley was gravely ill and pronounced dead but Peter insisted that she not be buried at sea. Three days later Mrs. Bulkeley showed signs of vitality returning. She subsequently recovered and survived the trip. Her son Gersho
1 CONC m was born soon after her arrival in New England.
1 CONT
1 CONT be575@freenet.carleton.ca (Dick von Briesen) quotes from "Burke's Landed Gentry / American Families" (photocopy, unknown year, published about 1940) (p. 2760): William Hyde went to Leyden, Holland, [with the Pilgrims] and afterwar
1 CONC ds, in 1635, to Boston, Mass., with the Rev. Peter Bulkeley (1583-1659, M.A. St. John's Coll., Cambridge, 1608, and Founder of Concord, Mass.)
1 CONT
1 CONT occ. minister, Latin scholar
1 CONT edu. St. John's, Cambridge 1604/5 AB; 1608 AM; BD
1 CONT rel. ord. Apr 1637, Cambridge, England
1 CONT res. Cambridge U.; Odell, Bedford; Concord, MA
0 @N6000000005758026950@ NOTE One of the more famous ministers at All Saints was Peter Bulkeley. Born in Odell in 1582, he became its rector in 1624, but since he did not ?conform? to the requirements of the establishment, was suspended. In May 1635, like many
1 CONC  other Nonconformists of the time, he emigrated to New England on the Susan and Ellen. On arrival he moved on to found the city of Concord, MA, becoming its first minister.
