0 @N6000000006348274429@ NOTE
1 CONT On Oct. 30, 1707, Thomas Muse, of West'd Co., and John Muse, Jr., of Richmond Co., Va., were granted 265 acres in West'd Co. On Nov. 10, 1711, Nicholas Muse, of West'd Co., received a grant of land of 179 acres in West'd Co. on Ho
1 CONC lling Branch on Great Rappahannock Creek, between the counties of Richmond and West'd. (Northern Neck Grant Book No. 4, p. 60).
1 CONT
1 CONT Later records prove conclusively that John Muse, Jr., and Thomas Muse, Sr., were brothers, and sons of John Muse, Sr., who was born in 1633, and died in 1723, at the age of 90 years. On the 7th of September, 1715, Edward Barrow, N
1 CONC icho A. Smith, John Tayloe and James Ingo, were ordered to take the depositions of John Mews and William Carter, which they did, and on Oct. 5, 1715, presented them in Court. In the deposition of John Muse, Sr., he stated that he 
1 CONC was then Eighty-two years of age. (Miscellaneous Records and Depositions, p. 93, Richmond Co., Va.).
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert, George and Michael Muse may have been brothers of John Muse, Sr., but from the records it appears that the latter was the only one who left male issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Muse, Sr., b. 1633, d. in Westmoreland Co., Va., 1723. He left will dated April 5, 1723, pro. Oct. 8, 1723, as follows, omitting the preamble:
1 CONT
1 CONT "My last will and testament is that all debts and funeral expenses be fully paid and discharged. My will is to give to Thomas Muse my son one shilling my daughter Jane Pritchett one shilling my daughter Ann Willson one shilling my
1 CONC  daughter Mary Quisenberry one shilling and all the rest I give to my daughter-in-law Ann Muse, as Witness my hand this 5 day of April 1723--John Muse mar. (Seal) Robert Sanford, George Dunbar, Westmoreland. ss I. At a Court held 
1 CONC for the said County the 25th day of September 1723--The last will and testament of John Muse deceased was presented into Court by Ann Muse his daughter-in-law who made oath thereto and being proved by the oath of Robert Sanford on
1 CONC e of the witnesses thereto is admitted to record. And for that no Exr. is ordained in the said will upon mocon of the said Ann (as greatest creditor) and her performing what is usual in such cases certificate is granted her for ob
1 CONC taining lettere of admicon on the said decedts, estate with the will annexed in due form--
1 CONT
1 CONT                         Tets: Tho: Sorrell Cler Comped.
1 CONT                            bris
1 CONT    Recordate 8    8            1723--
1 CONT                                        Pr. Eundom Clerum.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT There is no clue as to the identity of the wife of John Muse, Sr. His children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT   II.
1 CONT   (1) Thomas Muse, Sr., m. Elizabeth. (See II-1).
1 CONT   (2) John Muse, Jr., b. cir. 1680, d. perhaps, in 1722, as his inventory was returned
1 CONT       on April 6, 1722. (See II-2).
1 CONT   (3) Jane Muse, m. Christopher Pritchett, son and heir of Thomas Pritchett.
1 CONT       They were married as early as 1695, as on July 6 of that year, they
1 CONT       sold one hundred acres of land to Humphrey Quisenberry.
1 CONT   (4) Ann Muse, m.(???)Willson.
1 CONT   (5) Mary Muse, m. Humphrey Quisenberry.
1 CONT
1 CONT SOURCE:
1 CONT Bibliographic Information: Hopkins, Walter Lee. Hopkins of Virginia and Related Families. J.W.Fergusson & Sons. Richmond, Richmond Co., VA. 1931.
0 @N6000000000704475160@ NOTE James Muse Jr. was a captain of light horse in McDonalds Tory army."  Source: "History of Moore Co, NC"  by Blackwell P. Robinson
1 CONT
1 CONT The Children of James and Charity Muse are proven by will. Will of James Muse, 10 June 1780, proved 19 Jan 1782, Cumberland Co, NC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Excerpted from "Miscellaneous Ancient Records of Moore County, N. C." by Rassie E Wicker published in 1971, pp. 323-324: "MUSE--James Muse, Sr. came down from Virginia and settled on the south side of Deep River, above the mouth o
1 CONC f McLendon's Creek in 1755, upon a tract of land purchased of James Barton.  He died here, his will being dated 30 March, 1758.  He lists James, Thomas and Daniel as his sons.  James (Jr.) and perhaps all his brothers came down on
1 CONC  McLendon's Creek in the vicinity of Carthage.  James, the most active of these, immediately began the entry of many tracts of land on McLendon's Creek, Killet's Creek and that general area, and purchased others.  In February, 177
1 CONC 6, he joined the "McDonald" Army, and was captured at Moore's Creek.  He eluded his captors somewhere on their trek to Philadelphia, and returned home.  He evidently took the oath, as he remained in the county afterwards.  His wif
1 CONC e, Charity, believed by the writer to be Charity Dickerson (this is an error, as James Muse married Charity Brazille), the daughter of Robert Dickerson, who was a near neighbor of the elder James Muse's family on Deep River, was a
1 CONC warded by the Moore Court, the Cross Hill house and lands which James, Jr had sold to Dr. Alexander Morrison, and which was confiscated on account of Morrison's part in McDonald's Army.  It is the writer's opinion that she made th
1 CONC is place her home during her latter days.  Jesse Muse, son of James, Jr, married a daughter of George Glasscock, and seems to be the ancestor of the Carthage branch of the family.  The exact location of James Jr's home has long el
1 CONC uded a long search by the writer.  It was probably at or near the present crossing of NC Rt. 27 of McLendon's and Killet's Creeks."
1 CONT
1 CONT The Will Of James Muse, Jnr.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the name of God amin
1 CONT
1 CONT I James Muse of the County of Cumberland and province of North Carolina, planter in perfect health of body and memory thanks to God, calling unto Mind the mortality of my body and knowing that it is appointed for all men once to d
1 CONC ie, do make and ordain this my lastwill and testament. That is to say principally and first of all I give and recommend my soul to God that gave it and my body I recommend to earth to be buried in decent Christian burial at the di
1 CONC scretion of myexecutors, Nothing doubting but at the general resurrection I shall receive the same by the mighty power of God. And as Touching suchthings as Worldly estate therewith it has pleased God to bless me within this life 
1 CONC I give and devise and dispose of in the following mannerand form.
1 CONT
1 CONT First I lend to Charity, my beloved wife, the manor house and plantation wherein I now live with all the household furniture and stock of all kind to be kept for the use of the family and schooling the children after her decease, 
1 CONC the household furniture and stock tobe sold at pubic sail with 12 months credit with approved security and the money thence arising to be equally divided amongst all my children except my son Daniel and James and my daughter Ferri
1 CONC ba which has already received their full part of the household goods and stock.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give to my son Daniel Muse, three hundred acres of land by the name of the front land and one hundred fifty acres joyning that same called the spring land with that I have already given him to be hisfull part of my estate. 
1 CONC Likewise, I give and bequeath to my son, James Muse, the plantation where one he now lives and the land there unto belonging and 150 acres joyning called striblings folly with what I have already given him to be his full part of m
1 CONC y estate. [Note Stribling reference: A descendant has a link to a Stribling]
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my son, Jesse Muse, the old plantation with the land thereunto belonging and 150 acres joyning above the cain patch and one stone horse called Roebuck, saddle and bridle, and that to be his full part 
1 CONC of my estate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath to my daughter, Ferriba Seale, the 200 acresof land she now lives on and negro girl called Chloe with what I havealready given her to be her full part of my estate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my son Thos Muse one hundred and fifty acres of land at the mouth of mill swamp taken up by Charles Seale andone hundred and fifty acres between John Howell's and My old place with one gelding called 
1 CONC Rainbow, saddle and bridle, and that to be his full part of my estate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath to my daughter, Mary Muse, one negro girlcalled Venis to be delivered to her at the age of sixteen or the dayof her marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my daughter, Charity Muse, one negro girl named Rachel to be delivered to her at the age of sixteen or theday of her marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my daughter, Ludda, 200 acres of land joyning Anthony Seale of Virginia and the next child the negro woman Pen shall Bear to be delivered to her at the age of sixteen or day of marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath to my daughter, Elizabeth, 150 acres of land on the mill swamp joyning Anthony Seale and taken up by Anthony Seale of Virginia and 300 acres of land on the road joyning Morrison land to be delivered to her
1 CONC  at the age of sixteen or the day of her marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath to my son, Kindred Muse, the Manor plantation whereon I now live and at his mother's death and the Land thereunto belonging and privilege to build on any part of the land not interrupting his mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath to my daughter, Martha Muse, one negro girl named Luce to be delivered to her at the age of sixteen or day ofmarriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is my desire that my beloved wife shall have the use of negro Joand negro Pen during her Natural life and after her death them with all their not named increase to be sold and the money to be Equally divided Between all my chil
1 CONC dren that is then alive.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is also my desire . . . (illegible is assumed to read: that uponthe death of my children without) . . . a lawful heir their estate shall be sold and the money equally divided between all my children that is then alive.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is also my desire that when any of my children shall marry that their mother shall give them what she thinks proper and have it valued and take a receipt for the same that may be had at a final settlement after her death.
1 CONT
1 CONT And I do Constitute, Make, and ordain My dearly beloved wife, Charity Muse, and my loving sons, Daniel and Jesse Muse, Executrix andExecutors of this my last will and testament and I do hereby utterlydisallow, Revoke, and disannul
1 CONC l all and every other former testaments, wills, legacies, bequests, Executions by me . . . (words blurred) . .. before named willed or bequeather ratifying and confirming this mylast will and testament.
1 CONT
1 CONT In witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand and affixed my sealthis 10th day of June 1780.
1 CONT
1 CONT Signed sealed pronounced and James Muse declared by the sd James Museas his Seal last will and testament in the presence ofeach other have hereunto subscribed our names. John Howell Hannah Dean
